version: "2.0"
description: >-
  Gnomon — the Paradigm 8.0 kernel (docs/specs/gnomon.md). One daemon per user
  owning sessions, identity, the hash-chained log, approvals, the budget fuse
  and the localhost web endpoint; bins gnomon, gnomond and gnomon-web. PR-0
  SCAFFOLD (research 29 §1/§4): the §18 layout with a src/ prefix, placeholder
  bins that print their version, the import fence, the zero-deps check, the
  G-WIN-05 registry skeleton and the M1.0 probe harness. Node >= 24 for Gnomon
  packages only. 8.0 M3 (T-2026-10-21-030): published as @a-company/gnomon with
  the darwin platform packages as optional deps (#gnomon-npm-packaging) and part
  of the root build/test scripts (`npm run build:gnomon` / `npm run test:gnomon`
  still run it alone). Ships to users as the signed Gnomon.app (§18.1).

components:
  gnomon-kernel:
    description: >-
      The kernel package. ZERO runtime deps (G-SEC-01): package.json has no
      `dependencies` key; @a-company/gnomon-contracts is a devDependency that
      tsup bundles. Import fence (G-KRN-02): every import under src/kernel/**
      is node:*, relative, or @a-company/gnomon-contracts, and dist/ holds no
      bare import. src/kernel/ has the thirteen §18 modules (identity, log,
      runner, supervisor, broker, budget, scheduler, manifest, refs, socket,
      web, sandbox, modhost) as empty directories at PR-0. Placeholder bins
      write to stdio directly: the kernel cannot depend on the Paradigm logger
      (zero deps); #kernel-log brings its own logging at M1.2. PR-5 adds
      src/kernel/lifecycle/ — the $daemon-lifecycle core (spec §2.4): home
      resolution (~/.gnomon; GNOMON_HOME is a test seam only, a detached daemon
      gets --home in argv) created 0700 with the mode read back; the endpoint
      path (<home>/gnomon.sock, $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR fallback on Linux on sun_path
      overflow, never /tmp, refuse if nothing fits; win32 refuses pending the
      P8-D78 parity phase); the O_EXCL daemonPid:v1 pidfile lock lifted from
      warpline lifecycle.ts (live holder refuses, dead holder cleaned by
      rename-and-inode-check and re-raced, empty file = racer mid-write); the
      clean re-exec env (PATH fixed pending root-signed config, HOME/USERPROFILE,
      LANG, LC_*; NODE_OPTIONS and GNOMON_* dropped); the idle-exit monitor
      (holders = open #socket connections until sessions/subscribers exist).
      bins: `gnomond [--foreground] [--home] [--idle-ms]`, `gnomon daemon
      status|stop|--detach` (stop refuses not-implemented pending
      #approval-broker, M1.6). Lifecycle events are emitted to a callback using
      the spec §9.1 kinds daemon.start, daemon.exit{reason}, daemon.lock.refused
      (the kernel log takes them at M1.2); the spec declares no signals.
      SYMBOL CONVENTION (P8-D81, test/symbols-drift.test.ts): every source
      module names the symbol(s) it implements in its header (first 40 lines),
      one `@symbol <id>` per line, using the spec Appendix A id verbatim where
      the spec has one (e.g. `@symbol #kernel-log`, `@symbol $daemon-lifecycle`).
      Every .ts under src/kernel/** must carry a tag; every tag must be
      declared in a packages/gnomon*/.purpose; every declared symbol must be an
      Appendix A node or be listed in test/symbol-allowlist.json with a reason.
      G-WIN-05 MARKER ENFORCEMENT (T-2026-10-17-011): vitest.config.ts excludes
      every test file whose `// @gnomon-platform:` marker omits the running
      process.platform (test/platform-registry.ts excludedTestFiles); each such
      file has a registry row (sibling or n/a reason), and the registry test
      proves per platform that the excluded set equals the rows that omit it,
      and that each row's platforms equal its file's marker.
    path: src/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, paradigm-8, scaffold]
    components:
      - "#gnomon-probes"
      - "#socket"
      - "#kernel-log"
      - "#identity"
      - "#ref-resolver"
      - "#web-endpoint"
      - "#oracle-sandbox"
      - "#manifest-loader"
      - "#scheduler"
      - "#session-runner"
      - "#supervisor"
      - "#claude-adapter"
    tests:
      - test/symbols-drift.test.ts
      - test/fence.test.ts
      - test/sec-01.test.ts
      - test/platform-registry.test.ts
      - test/version.test.ts
      - test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts
      - test/home-resolution.test.ts
      - test/idle-exit.test.ts
      - test/reexec-env.test.ts
    oracles: [G-KRN-02, G-SEC-01, G-WIN-05, G-DMN-01, G-DMN-02]

  gnomon-probes:
    description: >-
      M1.0 probe harness (spec §19.1, research 29 §2). `node
      packages/gnomon/probes/run.mjs <id> [--out-dir d] [--timeout ms]` imports
      probes/<id>/probe.mjs (zero deps, no build), runs its default export with
      {id, platform, arch, probeDir, signal}, and writes a probeResult:v1 record
      to probes/results/<id>.<platform>.json. Throws and timeouts become verdict
      `fail`. Exit codes 0 pass / 1 fail / 2 partial / 64 usage. Probe ids match
      ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$ (no path traversal). p-harness is the self-test and
      its results are gitignored; real results are committed from hardware runs
      only. A `fail` on a ruling means a re-plan memo before dependent builds.
      Unsigned macOS probes: p10m-sbx-deny-mac (S29-03/04 sandbox-exec session
      denies + escape matrix), p11-ws-framing (RFC 6455 over node:http with a
      forked server child, 27 Autobahn-style raw cases, 4 MiB throughput, RSS
      and phys_footprint), p-noise-vectors (Noise IK/XX/XXpsk3 over node:crypto
      against a verbatim cacophony/snow vector subset in vectors.json, G-LNK-11)
      p-log-fsync (kernel-log head-anchor fsync stalls, arms A-G; arm G =
      the research 33 policy: coalesced anchor + forced fullfsync+anchor at
      1/64, judged by the restated G-LOG-04; env P_LOG_FSYNC_ARMS accepts
      A,E,G) and p01-claude-cli (read-only `claude --version`/`--help` flag check;
      live items pending-manual). M1.0 batch 2 (probes/README.md lists them):
      unattended p06-local-caps (llama.cpp /slots n_ctx + live tool-call
      detection; no endpoint = partial, recorded not failed), p09-ed25519-matrix
      (WebCrypto Ed25519 in Node + every local engine under the §11.2 CSP;
      safari.html for Safari by hand, P09_SAFARI_LINE to record it),
      p12-excalidraw-csp (pinned Excalidraw bundled by esbuild in a temp dir,
      loaded under the §11.2 CSP; violations by phase, size, parse time),
      p14-link-outbound (default-deny sandbox-exec link profile); founder-run
      p00-sign-notary, p04-se-helper, p05-keychain-acl (signed
      GnomonHelperProbe.app from _shared/helper/ProbeHelper.swift; fail fast
      with a founder-steps pointer when the identity / provisioning profile /
      gnomon-notary profile is missing; prompt-free halves run unattended),
      p07-launch-token (NSWorkspace hand-off, eslogger + log stream + ps
      capture with an argv positive control, browser history grep incl.
      mozlz4), p01-claude-cli-manual (hook session_id, control_request
      interrupt, loopback-override observation, managed policy; claude by the
      path p01 recorded, never PATH). s29-10-voice-custody is a README stub
      pending the signed helper. p14b-link-fdpass (research paradigm-8/37):
      the kernel stand-in dials the peer and passes the connected socket over
      IPC to a deny-default child with no network* allow; full duplex 4 MiB,
      peer close, redial hand-off, child connect/UDP/listen/unix/file/exec all
      EPERM. p14b-web-handoff (T-2026-10-18-020): the inbound twin — the
      kernel stand-in listens on 127.0.0.1 (pauseOnConnect) and hands each
      accepted socket to a deny-default child with no network-* rule; full
      duplex 4 MiB, client close, 5 repeated hand-offs, every child
      bind/listen (0.0.0.0, ::, en0, 127.0.0.1, ::1, no host), UDP bind,
      outbound TCP and unix connect EPERM; pass on darwin. p-replay-order
      (T-2026-10-21-008, re-review M-1/M-4, darwin, real pinned claude, haiku):
      the ordering of SENT, replay, message_start/stop, tool_result and result
      for a queued message (during a tool call, after the final message_stop,
      back to back, inside a Stop-hook continuation) and for a blocking Stop
      hook with and without --include-hook-events; finding: the replay line is
      written on consumption, never on receipt, and hook_response lines appear
      only with --include-hook-events (exit_code 2, outcome 'error'). probes/_shared/ (browser.mjs, signing.mjs, abort.mjs — synchronous
      cleanups on SIGINT/SIGTERM/ctx.signal for founder-run probes, ask()
      rejects on abort — wkwebview-load.swift, helper/) is shared code, not a
      probe id. p12 pins its whole tree with a committed package-lock.json
      (npm ci). CI probe results go to $RUNNER_TEMP, never probes/results/.
    path: probes/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, probes, m1.0]
    tests:
      - test/probe-harness.test.ts

  socket:
    journal-cursor: >-
      BUILT (slice A4, T-2026-10-19-001, TD-2026-10-19-648 item 8, §5):
      SERVER_FEATURES = ['journal.cursor'] (inside the signed
      canonical(features)). log.read `since` is the {logId, seq} cursor
      (validateLogReadCursor); a cursor whose logId is not the current log
      (another kernel, or a quarantined predecessor) is
      BAD_REQUEST{cursor-epoch}; a bare-seq since is refused bad-since for
      every view (no caller sends one; it is the epoch-blind resume the
      cursor exists to stop); any resume on a connection that did not
      negotiate journal.cursor is refused bad-since{needs}, never degraded.
      A read with no since starts at the current log and needs no feature.
      The cursor rides the envelope (page cursor/logId, subscription reply
      logId, each event notification's cursor, end.cursor); limited-view
      rows carry seq only (no hash, prev, sig or log). kernel.call (mods,
      in-process) carries SERVER_FEATURES; connectGnomon offers
      DEFAULT_FEATURES. Tests: test/socket-log-read.test.ts (journal.cursor).
      GUI READER (dogfood fix 2026-10-19): readerFor gives a human
      connection whose credential is `gui` (a web-endpoint launch-token
      session or its Reconnect; never settable by a hello field) the HUMAN
      (full) projection, per §9.3 "the one first-party human surface is the
      GUI session that consumed a launch token". A human device key stays
      community-client (fullView.needs names the GUI or a
      log.read:sensitive grant); read tokens, mods and peers are unchanged.
      Tests: test/socket-log-read.test.ts (credential gui row) and the
      darwin falsifier test/gui-session-e2e.test.ts (real dev kernel + the
      GUI client and store: session listed, reply text shown, second send
      reaches the kernel).
    planned-001-move: >-
      PLANNED, not built (TD-2026-10-19-648; spec §5, §7.4, §3.9). In the
      batched clientApiId move T-2026-10-19-001: the log.read cursor
      becomes {logId, seq}, a cursor from another log is
      BAD_REQUEST{reason:'cursor-epoch'}, limited views carry seq only (no
      hash, no prev); reserved there too: hello features session.resume
      and journal.cursor, the kind session.drained, claimGen on
      contract.claimed and the CAS_LOST reasons stale-claim, claim-expired
      and claim-superseded (built in M2.2, recorded in the kernel log).
      Item 12 (a kernel-key signature over the link handshake hash,
      verified against the ids pinned in peers.jsonl) is built in M2.8.
      Item 10, `gnomon guide <topic>`: CLI-only, not a socket method;
      topics compiled into the signed binary, pull-only, never injected; a
      CI honesty check fails the build if a guide names a verb, flag or
      reason the descriptors lack (T-2026-10-19-015, before Move 2).
    backpressure: >-
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 6 (spec §11.2 rule 12, Orca audit Adopt 6):
      high/low watermarks — server.ts pauses reading above writeHighWater
      (1 MiB) and resumes only at or below writeLowWater (256 KiB,
      DEFAULT_WRITE_LOW_WATER, clamped to the high mark); the context
      exposes writeLowWater; the log.read{follow} pump (log-read.ts) stopped
      at the high mark re-waits a drain until the buffer is at or below the
      low mark (no flapping). Delivery unknown: RpcConnection (client.ts)
      answers every pending (written, unanswered) call with ENGINE
      {reason:'delivery-unknown'} when the connection closes;
      a call() timeout (ETIMEDOUT) is documented as equally
      delivery-ambiguous (Reviewer B8: buffered bytes keep flushing after the
      timer, so no timeout proves "unsent"; callers re-read state);
      connection-closed for any call provably never sent (closed, or the
      socket destroyed / not writable / ended — GUI: readyState !== OPEN —
      or a write rejected with ERR_STREAM_DESTROYED/WRITE_AFTER_END).
      Flood bounds (Aegis M1, test/socket-flood.test.ts): maxInFlight holds
      inside one inbound chunk (excess frames held, admitted in order, never
      refused); maxWriteBuffer (8 MiB) destroys a non-reading peer;
      maxConnections (64). One high mark for ctx and pressure(); the low mark
      is kept >= the socket writableHighWaterMark so a drain re-wait cannot
      stall. Named pipes skip chmod/ACL read-back; close() unlinks the
      public name BEFORE server.close() (traceClose pins the order).
      Same in src/clients/ts/client.ts and the GUI client
      (DELIVERY_UNKNOWN). Client-local, not wire reasons. Tests:
      test/web-backpressure.test.ts (every platform), gnomon-gui
      test/gui.test.ts.
    method-registry: >-
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 4 (spec §5, Orca audit Adopt 4): methods.ts
      MethodRegistry — every gnomon-rpc:v1 method (12 verbs + 2 relays) is
      REGISTERED with a tier for every matrix class (false or a non-empty
      access string); a missing/undefined/empty tier, an unknown class key, a
      duplicate or a post-seal registration throws MethodRegistrationError.
      hello/auth are the pre-auth handshake, outside the tier registry
      (handled first in dispatch.ts). MethodRegistry.uncheckedForTests is
      the sealed test seam for planting malformed tiers (verb-access.test.ts).
      KERNEL_METHODS is built from the contracts' VERBS + CLIENT_RELAYS
      (RELAY_TIERS: caller principal, readToken/peer false) at module load
      and sealed. tier()/accessOf FAIL CLOSED: unknown method, unknown class
      or any stored tier that is not false/non-empty string ⇒ false; serve()
      refuses FORBIDDEN{matrix} unless the tier is a non-empty string (Orca's
      check fails open on undefined). DispatcherOptions.methods is a test
      seam. Tests: test/verb-access.test.ts (every platform).
    ownership: >-
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 2 (spec §2.4 rule 1, Orca audit Adopt 2): never
      treat "can't tell" as "dead". listenSocket refuses a socket directory
      that is not ours and 0700 (assertPrivateDir: no bind-then-chmod
      window), binds a private random sibling name (.s<8 hex>), chmods and
      reads it back, then publishes: link() when the public name is free,
      else rename() over it only after probeSocketHolder proved the old
      holder dead (ECONNREFUSED, confirmed by a second probe after
      HOLDER_REPROBE_DELAY_MS — Aegis L1: on Darwin a live listener with a
      full accept queue also refuses). A connect = live holder; timeout, EPERM or
      any other error = undecided; both refused endpoint-busy{holder} and the
      old socket is left in place. Node's server.close() unlinks only the
      private bind name; close() removes the public name only while its
      dev+ino equal the socket we published (pinned by our live listener).
      pidfile.ts no longer unlinks the socket (acquire or release). Peer uid:
      Node has no getpeereid — 0700 dir + 0600 socket + M1.4 mutual auth are
      the boundary. Tests: test/socket-ownership.test.ts (POSIX, registry row).
    status-numbers: >-
      M1.12 slice 3 (§9.1 G-HON-03, §9.6, G-HON-07): status-numbers.ts declares
      every numeric status leaf that is neither {n, of} nor a gauge `n` —
      gauge, ordinal, limit, duration, latency (needs sibling method + machine)
      or subset-of-<path> (must not exceed its denominator); checkStatusNumbers
      returns every bare or unprovenanced number, so a new status number
      cannot ship undeclared. Round 2: an {n} without of passes only at a
      path declared gauge. Tagged #socket (the declarations live beside the
      status verb) AND #client-conformance (the check is the honesty CI gate's;
      no kernel code calls it).
    description: >-
      #socket (spec §2.1, §4.9, §5) — the kernel's gnomon-rpc:v1 endpoint.
      T-2026-10-18-016 (P8-D93): hello carries a client-chosen clientNonce
      (validated, required); auth.ts echoes it in the result and signs
      serverSig over nonce + clientNonce + endpointPath + negotiated hello;
      connectGnomon (client.ts) sends a fresh 32-byte nonce, refuses a hello
      that does not echo it (client-nonce-mismatch) and verifyServerHello
      takes the SENT nonce, so a recorded genuine hello replayed from a
      squatted socket never reaches auth.
      PR-5 core: unix-socket server bound under a 0177 umask, chmod 0600 and
      the ACL read back before serving; byte-based NDJSON framing (4 MiB max
      inbound frame, UTF-8 safe partial lines, oversize discarded to its
      newline and answered BAD_REQUEST once, malformed lines BAD_REQUEST,
      connection kept); per-connection in-order replies; connIds are
      conn:<ULID>. M1.4 SLICE 1: errors carry contracts refusal:v1 in
      error.data with the §4.9 code map (rpc.ts). auth.ts — `hello`
      negotiation via contracts negotiateHello (highest shared gnomon-rpc
      version; none shared ⇒ UNSUPPORTED{offered[], supported[]}; tolerant
      param validation; client strings capped at 128 chars) answering
      {clientApi, features, nonce, serverKeyId, serverSig, clientApiId,
      kitId}; serverSig = kernel key over gnomon:auth-server:v2 + nonce +
      endpointPath; `auth {principal, deviceId, sig}` verified against the
      ROOT-signed device registry (#identity), nonce single-use (any attempt
      consumes it) and 30 s, accepted transcripts remembered so a replay is
      socket.auth.replay; {token}/{launchToken}/{reconnect} refused
      UNSUPPORTED (later M1.4 slices). Any failed auth = ONE AUTH refusal,
      then the connection is closed. dispatch.ts — before auth only hello and
      auth (every other name, known or unknown, AUTH{unauthenticated} then
      closed — Aegis L6: no pre-auth method enumeration; UNKNOWN_VERB only
      after auth); after auth the §5 verb × principal matrix
      (contracts VERBS.access; peer refused everywhere) then `status` served
      for real (principal, connId, surface, clientApi, clientApiId, kitId,
      abiId, nextLegalVerbs from the §5 table, verbs/relays with
      availability + milestone, daemon info, log head, sessions 0, cards 0,
      kernelKeyCustody partial, root state, authenticated clients) under
      50 ms (G-ABI-03), every other verb/relay ENGINE{not-implemented,
      milestone} — no fake success, no extra verb (the PR-5 daemon.status /
      daemon.stop placeholders are removed). Kernel-log rows before the
      reply: socket.hello, socket.auth.ok, socket.auth.fail,
      socket.auth.replay (+ socket.connect, socket.frame.refused as
      diagnostics); a failed auth.ok write refuses the auth. client.ts —
      rpcCall, RpcConnection, verifyServerHello (serverSig against the
      registry's kernel key over the CLIENT-derived path), signAuth and
      connectGnomon (never sends auth to an unverified server; reason
      unverified-server). ROUND 2: both signatures also bind the negotiated
      clientApi + canonical features (downgrade-proofing; a deviation from the
      §4.9 preimage text, pending spec/contracts); the registry is cached by
      size+mtime+sha256; qualified matrix access (e.g. mod 'own view') is
      refused ENGINE not-implemented with its milestone, never the full view;
      hello/auth.ok log failures close the connection (fail rows are
      best-effort); pre-auth limits — no hello in 10 s or no auth by the 30 s
      nonce TTL closes, at most 64 pre-auth connections; flow control pauses
      reading at 32 queued requests or 1 MiB unsent replies; pre-auth
      diagnostics are coalesced into one signed counter row per window.
      ROUND 3: socket.hello and socket.auth.fail are rate-limited — the
      first 8 per 10 s window individually, the rest folded into that
      window's signed counter row (byOutcome / byReason); auth.ok/.replay stay
      individual; inbound frames capped at 16 KiB until auth succeeds; the
      client refuses a hello whose clientApi/features it did not offer
      (hello-not-offered, client-local); RegistryCache parses the bytes it
      hashed. Windows named pipe: parity phase (P8-D78).
      M1.4 SLICE 2 (log-read.ts): `log.read` and `ref.resolve` served for
      authenticated principals, each handled EXPLICITLY per reader class
      (never the generic 'full'): human → the §9.3 COMMUNITY-CLIENT projection
      (full minus sensitive and secret, sensitive-column kinds as {kind, hsid,
      at, hash} stubs, registered sensitive fields → {redacted, hash}) because
      a first-party surface / log.read:sensitive grant cannot be proven on
      the socket yet (result names projection + fullView.needs); mod → own
      modns.<name>.* + granted kinds (none yet); agent → UNSUPPORTED Move 2;
      voice → UNSUPPORTED Move 4; session → ENGINE not-implemented M1.7 (no
      principal → hsid binding); read tokens do not exist yet. Pages:
      {since, until?, kinds? (exact or ns.*), scope? {hsid, repo, loop,
      contract} applied to the PROJECTED view (a stub never matches a
      repo/loop/contract filter), limit? ≤ 1000} → {rows, next (last scanned
      seq, stable cursor), head, caughtUp, projection, withheld}; ≤ 10 000
      rows scanned and ~2 MiB per page, a row > 1 MiB becomes {view:
      'oversize', kind, hash, bytes}. Subscriptions (follow: true): reply
      {subscription, since, head, projection, backlogCap} written first
      (ctx.afterReply), then backlog + live rows as `event` notifications
      {subscription, row} in seq order, read lazily from the log; the pump
      writes only under the connection's write high-water mark and resumes on
      'drain'; per subscription a queue of undelivered post-subscribe seqs
      (numbers only) — beyond 10 000 the subscription is dropped, the client
      gets event{subscription, end{reason:'dropped', lag, since}} and the
      kernel logs socket.subscription.dropped{subscription, lag, since} with
      the connId (deferred, never inside a commit); ≤ 8 subscriptions per
      connection, 256 per kernel; each holds the idle monitor ('subscriber');
      subscriptions end with their connection. server.ts gains ctx.notify /
      writableLength / writeHighWater / onDrain / afterReply; client.ts
      RpcConnection.onNotification. status adds subscriptions{n,
      onThisConnection, perConnectionMax, backlogCap}. ROUND 2: limited
      views (community-client, mod, read-token) carry no hash/sig/prev,
      redacted fields are {redacted: true} and stubs {kind, hsid?, at} (no
      confirmation oracle; ref.resolve returns no hash to them); only rows at
      or below the log's RELEASED head are read or streamed (failed critical
      rows never); lag = committed head − cursor, a subscribe with head −
      since > 10 000 is BAD_REQUEST{backlog-too-large} (page first); the
      commit-path fan-out is one metadata check per subscriber (mayBeVisible
      + kind filter; no projection, copy or hash — projection happens lazily
      in the pump); writeHighWater is clamped to ≥ the socket's
      writableHighWaterMark; an unencodable frame ends the subscription with
      end{reason:'internal'}; follow on a JSON-RPC notification (no id) is
      refused follow-needs-id; queued requests of a closed connection never
      run (server `closed` flag) and dispatch refuses ENGINE{gone} for a
      closed ctx, so zombie pre-auth entries cannot fill maxPreAuth. ROUND
      3: limited views also drop idem and refHash; lag = min(seq, released
      head) − cursor; subscribe admits at most cap/2 backlog measured
      against the released head (page until caughtUp, then follow).
      M1.4 SLICE 3 (round 2: P8-D89 / D89a): read tokens are PROOF OF
      POSSESSION — auth {tokenId, sig} (exactly those keys, after hello, inside
      the nonce TTL), sig = the token's Ed25519 key over gnomon:auth-token:v1 +
      contracts authTokenMessage(nonce, serverKeyId, tokenId, endpointPath,
      negotiated hello), verified against the public key in #identity's
      root-signed token file; a bearer {token} is auth-params; the seed never
      crosses the wire. The connection binds to the token's principal with
      credential 'read-token' and matrix class readToken: status, log.read
      (the §9.3 read-token projection), ref.resolve; every other verb and both
      relays FORBIDDEN{matrix}. Unknown / expired / not-yet-valid / revoked
      (sticky) / bad or replayed proof / in use (one live connection per
      token) / untrusted token file ⇒ AUTH + close; the token is re-checked on
      every call, at its expiry and every 5 s; a dead token closes the
      connection with a best-effort socket.disconnect{reason, tokenId}. A
      connection that closes during the awaited auth.ok write is refused
      ENGINE{gone} and gets no timers or lifted cap (timerCount() for tests).
      Rows carry only tokenId. connectGnomon({readToken}) validates options
      before connecting and proves possession only to a verified server.
      G-SOCK-01: a known verb before auth is AUTH and CLOSES the connection.
      log-append.ts — the §5 namespace-ownership gate (G-SOCK-04): unknown and
      kernel-owned kinds FORBIDDEN{kernel-owned} for every class, the human
      request.* exception too (needs: first-party human-class connection,
      unprovable until #web-endpoint); voice request.voice.arm and voice.*
      ENGINE Move 4; userland kinds FORBIDDEN{ungranted, M1.5 — grant.bind
      needs M1.3 grants over M1.5 manifests} for human/agent; a mod's own
      modns ENGINE M1.11, else modns-not-own; nothing is ever appended in M1.4.
      status adds credential, token{tokenId, expiresAt}, relay access, and
      nextLegalVerbs filtered to the caller's class. Preimages come from
      contracts (T-2026-10-17-017). abiId pinned in test/abi.ids.json
      (G-ABI-04). Suites: test/socket-abi.test.ts (G-ABI-01..05 incl. a literal
      §5 matrix fixture and the userland-namespace list, G-SOCK-02..04, the
      auth race; todos naming later milestones). ROUND 3: the PoP is verified
      before any token state is told (no valid proof ⇒ uniform unknown-token,
      no tokenId on the row); the daemon re-reads the token file at start and
      every 60 s (tokenRefreshMs) so revocations reach the chain with no live
      token connection, retrying a failed key.token.revoked write and keeping
      it pending (daemon.token.revocation.unrecorded event) — never dropped;
      every reason the kernel sends is registered (test/refusal-reasons.test.ts
      scans socket/, web/, refs/, redaction.ts). M1.4 #web-endpoint: auth.ts
      takes a `web` hook for {launchToken}/{reconnect} (credential 'gui',
      human class); on the unix socket those forms stay UNSUPPORTED
      web-auth-not-implemented. P8-D98 (T-2026-10-19-001 A0): the registry
      (methods.ts) also registers the three human-device verbs gui.open,
      rules.revoke and daemon.stop (human tier only, every other class false, deny by
      default); dispatch.ts refuses any non-human class FORBIDDEN{matrix},
      refuses a human GUI launch-token connection the same way, and routes a
      human DEVICE connection to DispatcherOptions.humanVerbs (one handler
      module per verb, wired by the daemon so dispatch.ts does not change
      again) or ENGINE{not-implemented} when the port is absent; status lists
      them as humanDeviceVerbs. SERVER_FEATURES stays [] (no reserved hello
      feature advertised). abiId still hashes the twelve VERBS only.
    path: src/kernel/socket/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, socket, paradigm-8, pr-5]
    tests:
      - test/ndjson-framing.test.ts
      - test/socket-server.test.ts
      - test/socket-auth.test.ts
      - test/socket-log-read.test.ts
      - test/socket-abi.test.ts
      - test/human-device-verbs.test.ts
      - test/refusal-reasons.test.ts
      - test/verb-access.test.ts
      - test/web-backpressure.test.ts
    aspects: ["~ample-internal-logs"]
    oracles: [G-DMN-02, G-ABI-01, G-ABI-02, G-ABI-03, G-ABI-04, G-ABI-05, G-SOCK-01, G-SOCK-02, G-SOCK-03, G-SOCK-04]

  web-endpoint:
    gui-bundle-binaries: >-
      #gui S1 (2026-10-20): the embedded GUI bundle carries binary files
      (woff2 fonts) and sub-directory paths (fonts/<name>). gui-bundle.ts
      stays opaque base64; materializeGuiBundle creates the sub-directories
      (0700 best-effort, never relied on) under <home>/gui/bundle/; the tree
      hash's SAFE_PATH still refuses `..`. rules.ts MIME map gains
      svg -> image/svg+xml for the bundle's own grain.svg (static only; /blob
      still never serves SVG); woff2 -> font/woff2 was already mapped. CSP
      unchanged (font-src 'self', img-src 'self' blob:).
    watermarks: >-
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 6 (§11.2 rule 12): the transport child reports
      `pressure` above writeHighWater (WS_WRITE_HIGH_WATER 1 MiB) and `drain`
      only at or below writeLowWater (WS_WRITE_LOW_WATER 256 KiB, a new
      ChildConfig field); 1008 past 8 MiB unchanged. The GUI WebSocket
      connection context carries writeLowWater for the log.read{follow}
      pump. The built-in GUI bundle was re-embedded (gui-bundle.ts) for the
      GUI client's DELIVERY_UNKNOWN change. Tests:
      test/web-backpressure.test.ts, test/web-child.test.ts.
    description: >-
      #web-endpoint (spec §2.1, §4.9 WebSocket paragraph, §11.2, §15.5
      `transport`, §2.4 linger; M1.4). The kernel half (src/kernel/web/) and
      the `gnomon-web` transport child (src/transport/). Listener
      (T-2026-10-18-020, §11.2 rule 1): the KERNEL listens on the 127.0.0.1
      literal (OS-chosen ephemeral port, pauseOnConnect) and hands each
      accepted socket, unread, to the child with subprocess.send('conn',
      socket) over the child's IPC channel (stdio fd 3, WEB_CONN_MESSAGE);
      accept-and-forward only, so the child stays the only parser (A24-1).
      The listener outlives a child restart (same port); stop(), the restart
      cap and a failed respawn close it (the port is then ECONNREFUSED, and
      squattable by a local process, §A.7); a connection accepted while no
      child serves is closed. Hand-off bounds (round 2, Aegis C1): listener
      maxConnections WEB_MAX_PENDING_HANDOFFS = 128 (Node counts a socket
      until it is sent and queues later hand-offs behind an unACKed one); an
      ACK watchdog (handoffAckMs, default 5 s, ACKs seen via the undocumented
      'internalMessage' NODE_HANDLE_ACK, FIFO) SIGKILLs a stalled child;
      any user IPC message and any internal one but NODE_HANDLE_ACK kills the
      child and closes an attached handle; an UNSOLICITED NODE_HANDLE_ACK
      (none in flight, or one more than sent) would make Node's own handler
      throw ERR_INTERNAL_ASSERTION in the daemon, so a prependListener
      (relies on the Node internal _handleQueue) gives Node an empty queue
      and kills the child (round 3); a failed send forgets its watchdog
      entry; a stop() during the exit-log write cancels the respawn and
      writes its stop row. Residual: Node's fd-3 JSON-line
      reader is unbounded (DoS-only, §A.7). A start that began before a
      stop() (stop generation counter) kills its child and never listens or
      writes a second socket.web.stop row; listenAddress() exposes the bound
      address. The CHILD listens on nothing and parses bytes only: node:http
      over the handed sockets (server.emit('connection')) with per-socket
      slowloris deadlines (a never-listening http.Server runs no
      headersTimeout/requestTimeout checker): headersTimeout 10 s from the
      hand-off and after each response, requestTimeout 30 s for the body;
      the ready record names no port (channel gnomon-web:v2), the RFC 6455 upgrade and zero-dep framing (ws-codec.ts: text only — binary
      1003; no fragmentation — FIN=0/continuation 1009; no extension ever
      negotiated — RSV bits 1002; masking required 1002; invalid UTF-8 1007;
      >4 MiB 1009 from the header; close mirrored; pings every 30 s, closes
      after 2 missed pongs), and static serving of the bundle bytes it hashed
      at start (held in memory). It forwards {rid, method, path, headers} /
      {rid, data} records over stdio (protocol.ts) and writes back only what
      the kernel says; its import closure is framing + channel + bundle hash
      (test/seams-fence.test.ts). The KERNEL decides every rule (rules.ts):
      Host = 127.0.0.1:<port> else 421 (host) before routing; Origin =
      http://127.0.0.1:<port> on every upgrade and non-static request else
      403 (origin); a present Sec-Fetch-Site same-origin|none else 403 (an
      absent one passes only an upgrade, and /diag and /blob, which then
      still need a live X-Gnomon-Token: Aegis finding 2); no cookies, no
      ACAO, the rule-6 CSP/COOP/CORP/nosniff/no-referrer on every response,
      no-store on non-static; traversal refused; /blob raster-only by content
      sniffing and verified content hash, GUI token in X-Gnomon-Token; /diag
      ≤ 64 KiB to <home>/logs/gui.log after redactDiagText (32-byte
      base64url tokens ⟨redacted⟩, contracts display sanitizer, Aegis C5); /blob and /diag are POST (a
      same-origin GET carries no Origin; the page sends them with
      referrerPolicy same-origin), GET/HEAD 405; /rpc and /diag match
      exactly, only /blob/ is a prefix; no HTTP RPC. Every child record is
      validated against the closed shape set (protocol.ts
      validateChildRecord) and the child is SIGKILLed on the first violation
      or on any throw in record handling (Aegis C1); a compromised child is a
      compromise of the GUI sessions it relays (spec §A.7). Child-written
      503/404 carry the rule-6 default headers. Per connection: the child
      starts at a 16 KiB inbound cap, raised by a kernel `limit` record on
      authentication; unsent bytes are reported as pressure/drain (the
      dispatcher's writableLength/onDrain are per connection) and over 8 MiB
      the child closes 1008; more than 32 queued requests close 1008. Read
      tokens do not authenticate on the web endpoint (Aegis C3). WebSocket: hello then
      auth within 5 s else 1008, frames before auth never dispatched as verbs;
      ≤ 64 live (non-closing) connections, at the cap the oldest live
      unauthenticated one is closed (1013), never an authenticated GUI one
      (503 instead); ≤ 5 auth
      attempts/min/connection. Its own dispatcher (authenticator signs
      ws://127.0.0.1:<port>/rpc) with a `web` auth hook (launch.ts): a
      single-use 60 s launch token minted in-process for an authenticated
      human device connection (DaemonHandle.requestWebLaunch — §5 names no
      verb for `gnomon open`), exchanged for a 32-byte GUI token held as a
      digest; a second presentation inside the TTL is theft (refused, the
      consuming session revoked, socket.auth.theft + socket.gui.session.ended
      {theft}); after the TTL a replay (socket.auth.replay only); both answer
      `replayed-transcript`. Reconnect logs socket.auth.reconnect.requested
      and is refused UNSUPPORTED web-auth-not-implemented {milestone M1.3,
      cause presence-unavailable} until the signed helper's presence. GUI
      sessions live as long as their socket (≤ 12 h; revoke-all; daemon
      exit). Bundle pin (bundle.ts): gnomon-bundle-tree:v1 hash; since
      M1.10 slice 1 the #gui bundle (packages/gnomon-gui, embedded as opaque
      base64 in the GENERATED gui-bundle.ts, read by gui-pin.ts outside the child closure) pinned by the GUI_BUNDLE_HASH
      literal until the root-signed `gnomon gui pin` (M1.3), materialized
      under <home>/gui/bundle/ (the placeholder bundle stays a fixture);
      a mismatch logs
      socket.web.bundle.mismatch and never opens. Supervision: restart cap
      3/10 min (socket.web.transport.exited, then socket.web.stop
      {transport-restart-cap}; a failed respawn ⇒ {transport-restart-failed};
      both clear the timers); 2-min linger after the last GUI session
      (socket.web.linger, socket.web.stop{no-sessions}). Production spawn
      only through the platform's `transport` sandbox profile
      (#oracle-sandbox; on macOS that profile has no network-* rule, so the
      child opens no network socket of its own — every bind, listen and
      outbound connect is EPERM, P14b-web, T-2026-10-18-020); the unsandboxed child exists only via the internal
      seam `web` (testing.ts createWebEndpointForTest). Event kinds emitted
      (§9.1 socket namespace): socket.web.listen, socket.web.bundle.mismatch,
      socket.web.linger, socket.web.stop, socket.web.transport.exited,
      socket.gui.token.issued, socket.gui.session.ended, socket.auth.theft,
      socket.auth.replay, socket.auth.reconnect.requested, socket.auth.fail
      {origin|host|sec-fetch-site|gui-token}. The spec declares no signals.
    path: src/kernel/web/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, web-endpoint, transport, paradigm-8, m1-4]
    tests:
      - test/web-units.test.ts
      - test/web-endpoint.test.ts
      - test/gui-endpoint.test.ts
      - test/web-child.test.ts
      - test/web-sandbox.test.ts
      - test/seams-fence.test.ts
      - test/refusal-reasons.test.ts
    oracles: [G-WEB-01, G-WEB-02, G-WEB-03, G-WEB-04, G-WEB-05, G-WEB-06, G-WEB-07, G-WEB-08]

  oracle-sandbox:
    description: >-
      #oracle-sandbox (spec §15.5) — the kernel's sandbox profiles. M1.4
      lands the `transport` profile for gnomon-web (src/kernel/sandbox/
      transport.ts): profile data, the darwin sandbox-exec SBPL generator
      (default-deny; read of `/`, /usr/lib, /System/Library, the node binary
      and its install's lib/ — never the prefix — plus Homebrew's Cellar, opt
      and etc/openssl@3; refused `sandbox-unavailable` if any granted read
      subpath equals or contains $HOME, the gnomon home or its keys dir
      (Aegis C2);
      the child's entry dir and package.json ancestors, the pinned bundle;
      metadata of their ancestors; sysctl-read; exec of node only; NO
      network-* rule at all (no bind, no inbound, no outbound — the kernel
      listens and hands connected sockets over the IPC channel,
      T-2026-10-18-020); no fork, no write, no mach-lookup; every path
      realpath'd; env empty; TRANSPORT_PROFILE v2). Platform registry TRANSPORT_BACKENDS
      (G-WIN-05): darwin only; win32 is the Windows parity phase (P8-D78);
      any platform without a backend refuses `sandbox-unavailable` (fail
      closed). Residuals: ancestor metadata is visible; node may re-exec
      itself; the child reads and writes the sockets it is handed.
      T-2026-10-18-017 (measured): SBPL accepts only `*` or `localhost` as a
      network host, and `(local ip "localhost:*")` matches every address
      configured on this host's interfaces (LAN and wildcards included), so
      no listen rule can be loopback-only. T-2026-10-18-020 (Aegis ruling)
      therefore removed every network-* rule: the kernel listens on 127.0.0.1
      and passes the CONNECTED socket (never the listening fd, whose listen()
      would be re-checked). Falsifiers in web-sandbox.test.ts: every child
      bind/listen/UDP/outbound/unix attempt EPERM against an unsandboxed
      control; a handed socket serves HTTP + a WebSocket round trip; the en0
      address at the endpoint port is refused; the SBPL names no network
      rule. The T-017 loopback-listen retry is gone (nothing left to flake).
      The other seven profiles land with their milestones (session M1.7,
      oracle M2.3, …).
    path: src/kernel/sandbox/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, sandbox, paradigm-8, m1-4]
    tests:
      - test/web-sandbox.test.ts
      - test/web-units.test.ts
    oracles: [G-WEB-07]

  ref-resolver:
    description: >-
      #ref-resolver (spec §3.7, §4.4, §5 verb 12; M1.4 slice 2) —
      src/kernel/refs/resolve.ts, the kernel's read-only resolver for its
      built-in schemes. Served: `ev:<ULID>` → {ref, target: the §9.3
      projected view for the caller's reader class, hash}; every row is
      re-hashed (mismatch ⇒ ENGINE{row-integrity}, withheld); a missing row
      and an invisible one are the same NOT_FOUND (no existence oracle); an
      @rev on an event is BAD_REQUEST. Validated then ENGINE{not-implemented,
      milestone}: turn:<hsid>/<n> and hsid: (M1.7), cc (M1.7), gkey/dev
      (M1.3), permit/card (M1.6), contract/node (M2.1), pev (Move 2 — the
      spec names none). UNSUPPORTED: peer: (only a brief's copy, §3.9),
      package schemes (no-resolver: userland), unknown schemes. Malformed
      refs, dropped schemes (voice) and refs over 512 chars are BAD_REQUEST.
    path: src/kernel/refs/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, refs, paradigm-8, m1.4]
    tests:
      - test/socket-log-read.test.ts
    oracles: [G-ABI-02]

  identity:
    approval-devices: >-
      DEFERRED, not built (TD-2026-10-19-946; spec §22 items 23-24).
      Approvals happen only on the Mac. Enrolled approval devices (phone
      Face ID, the Watch) come well after the core loop is tested, after
      the kernel-key-bound link handshake (TD-2026-10-19-648 item 12): a
      non-exportable hardware key per device, enrolled from the Mac by a
      root-signed registry row with root presence, revoked by a root-signed
      row, a fresh biometric check on every decision, and its own signature
      domain and presence value so it never verifies as a hand decision.
      The bearer-token model (a phone token as approval authority with no
      biometric) is REJECTED.
    description: >-
      TD-2026-10-20-514 (G-ID-09, G-ID-10): HelperApprovalsKey.signDecisionBatch
      sends ONE decision.sign (helper >= 1.8.0, chosen from info.version) for a
      whole card decision, pre-checked by decision-batch.ts (the TS mirror of the
      helper rules, shared vectors), every signature verified against the pinned
      keys and `shown` compared byte-exact; HelperProcess / SupervisedHelper
      stash the handshake version.
      #identity (spec §3.1; M1.4 slice of it — hardware custody is M1.3,
      blocked on the founder's provisioning profile). root-authority.ts — the
      RootAuthority seam: UnprovisionedRootAuthority is the production default
      (verifies nothing, signRow refuses UNSUPPORTED{root-unprovisioned,
      milestone M1.3}; never a software fallback); DevTestRootAuthority is a
      software P-256 root constructible only with the literal
      DEV_TEST_ROOT_ACK, never from env/argv (§15.3), reported as root state
      `dev-test` in status and daemon.start; RootVerifier verifies rows from a
      pinned root public key (the client side). Row sigs: gnomon:row-sig:v1
      over canonical JSON, P-256 IEEE-P1363. device-key.ts — the Ed25519
      device key at <home>/keys/device.key: written O_EXCL 0600, loaded only
      if a regular owner-only file in an owner-only keys/ dir; a device
      signer cannot approve. registry.ts — <home>/keys/registry.jsonl,
      append-only {row, signer, sig} rows (kind kernel | device); fail-closed
      reader: untrusted signer, bad signature, keyId ≠ sha256(PEM), principal
      or deviceId outside the root's ns, foreign principals and peer/kernel
      kinds never resolve and are surfaced in rejected[]; first row pins a
      deviceId and a keyId. The kernel principal is p:<root ns>/kernel, or
      p:00000000/kernel while no root exists (UNPROVISIONED_NS).
      kernel-pub.ts — PUBLIC halves of every kernel key the home ran with
      (<home>/keys/kernel-pub.jsonl, 0600), loaded into the log's key ring and
      kernelKeyIds so verify works across restarts; same-user writable and not
      root-signed, so across restarts kernel signatures prove nothing against
      a same-user forger until M1.3 (custody partial). presence.ts
      (T-2026-10-17-009(c), Aegis S34-10) — PresenceToken, a branded type
      minted only by this module: private constructor guarded by a
      module-private mint key, a `#state` private field and a `unique symbol`
      brand (nominal by type; `PresenceToken.is` is a `#state in v` runtime
      brand check no copy, prototype object or Proxy passes); bound to one
      purpose (`log.gap.acknowledge`; M1.5 adds `mod.install` and
      `mod.rollback`), single-use and fresh for 60 s
      (`consumePresence` → ok | not-a-token | wrong-purpose | stale | used).
      `requestPresence` refuses UNSUPPORTED{presence-unavailable, M1.3} until
      the signed helper's prompt exists; the only mint before then is the
      internal test seam (method `test-seam`).
      read-token.ts (M1.4 slice 3, P8-D89a) — <home>/keys/read-tokens.jsonl,
      append-only root-signed rows {op: mint, tokenId rtk:<26>, keyId,
      publicKeyPem, principalId (root ns, not kernel, not foreign), createdAt,
      expiresAt (default 1 h, ≤ 24 h)} and {op: revoke, tokenId}; PUBLIC keys
      only. mintReadToken (library only: no verb, no argv; now within 5 min of
      the real clock and clamped to it, so expiresAt ≤ real now + ttl) returns grt:v1:<id>.<seed> once; parseReadToken gives
      the client a readToken-class signer that signs only gnomon:auth-token:v1.
      ReadTokenStore: fail-closed reader; not valid before createdAt − 60 s;
      revocations STICKY for its life and seeded from key.token.revoked rows
      on the chain (daemon.ts records each newly seen revoke); the file must
      grow append-only — shrinking or a lost/rewritten line, or more than
      1 MiB / 10 000 rows, makes it untrusted for the store's life (nothing
      resolves; key.registry.malformed{file, reason}). Open questions (spec
      silent; most restrictive reading chosen): the CLI verb that mints, the
      revocation re-check period.
      M1.3 slice 1 (hardware custody, the signed #gnomon-helper):
      helper.ts — pinHelper resolves the path once (realpathSync.native),
      refuses missing / non-executable / group- or world-writable files,
      verifies `codesign --verify --strict` + HELPER_REQUIREMENT (Developer
      ID Application, team SW4BMQ8PNL, identifier gnomon.helper), records
      inode/size/mtime/sha256 and RE-CHECKS them synchronously before every
      spawn; HelperProcess runs `gnomon-helper serve` (NDJSON, one request in
      flight, ≤ 16 queued, 1 MiB lines, timeouts kill it; out-of-order or
      oversized replies kill it — fail closed); SupervisedHelper restarts it
      on demand; helperOnceSync runs `once` for synchronous root signatures.
      The unsigned verifier exists only behind DEV_TEST_HELPER_ACK.
      Round 2: the pin is hash → verify → re-hash (H5); after spawn the
      RUNNING child is code-sign-verified by pid and must answer `info` with
      gnomon-helper:v2 (helper 1.6.0) and version ≥ MIN_HELPER_VERSION before any request
      (H1 c — a version floor, not a CDHash allowlist, because timestamped
      signatures change every build); the platform check is
      src/platform/helper-verifier.ts (R6); SupervisedHelper never restarts
      after close() and backs off 1 s doubling to 30 s (R5).
      G-HLP-01 wired (T-2026-10-21-028, #release-pins): in a RELEASE build
      pinHelper also requires the sha256 the release build compiled in for
      this arch (releaseHelperSha256; mismatch, no entry for the arch, a
      broken compiled manifest or another requirement ⇒ 'checksum'; a pinned
      version below MIN_HELPER_VERSION ⇒ 'version'). Every call site
      (provisioner, openHardwareIdentity, HelperRepin, preflight) goes through
      it; a dev build (no manifest) keeps signature + version floor only.
      hardware.ts — HardwareRootAuthority (state `hardware`; verifies with
      the pinned root; signRow sync via `once`, signRowAsync via the daemon's
      helper; fresh presence per signature; a signature that does not verify
      under the pinned root is refused); HelperApprovalsKey (ApprovalsKey
      whose decisionSig the helper makes after presence: fresh, optionally
      kept as the connId's cached unlock IN THE HELPER, or cached; the same
      touch signs a kernel presence challenge so the broker gets a bound
      `helper-se` PresenceToken; every helper signature verified before use;
      approver must be the key's human). presence.ts — production mint =
      hardware attestation: beginHardwarePresence (single-use challenge,
      ≤ 64 outstanding, 120 s) / completeHardwarePresence (mints only on a
      `gnomon:presence:v1` signature that verifies in the pinned ring).
      provision.ts — <home>/keys/identity.json (identity:v1, public only:
      gen, root key + ns, root-signed approvals binding to the human, device,
      helperPath) and keys/identity.jsonl journal; openHardwareIdentity opens
      it for gnomond only when the pinned helper confirms both SE public keys
      and the binding verifies under the root, else the daemon refuses to
      start (identity-<reason>), never a software root.
      cli/identity-cli.ts — `gnomon identity init` provisions the real root
      once (refused from a kernel-spawned shell, while the daemon runs, and
      when identity.json exists unless `--rotate` on a TTY with the typed
      confirmation, logged to stderr and a journal line signed by the OLD
      root; old registry moved aside as evidence, old SE keys retired):
      pins the helper, creates root + approvals in the SE under a fresh
      generation, two root signatures with presence (approvals binding,
      device row), identity.json last; a failure deletes the keys it made.
      Round 2: rotate needs the OLD root in the SE to sign a
      gnomon-root-rotate:v1 statement the helper demands before it creates
      the new generation (H4/H2); `--recover` (typed `recover`) replaces a
      LOST root with a new namespace, journalled as not signed by it; init
      asks the helper (key.list) and refuses while another generation is
      held; every touch's expected digest is printed (H3); the device row is
      signed first and appended only after identity.json (R3); a failure
      before the record restores moved files (R2) and deletes its keys via
      the live helper or a fresh one with presence (R4), never after; Ctrl-C
      stops at the next step and cleans up. `gnomon identity purge-keys
      --gen <g>` deletes another generation's keys, one touch each.
      registry.ts gains deviceRowFor + appendSignedRegistryRow.
      `gnomon identity status`. Founder hardware test:
      GNOMON_HW_TESTS=1 test/identity-hw.test.ts (it.todo in CI).
    path: src/kernel/identity/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, identity, paradigm-8, m1.4, m1.3]
    tests:
      - test/socket-auth.test.ts
      - test/presence.test.ts
      - test/socket-abi.test.ts
      - test/identity-hardware.test.ts
      - test/identity-helper-pin.test.ts
      - test/identity-cli.test.ts
      - test/identity-hw.test.ts

  dev-launch:
    description: >-
      #dev-launch (spec §2.4 rule 6) — the UNSIGNED, DEV-ONLY dogfood launch
      path until #identity M1.3. src/cli/dev-home.ts + src/cli/dev-cli.ts.
      `gnomon dev init [--home <abs>]` provisions a DEV home (default
      ~/.gnomon-dev): a software P-256 dev-test root (DevTestRootAuthority
      behind DEV_TEST_ROOT_ACK, persisted 0600 as keys/DEV-ONLY-root-p256.pem),
      a software kernel key (keys/DEV-ONLY-kernel-ed25519.pem), the device key
      and the root-signed registry rows for the kernel key and ONE human
      device (p:<ns>/founder); the marker DEV-ONLY-gnomon-home.json is written
      LAST. Refuses (falsifiers in test/dev-launch.test.ts): the production
      home ~/.gnomon, anything inside or containing it (production-home); a
      home holding a registry it did not write (production-root); any other
      non-empty unmarked home (home-not-empty). The production-home check
      (Aegis C1) covers ~/.gnomon lexically and realpath'd, its symlink target
      (dangling included), and on darwin compares case-folded (~/.GNOMON); a
      symlinked dev home is refused (unsafe-home). TRIPWIRE: gnomond (both
      paths) and `gnomon daemon start` refuse loudly a home holding the marker
      (daemon-cli.ts refuseDevHome). Loading (dev up) refuses a
      home without the ack-bearing marker (not-a-dev-home) and one whose keys
      do not match the marker or whose registry has ANY rejected row
      (dev-home-damaged). gnomond and a bare startDaemon never read these
      files: the production root stays unprovisioned (falsifier: device auth
      on a dev home without the dev root is refused root-unprovisioned). No
      command prints key material. `gnomon dev up [--home <abs>]`
      (startDevKernel) runs startDaemon IN THE CLI'S OWN PROCESS with the dev
      root and dev kernel key, connects as the registered human device through
      the published TS client (kernel key pinned, serverSig verified before the
      device signs), and mints the launch token through the existing
      in-process `gnomon open` path (DaemonHandle.requestWebLaunch, §11.2 rule
      2; the web endpoint's rules are untouched). The URL is printed ONLY to an
      interactive terminal (a pipe or file gets the port and no token); Enter
      mints a fresh one (Reconnect is UNSUPPORTED until M1.3), q / Ctrl-C stop
      the kernel. The CLI's device connection is held open, so the kernel does
      not idle out while dev up runs.
      SMOKE (test/dev-launch-session.test.ts, darwin): dev init → startDevKernel
      → launch URL → a second device client → session.open on the FAKE claude
      under the real session profile → session.send → turn.result, with no
      approval card on the path. The one seam is the internal-seams `daemon`
      slot (lifecycle/daemon.ts registers it; only testing.ts's
      startDaemonForTest reads it): the supervisor's test-rig options (script
      claude, its path, a throwaway user home and env) and the fuse's law-7
      billing source. A socket device connection is a community-client reader,
      so turn.event (the reply text) reaches it as {kind, hsid} stubs; the
      GUI's launch-token session is the first-party surface that sees it.
    path: src/cli/
    type: cli
    tags: [gnomon, dev-only, dogfood, identity, paradigm-8]
    tests:
      - test/dev-launch.test.ts
      - test/dev-launch-session.test.ts

  wrapper-channel:
    description: >-
      #wrapper-channel (spec §19.1 M1.13 row, Appendix A; G-GUI-12,
      G-WEB-01..06, G-GUI-03) — the gnomon-wrapper-channel:v1 launch contract
      between Gnomon.app (#gnomon-wrapper) and `gnomon up --wrapper` /
      `gnomon dev up --wrapper`. The app spawns `node --disable-sigusr1
      <gnomon.js> up --wrapper` with a clean env; the child writes NDJSON on
      its stdout pipe (up, launch{port, launchToken, kernelPublicKey,
      bundleHash, expiresAt}, launch-failed, refused{code, reason, message,
      next}, down) and reads `launch` | `quit` on stdin (EOF = quit). The
      token is on that pipe only. Child side: src/cli/launch-tty.ts
      (serveWrapperChannel, wrapperChannelProblem, channelRefused,
      WRAPPER_LAUNCH_CAP 8/min with one mint in flight, stopping re-checked
      after each mint, WRAPPER_UNSAFE_ENV_NEXT). Wrapper side:
      packages/gnomon-wrapper/src/contract.rs (parse_line, read_line_capped at
      64 KiB, redacting Debug).
    path: src/cli/launch-tty.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, wrapper, contract, M1.13]
    related: ["#gnomon-up", "#dev-launch", "#gnomon-wrapper", "#web-endpoint"]
    tests:
      - test/wrapper-channel.test.ts

  update-watch:
    description: >-
      #update-watch (TD-2026-10-19-063, flow 2) — self-update pickup. The
      build writes dist/build-id.json {version, commit, distHash,
      guiBundleHash} (scripts/build-stamp.mjs); at boot the kernel writes
      <home>/kernel-build.json (0600) with what it loaded
      (lifecycle/build-stamp.ts). lifecycle/update-watch.ts polls the stamp on
      the configured cadence UPDATE_POLL_MS (30 s) and, once per distinct new
      stamp, the kernel raises its OWN daemon-stop{reason:'upgrade'} card
      (broker/daemon-stop.ts raiseUpgrade, UPGRADE_CARD_TIMEOUT_MS 24 h,
      first line "Gnomon was updated: v→v′ (commit)"; at most one per
      UPGRADE_CARD_COOLDOWN_MS 1 h per kernel (Aegis L); it shares the one-card
      slot with a launcher's daemon.stop{reason:'upgrade'}). Accept takes one
      Touch ID (F3), drains (§2.4 rule 11), releases the lock and socket, and
      runForeground spawns ONE detached successor (lifecycle/successor.ts:
      reexecArgs + cleanReexecEnv + --prefer-port <old web port>, 127.0.0.1
      only, exclusive; a taken port falls back and status reports
      web.portFallback). The helper binary is watched on the same tick
      (identity/helper.ts HelperRepin): changed + nothing in flight ⇒ re-pinned
      through pinHelper and `serve` recycled, no card; unsigned, unverifiable
      or not holding this identity's keys ⇒ fail closed.
    tags: [kernel, lifecycle]
    signals: ["!daemon-update-detected"]
  release-pins:
    description: >-
      #release-pins (T-2026-10-21-028, TD-2026-10-21-758 zero-build, G-HLP-01;
      Aegis M1 review) — the exact native bytes THIS kernel build accepts,
      COMPILED INTO THE BUNDLE. npm run build:release (scripts/build-release.mjs):
      tsup (dev pass) → scripts/write-release-manifest.mjs (release-manifest:v1
      from the signed GnomonHelper.app / GnomonVoiceIO.app executables: name,
      --version ≥ the kernel floor, sha256, the requirement the kernel enforces
      checked with codesign -R, arch via lipo) → tsup again with
      GNOMON_RELEASE_MANIFEST, so tsup.config.ts defines __GNOMON_RELEASE__ and
      __GNOMON_RELEASE_MANIFEST__ → build-stamp.mjs (copies the manifest into
      the stamp for status only). lifecycle/release-pins.ts reads ONLY the
      compiled values: release ⇒ identity/helper.ts pinHelper and
      voice/voice-install.ts (install and recheckVoiceApp right before the
      spawn) require the pinned sha256 for this arch on top of the signature
      and version floor ('checksum'); a release with a missing or invalid
      compiled manifest is broken (every pin refused); deleting or editing the
      stamp, loading dist/index.js or copying dist never makes it dev. Not a
      release ⇒ dev (signature + version floor only). The daemon records the
      mode in <home>/kernel-build.json; `gnomon daemon status` reports build:
      release | dev | broken; the setup helper row says which.
    path: src/kernel/lifecycle/release-pins.ts
    type: module
    tags: [kernel, lifecycle, release, security]
    related: ["#identity", "#gnomon-voice", "#update-watch", "#setup-preflight"]
    tests:
      - test/release-pins.test.ts
      - test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts
  app-bundle:
    description: >-
      #app-bundle (T-2026-10-21-029, TD-2026-10-21-758 zero-build; 8.0 M2) —
      where a kernel running from a release Gnomon.app finds its siblings,
      derived from its OWN file's realpath at run time (never recorded), so a
      moved or copied app (M4: ~/Applications) still works. Layout:
      <X>.app/Contents/Resources/kernel (this kernel), Resources/node/bin/node
      (the bundled node: the wrapper spawns the kernel with it, so
      process.execPath and every child — gnomon-web, mods, the voice client,
      the model fetch, successors — use it, never PATH node),
      Resources/gnomon-voice (the voice client + sherpa), Helpers/GnomonHelper.app,
      Helpers/GnomonVoiceIO.app. appBundleOf(from) is null outside a bundle,
      where every caller keeps its monorepo / node_modules lookup. In a bundle:
      defaultHelperPath and helperPathFor (identity-cli, provision.ts
      openHardwareIdentity, setup-cli and setup-endpoint completeDevice) use the
      app's own helper over a recorded identity.json path (stale after a move);
      resolveVoiceClientEntry looks ONLY in Resources/gnomon-voice (never walks
      above the app); voiceIoSourceApp copies voice-io from Helpers. Trust is
      unchanged: requirement + release sha256 pin (#release-pins), never path.
      appInstallDir (M4's one install-location choice): /Applications when
      writable without sudo, else ~/Applications (App Management protection).
      8.0 M4 (T-2026-10-21-031): Resources/gnomon-mods (AppBundle.mods,
      BUNDLE_MODS_REL) is the first-party mods catalog; inside a bundle
      mods-admin findFirstPartyRoot reads ONLY it (null when absent, never
      walks out of the app). openHardwareIdentity takes `from` and `pins`
      (test seams) and HelperRepin `pins`: a running release kernel whose app
      was replaced in place refuses the newer helper (its compiled pin names
      the old sha256) until restart; the identity (SE keys bound to the
      helper's access group, never its path) survives a move, a replace and a
      raised helper floor (test/app-upgrade-identity.test.ts). The paradigm
      shim (#gnomon-app-installer) mirrors appInstallDir in plain JS.
    path: src/kernel/lifecycle/app-bundle.ts
    type: module
    tags: [kernel, lifecycle, release, distribution]
    related: ["#release-pins", "#release-darwin", "#identity", "#gnomon-voice", "#gnomon-wrapper", "#mods-admin", "#gnomon-app-installer"]
    tests:
      - test/app-bundle.test.ts
      - test/app-upgrade-identity.test.ts
  release-darwin:
    description: >-
      #release-darwin (T-2026-10-21-029, TD-2026-10-21-758; 8.0 M2) —
      scripts/release-darwin.mjs builds ONE relocatable Gnomon.app per arch on
      the founder's Mac (host arch only; another arch is refused; Gnomon 8.0
      ships for Apple silicon Macs only, TD-2026-10-22-310, so --arch x64 is
      refused with X64_REFUSED unless --experimental-x64): helper and
      voice-io (their own signed builds), build:gnomon + build:release (#release-pins
      compiled in), the official Node 24 tarball from nodejs.org REFUSED unless it
      hashes to packages/gnomon-wrapper/bundled-node.lock.json (never which node),
      a Contents-shaped stage (Resources/node, Resources/kernel = dist +
      package.json only: zero runtime deps, Resources/gnomon-voice = package,
      dist, models manifest, sherpa-onnx-node + its darwin-<arch> package),
      inside-out signing in the stage (sherpa libs, then node with
      NodeEntitlements.plist), cargo tauri build with
      GNOMON_WRAPPER_BUNDLE_STAGE (build.rs pins bundle-relative paths and the
      staged hashes), assembly with Helpers/ (ditto, signatures kept; the
      assembled app must re-hash to the staged pins), the app signed with its
      empty Entitlements.plist (no --deep), verify --strict --deep + the wrapper
      requirement, optional --notarize (notarize-darwin.mjs, keychain profile
      gnomon-notary), then ditto -c -k --keepParent zip + .sha256. --unsigned
      = ad-hoc (CI/tests); --dry-run = no swift/npm/cargo/network/codesign,
      placeholder binaries, the real layout and pin logic. kernelTreeHash is the
      JS twin of the wrapper's tree.rs (shared test vector).
      Aegis M2 review: the lock must carry gpg provenance from
      scripts/bump-bundled-node.mjs (SHASUMS256.txt.sig verified against the
      vendored Node release keys, both tarballs checked, signer recorded);
      staged trees refuse symlinks (dereference false); node is signed with
      identifier gnomon.node and verified with codesign -R against the
      requirement the wrapper compiles; the built wrapper must report
      --build-mode bundle before signing; --unsigned signs node ad-hoc without
      the hardened runtime (ad-hoc sherpa addons fail library validation).
      8.0 M3 (T-2026-10-21-030): step 10 copies the zip + sha256 into the npm
      platform package as Gnomon.app.zip(.sha256) (copyIntoPlatformPackage; a
      SIGNED release defaults to packages/gnomon-darwin-<arch>, an --unsigned
      or --dry-run build only with --package-dir); --dry-run --zip runs the
      ditto zip on the placeholder app, whose placeholder GnomonHelper.app
      carries a versioned framework with real symlinks (PLACEHOLDER_FRAMEWORK)
      for the pack round trip.
      8.0 M4 (T-2026-10-21-031): stageMods copies packages/gnomon-mods byte
      for byte (no symlinks, no native code) to Resources/gnomon-mods
      (LAYOUT.mods, required by missingFromLayout); the dry-run test checks
      the moved app's kernel finds them and each still hashes to a
      FIRST_PARTY_PACKAGE_HASHES pin.
    path: scripts/release-darwin.mjs
    type: script
    tags: [release, distribution, signing]
    related: ["#app-bundle", "#release-pins", "#gnomon-wrapper", "#gnomon-helper", "#gnomon-voice"]
    tests:
      - test/release-darwin.test.ts
  gnomon-npm-packaging:
    description: >-
      #gnomon-npm-packaging (T-2026-10-21-030, TD-2026-10-21-758; 8.0 M3) —
      @a-company/gnomon is PUBLIC (publishConfig access public; files dist +
      gnomon-mods; bins gnomon, gnomond, gnomon-web; exports ./cli and
      ./package.json for the paradigm shim). Its tarball carries no unpublished
      dependency: gnomon-contracts is a devDependency inlined by tsup
      (noExternal; the G-KRN-02 dist scan), and scripts/prepack.mjs copies the
      first-party mods (packages/gnomon-mods, no package.json, symlinks
      refused) to packages/gnomon/gnomon-mods byte for byte so
      findFirstPartyRoot resolves them beside an installed kernel with their
      pinned packageHash; postpack removes the copy. gnomon-local, mod-sdk and
      gui are not runtime imports and do not ship. optionalDependencies are
      exactly @a-company/gnomon-darwin-arm64 at this version (the one
      G-SEC-01 exception: a zip-only package the kernel never imports).
      Apple silicon only in 8.0 (TD-2026-10-22-310, "Gnomon 8.0 ships for
      Apple silicon Macs only"): there is no x64 platform package; the
      lockstep flags any other gnomon-darwin-* optional dependency and the
      sync removes it, the publish gate, the release preflight, prepack and
      seal-npm-release cover arm64 only, and release-darwin refuses --arch
      x64 without --experimental-x64 (the x64 path is kept, unpublished).
      Node floor: engines node >=24.0.0, the same as paradigm and every other
      package (8.0 is Node 24 everywhere; scripts/gnomon-node-gate.mjs is gone).
      Lockstep: scripts/sync-gnomon-versions.mjs (repo root; --check for CI)
      makes the platform package version, this optional dep and
      @a-company/paradigm's exact dependency follow this package's version;
      test/version-lockstep.test.ts fails on drift. The platform packages are
      excluded from the root workspaces ("!packages/gnomon-darwin-*"): their
      os/cpu fields make npm install fail on the other arch. Acceptance:
      scripts/pack-roundtrip.mjs builds a zip with release-darwin --dry-run
      --unsigned --zip (or takes --zip <signed zip>), copies it into a copy of
      the platform package, npm pack, tar -x, ditto -x -k, then checks the
      file set, the sha256, the app layout (missingFromLayout), every symlink
      against the source app and each framework's Versions/Current, and runs
      codesign --verify --strict --deep + spctl -a only for a Developer ID
      signature (otherwise skipped with a message); it reports packed and
      unpacked sizes.
      Aegis M3 review: (high 1) nothing unsigned or dev is publishable.
      `npm run publish:gnomon` runs scripts/gate-gnomon-publish.mjs FIRST as a
      plain command (lifecycle hooks are skippable with --ignore-scripts; it is
      also gnomon's prepublishOnly): lockstep; each platform package has no
      scripts and exact files, and its zip, unpacked with ditto into a temp
      dir, passes codesign --verify --deep --strict -R (the wrapper
      requirement, team OU SW4BMQ8PNL), xcrun stapler validate and spctl -a -t
      exec; gnomon's dist is a RELEASE build of this version whose compiled
      appZips equal the arm64 zip. prepack refuses a non-release dist too.
      Release order (scripts/publish-gnomon.mjs, #publish-gnomon): gate, then
      arm64, then gnomon, verified on npm, before paradigm. CI never
      publishes them: `npm run release` (scripts/release.mjs, #release-publish)
      refuses unless both are already on npm at this version, so
      changeset publish skips them and paradigm cannot ship first
      (test/release-excludes-gnomon.test.ts).
      release-darwin's `signed` now means Developer ID + notarized + stapled
      (stapler validate after notarize) and --package-dir refuses anything
      else without --allow-unsigned-for-tests. (high 2) each zip's sha256 is
      COMPILED into the published gnomon: scripts/seal-npm-release.mjs runs
      build-release.mjs with write-release-manifest --app-zip <arch>=<zip> for
      the arm64 platform zip (release-manifest:v1 appZips; the contract
      still admits x64 for when it returns); src/kernel/lifecycle/
      app-zip.ts verifyAppZip checks a zip against that pin (dev ⇒ refused
      `dev`, no entry ⇒ `unpinned`, mismatch ⇒ `checksum`) and never reads the
      shipped .sha256; platformPackageZip realpaths the resolved package and
      checks its name. Exported as appZip from the package index; the M4
      installer must call it before unpacking. Changesets cannot exclude
      these packages (its `ignore` is versioning-only and rejects
      non-workspace packages and paradigm's dependency on gnomon), so
      release.yml's `changeset publish` is stopped by gnomon's prepack and
      prepublishOnly gate instead, and never sees the platform packages
      (not workspaces).
    path: scripts/prepack.mjs
    type: script
    tags: [release, distribution, npm, gnomon]
    related: ["#release-darwin", "#gnomon-darwin-arm64", "#gnomon-shim", "#mods-admin"]
    tests:
      - test/version-lockstep.test.ts
      - test/pack-roundtrip.test.ts
      - test/sec-01.test.ts
      - test/app-zip.test.ts
      - test/gate-gnomon-publish.test.ts
      - test/release-darwin.test.ts
      - test/release-excludes-gnomon.test.ts
  gui-resume:
    description: >-
      #gui-resume (TD-2026-10-19-063, founder ruling "ONE Touch ID") — the
      `{resumeToken}` web auth form (kernel/web/resume.ts). Minted only when
      an upgrade card is accepted from a live GUI session: 32 random bytes
      sent to THAT connection alone (gui.resume notification); the digest,
      bound to the session's principal, device id and root ns, is handed to
      the successor on its STDIN pipe together with its kernel key (Aegis M:
      never a file; a record planted in the home is never read, and none is
      read without --kernel-key-stdin). The successor serves on the old
      kernel's listening socket, inherited as fd 3 (--web-listen-fd), so the
      port is never unbound (Aegis H); on any other port the record is
      dropped. The GUI checks the successor signs a fresh nonce with the
      delivered key before it reloads. Refused if malformed, expired or with a
      TTL above RESUME_TOKEN_TTL_MS 60 s; accepted ONCE: replay, expiry, a device
      no longer that principal's human device, or another root ns are
      refused; RESUME_MAX_MISSES wrong tokens burn it. It authenticates a GUI
      connection only — never presence, never a card decision.
    tags: [kernel, web, auth]
  gui-relaunch:
    description: >-
      #gui-relaunch (T-2026-10-20-001) — a kernel restarted outside the
      self-update path (stop, then start) reopens the GUI with no terminal.
      (1) A start without a TTY opens it: printLaunch with no TTY hands the
      launch URL to the browser opener ONLY (osascript stdin; never printed),
      and `gnomon daemon start | --detach` / `gnomond` (activator) open it
      after a FRESH ready through cli/relaunch.ts openGuiAfterStart — the same
      gui.open path bare `gnomon` uses (home pin verifies the hello before the
      device signs; one shared launch-grant cap; single use, 60 s). `--no-open`
      / GNOMON_NO_OPEN=1 turn it off; `gnomond --foreground --no-open` is a
      usage refusal (the foreground kernel never opens anything). (2) NO
      remembered web port (Aegis H1, withdrawn from 945349a3): a port reused
      across stop/start lets a local squatter serve the old origin while
      Gnomon is down, so a reloaded stale tab could plant a service worker
      that the next auto-opened `#launch=` would load into. Every plain start
      binds an OS-chosen port and deletes any leftover `web-port.json`
      without reading it; the upgrade successor keeps its inherited fd. The
      stale tab cannot rejoin in place (each start has a new ephemeral key
      that only a launch fragment delivers); see gnomon-gui core/stale.ts.
    tags: [kernel, web, cli, lifecycle]
  bare-entry:
    description: >-
      #bare-entry (spec §2.4 rule 8) — bare `gnomon` in a folder, like
      `claude`: src/cli/bare-cli.ts runs #gnomon-up's runUp (every guard
      unchanged and in order: unsafe-env, unprovisioned → `next: gnomon
      identity init`, dev-home, …) with refusal code GNOMON and a FOLDER HINT
      — folderOf(cwd) = the nearest ancestor holding .git, else the cwd
      (said so) — carried ONLY as a launch URL fragment parameter
      `&cwd=<base64url utf-8>` appended after the token on the same TTY line
      (launch-tty.ts printLaunch urlSuffix; never argv, env, a file, the
      wrapper channel or another line; over HINT_MAX_BYTES 1024 it is not
      carried). displayPath escapes every code point that is not a letter,
      number, punctuation, symbol or plain space, plus Cf, default-ignorables
      (fillers, variation selectors, tags), U+FFFD and backslash, as \u{..},
      and caps at 240 code points (shared vectors
      test/fixtures/cwd-hint.v1.json, Aegis L3); the GUI
      (#gui, core/launch.ts cwdHintOf/applyCwdHint) applies the same rule,
      prefills only a clean absolute path and never submits. A kernel
      already running on the home: no second one starts. On an interactive
      terminal bare `gnomon` asks it for a launch grant over the P8-D98
      human-device verb `gui.open {cwdHint}` (up-cli.ts requestRunningLaunch:
      this home's device key + root-signed human row checked like
      startUpKernel's deviceOf, the kernel hello verified against the home's
      pin via kernelPinOf, then device auth; T-2026-10-19-001 A3) and
      prints/opens that URL with the same `&cwd=` fragment (ATTACHED readout).
      Not a terminal: the TTY check runs BEFORE the RPC, nothing on stdout,
      no gui.open call, `REFUSED GNOMON already-running`, exit 3
      (BARE_EXIT_ALREADY_RUNNING), naming the instance from the pidfile pid
      classified via `ps -o command=` (diagnostic only). The kernel side is
      #socket src/kernel/socket/gui-open.ts (createGuiOpenHandler, wired in
      DispatcherOptions.humanVerbs by the daemon): verified human device
      only; cwdHint ≤ 1024 UTF-8 bytes else BAD_REQUEST{cwd-hint-too-long},
      returned verbatim, never read or logged; the grant is the in-process
      requestWebLaunch's (single-use, at most 60 s) under the ONE shared
      launch-grant cap src/kernel/web/launch-cap.ts (LaunchGrantCap on
      WebEndpoint.requestLaunch: one in flight, 8 a minute across the
      terminal, the wrapper channel and gui.open — shared, not added on
      top; ENGINE{web-launch-capped}). Fairness (Aegis M2): gui.open is a
      `socket` asker that may hold at most LAUNCH_GRANT_SOCKET_SHARE (4) of
      the 8 window slots, so the in-process asker (terminal, wrapper) always
      keeps the rest; a socket mint in flight (a cold start) makes ONE
      in-process asker wait and go next, never refuses it; a mint that
      throws refunds its slot (the token is the mint's last throwing step).
      The human-device verbs are UNIX-SOCKET ONLY (Aegis L1): the daemon
      builds them as their own `humanVerbs` object and hands it only to the
      unix dispatcher (daemon.ts dispatcherOptionsFor), so the web
      dispatcher reports them unavailable and refuses them. A grant whose
      caller's connection closed during the mint is withdrawn
      (GuiAccess.withdraw via WebEndpoint.withdrawLaunch, Aegis L2), never
      left pending. Falsified in test/gui-open.test.ts and
      test/human-device-verbs.test.ts. Hardening: requestRunningLaunch
      refuses a gui.open grant whose url fails isOpenableUrl
      (gui-open-bad-grant) and sanitizes the server's refusal text
      (sanitizeDisplayText); bare entry builds `&cwd=` from its own capped
      folder, never the kernel's echoed cwdHint (Aegis L3, L4). log.read's
      one-page since-ahead is judged against releasedHead(), as subscribe
      does (Aegis L6).
      Also `gnomon help|--help|-h` (GNOMON_HELP, one line per verb) and an
      unknown verb → usage exit 64 without echoing it.
      scripts/install-darwin.mjs is the idempotent installer (build, link
      `gnomon` into /opt/homebrew/bin or /usr/local/bin, refuse a
      non-symlink or a link elsewhere, NODE_OPTIONS check, next steps; no
      sudo, never touches ~/.gnomon). AUTO-OPEN (spec §2.4 rule 10):
      src/cli/open-browser.ts openInDefaultBrowser hands every terminal
      launch/setup URL to the default browser via LaunchServices — it runs
      /usr/bin/osascript - (absolute, no shell, EMPTY env) and writes
      `open location "<url>"` on its STDIN, so the token is on no argv or env
      (`open <url>` is forbidden: argv is readable by ps); the URL is
      validated first (loopback, fragment [A-Za-z0-9_=&%.-] only), 5 s
      timeout, never throws. The TTY shows `opened in your browser` with the
      URL kept below as the fallback, or the old copy line on failure /
      non-darwin. Covers bare `gnomon`, `gnomon up`, `gnomon setup`, the
      Enter fresh URL, the setup re-link on expiry and the setup→up hand-off.
      Off with `--no-open` (all three verbs) or GNOMON_NO_OPEN=1; never
      without a TTY; never on the wrapper channel (`up --wrapper`).
    path: src/cli/bare-cli.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, cli, launch, dogfood]
    related: ["#gnomon-up", "#gui", "#wrapper-channel", "#web-endpoint"]
    tests:
      - test/bare-entry.test.ts
      - ../gnomon-gui/test/gui.test.ts
      - test/open-browser.test.ts
      - test/open-browser-darwin.test.ts

  provisioner:
    description: >-
      #provisioner (spec §3.1 Provisioner; T-2026-10-19-005 MVP-a) — the ONE
      engine behind `gnomon identity init|--rotate|--recover|purge-keys`
      (identity-cli.ts, a thin renderer, byte-identical output) and the guided
      #first-run (and the GUI setup phase, MVP-b). An EventEmitter with an
      AbortSignal: events step / presence {n, of, purpose, digest12} / done /
      aborted; it renders nothing. Enforces Aegis I1 (a root signature is
      accepted only when the helper's own prompt line, reply.shown, names the
      digest shown: helperLineShows; HardwareRootAuthority.signRowShown), I5
      (helper path pinned, from the caller default or identity.json), I6 (a
      generation it did not create is never adopted; purgeGeneration takes
      presence per key), I7 (ROOT_PROMPT_BUDGET init 2 / rotate 3 / recover 2
      / complete 0), I8 (O_EXCL `<home>.provisioning.lock`, provisioning-locked,
      before key.list), I9 (pre-commit failure restores moved files and
      deletes only keys it created), I10 (after identity.json, keys are never
      deleted; completeDevice() appends the device row the root signed during
      that provisioning, saved in identity.json as pendingDeviceRow with
      deviceKeyId — verified under the root, keyId === device key on disk ===
      deviceKeyId, deviceId/principalId === the record's; ZERO new signatures,
      budget 0; no saved row → no-saved-device-row, use --rotate; Aegis
      HIGH-1), I12 (validates name/host itself).
    path: src/kernel/identity/provisioner.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, identity, setup]
    related: ["#identity", "#first-run", "#setup-preflight", "#gnomon-helper"]
    tests:
      - test/identity-cli.test.ts
      - test/setup.test.ts

  setup-endpoint:
    description: >-
      #setup-endpoint (spec §11.2 rule 11, §2.4 rule 9; T-2026-10-19-005
      MVP-b) — the SETUP MODE loopback endpoint: a #web-endpoint (same
      gnomon-web transport child, rules.ts, 127.0.0.1 only, the SAME pinned
      #gui bundle) whose dispatcher is the closed gnomon-setup:v1 protocol
      (gnomon-contracts setup.ts, own SETUP_API_ID; clientApiId unchanged):
      hello / auth {setupToken} / setup.preflight / setup.name / setup.begin /
      setup.purge {gen} / setup.complete (I10, the saved row, 0 prompts) /
      setup.finish, events setup.state and setup.presence
      {n,of,purpose,digest12}. No gnomon-rpc verb is reachable. An ephemeral
      Ed25519 setup key signs serverSig. One setup connection; a closed
      socket aborts the #provisioner at its next step boundary. Exact params
      key sets: the page carries only name, a server-listed gen, and clicks.
      Entry: bare gnomon / gnomon setup on a TTY print the setup URL (and
      every expected digest: the terminal is the surface to trust); gnomon
      setup --cli keeps the prompts; up --wrapper emits {t:setup,...} and then
      continues into {t:up}. Falsified in test/setup-endpoint.test.ts
      (G-SET-08..14) and test/wrapper-channel.test.ts. Never hangs: it
      settles on finish (also for an already-set-up home), token expiry, a
      10 min idle cap, socket close, transport stop or token reuse; a refused
      purge (GNOMON_HOME guard, in-use / identity-malformed) or a failed
      begin returns to the checks. The checks are read-only: setup state
      lives in a private scratch dir and the home is created at begin.
      stop() removes that scratch dir BEFORE it waits for a running step, so
      the wrapper's quit / stdin EOF (Gnomon.app Cmd-Q, which kills the
      kernel at most 10 s later) cleans it even with a Touch ID prompt held
      open; an EOF that came before waitSetup listened also stops it. A
      failed rm (e.g. EACCES) never rejects stop(); the next run sweeps it.
    path: src/kernel/setup/setup-endpoint.ts
    type: service
    related: ["#provisioner", "#setup-preflight", "#web-endpoint", "#gui-setup", "#first-run"]
    tests:
      - test/setup-endpoint.test.ts
      - test/wrapper-channel.test.ts
    gates: ["^setup-token", "^unprovisioned-only"]
    tags: [kernel, setup, web]
  setup-preflight:
    description: >-
      #setup-preflight (spec §2.4 rule 9) — READ-ONLY first-run checks (no
      key, no file write, no prompt; only key.list): helper signed+pinned and
      not from a sandboxed shell, touch-id (helper 1.4.0 presence.available:
      LAContext canEvaluatePolicy, never a prompt; pass or fail), macos (floor 15 =
      SESSION_SANDBOX_MIN_MACOS, TD-2026-10-22-220: below it fails naming the update,
      matching the app's LSMinimumSystemVersion and the helper/voice-io targets;
      test/macos-floor.test.ts pins them), sip (csrutil;
      off = warn), claude (resolved, pin none/match/mismatch → `gnomon session
      repin-claude`), law-7 billing env, runtime (NODE_OPTIONS / node flags),
      node floor (NODE_MAJOR_FLOOR 24, equal to scripts/install-darwin.mjs: the
      source-install floor; a zero-build release bundles its own Node),
      leftover generations (purge, never adopt), home state (dev
      marker, symlink, malformed identity.json: shown, never touched). Each
      check {id, status pass|warn|fail, detail, fix}. INFORMATIVE only (Aegis
      I12): #provisioner and #gnomon-up enforce on their own. The helper row
      names the build's #release-pins mode (release sha256-pinned, or dev).
    path: src/kernel/setup/preflight.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, setup]
    related: ["#first-run", "#provisioner"]
    tests:
      - test/setup.test.ts

  first-run:
    description: >-
      #first-run (spec §2.4 rule 9) — the guided first run. Default: the
      setup SCREENS (#setup-endpoint, #gui-setup; `gnomon setup` and bare
      `gnomon` print a one-shot setup URL and every expected digest, the
      terminal being the surface to trust; Gnomon.app runs it in its
      window). `gnomon setup --cli` keeps the plain readline prompts (no in-terminal UI, P8-D51): welcome paragraph,
      #setup-preflight results with fixes, leftover generations offered for
      purge, a name prompt prefilled from $USER (PRINCIPAL_NAME_PATTERN,
      re-checked by #provisioner), "exactly 2 Touch ID prompts; a third is an
      attack", then 1 of 2 / 2 of 2 each preceded by the expected digest shown
      big and grouped on the terminal (I2), a summary (root keyId, principal,
      device, files) and the hand-off to #gnomon-up's runUp in the same
      process. Bare `gnomon` (#bare-entry) on a TTY with an unprovisioned
      default home runs it first, then continues with the folder hint. Home
      strict (I4: never GNOMON_HOME, lstat, symlink refused — the raw
      `gnomon identity init` still honours GNOMON_HOME/--home). Gated by
      ^unprovisioned-only. `up` / `up --wrapper` unprovisioned refusals now
      name the first run. On a terminal each setup URL (and each re-link
      after expiry) is also opened in the default browser (#bare-entry's
      open-browser.ts, osascript stdin; `gnomon setup --no-open` or
      GNOMON_NO_OPEN=1 prints only, and --no-open carries into the up
      hand-off); the wrapper path never opens.
    path: src/cli/setup-cli.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, cli, setup, onboarding]
    related: ["#provisioner", "#setup-preflight", "#bare-entry", "#gnomon-up", "$first-run", "^unprovisioned-only"]
    tests:
      - test/setup.test.ts
      - test/bare-entry.test.ts
      - test/open-browser-darwin.test.ts

  gnomon-up:
    description: >-
      #gnomon-up (spec §2.4 rule 7) — `gnomon up [--home <abs>] [--wrapper]`, the
      production counterpart of `gnomon dev up`: the REAL-HOME GUI launcher.
      M1.13 (#gnomon-wrapper): `--wrapper` (also on `gnomon dev up`) is the
      mode Gnomon.app spawns: launch-tty.ts serveWrapperChannel speaks
      gnomon-wrapper-channel:v1 NDJSON on stdout (up / launch{port,
      launchToken, kernelPublicKey, bundleHash, expiresAt} / launch-failed /
      refused{code,reason,message,next} / down) and reads `launch` | `quit`
      on stdin (EOF = quit); human lines go to stderr; a stdout or stdin that
      is a terminal or a regular file is refused `wrapper-channel` before any
      kernel starts. The token is on that pipe only — never argv, env, a file
      or a log (test/wrapper-channel.test.ts, P07-style ps argv+env sampler
      with a positive control).
      src/cli/up-cli.ts; the in-process launcher and terminal loop are shared
      with #dev-launch in src/cli/launch-tty.ts (attachInProcess, printLaunch,
      serveInProcess: one copy of the launch rules). Runs startDaemon IN THE
      CLI'S OWN PROCESS on a PROVISIONED home (default ~/.gnomon; never
      GNOMON_HOME, like gnomond) with the hardware identity opened by
      openHardwareIdentity — the same path gnomond takes (root + approvals key
      behind the pinned signed helper; the helper is closed on every failure
      and at shutdown) — connects as the home's registered human device
      (loadDeviceCredential: one owner-only read) with the kernel key pinned
      in-process, and mints the launch token through
      DaemonHandle.requestWebLaunch. URL ONLY to an interactive terminal;
      Enter re-mints; q / Ctrl-C stop. Approvals from the GUI: broker →
      HelperApprovalsKey.signDecision (fresh presence, Touch ID) →
      decisionSig + helper-se presence token. DESIGN (lore): in-process, NOT
      `gnomon open` against a running gnomond — that needs a new wire verb
      and moves clientApiId. GUARDS on a home resolved once (lstat plain
      directory, then realpath), each with a falsifier in
      test/up-cli.test.ts: FIRST (Aegis C1, also in dev up) a non-empty
      NODE_OPTIONS or any node interpreter flag (process.execArgv) other
      than exactly --disable-sigusr1, or that flag MISSING (T-2026-10-18-030:
      the bin shebang `env -S node --disable-sigusr1` passes it) →
      unsafe-env — refused, not re-executed, so no second process holds
      the helper (scope: an AMBIENT unsafe runtime, not a hostile preload
      already running as the user); a stdout/stderr 'error' (EIO after
      SIGHUP) is handled as a stop so the shutdown sweep runs; the device
      key opens O_NONBLOCK (a swapped-in FIFO cannot hang it,
      test/device-key-fifo.test.ts); missing home / no identity.json → unprovisioned;
      symlink / non-directory → unsafe-home; DEV-ONLY marker → dev-home; a
      live pidfile holder or an answering socket → daemon-running (and the
      O_EXCL lock as backstop); any IdentityError → identity-<reason> (fail
      closed); a registry row this root does not verify → registry-damaged;
      a device key without its matching human row → device-unregistered.
      Never prints a key or a token. Known gap: the kernel key is still
      ephemeral per start with no root-signed row, so OTHER socket clients
      report serverVerified:false (the keystore item). Seams (UpDeps: start,
      verifier, startHelper, userHome) are programmatic only; gnomon.ts
      passes none (falsifier). END TO END (test/up-launch-session.test.ts,
      darwin): identity init with the FakeHelper → gnomon up → launch URL →
      the GUI WebSocket authenticates with the launch token → session.open on
      the fake claude raises a plan card → GUI approve → one helper prompt →
      approval.decide{presence:fresh, via:gui} whose decisionSig verifies
      under the approvals key.
    path: src/cli/
    type: cli
    tags: [gnomon, identity, launcher, paradigm-8]
    tests:
      - test/up-cli.test.ts
      - test/up-launch-session.test.ts

  manifest-loader:
    description: >-
      Aegis final review: the grants lock is taken by linking a temp file
      holding pid:nonce (O_EXCL fallback where links are unsupported, L1); a
      deny over an install whose rows are written is refused (M3).
      TD-2026-10-20-514: prepareInstall (every check, rows BUILT with cardId,
      checked against the card's detail.rootRows) / commitInstall (token consumed,
      appendSignedGrantRows); buildGrantRow(s) and appendSignedGrantRows re-check
      every signed row under a cross-process lock file (grant-lock.ts: torn-tail
      repair, dir fsync, one in-flight root decision per home) and write once,
      grant.install last; the reader drops a cardId row whose batch anchor is
      missing (unanchored).
      S0b user-mcp.ts: userMcpServerNames(home, {repo?}) reads ONLY server NAMES
      (never commands, args or env) from ~/.claude.json (user scope mcpServers and
      local scope projects.<path>.mcpServers) and <repo>/.mcp.json (project scope,
      when a repo is given), fail-soft on a missing or unparseable file; fed to the
      install/decide/load COLLISION check so a mod's gm_<pub>_<name> server never
      collides with a server the user already has (Aegis S0b M1).
      #manifest-loader, the Desk (TD-2026-10-20-812). manifest.ts: provides.offers
      (offer-spec:v1 from gnomon-contracts, at most 6, unique kinds, needs a
      provides.mcp server since reach is mcp) and 'offers' in WIDENING_FIELDS (key
      kind@phase→handoffTool; a text-only change is no widening); a provides.mcp entry
      may declare a closed per-user override surface config{command?:true, args?:true,
      env?:[names]} (env names never PATH/HOME/NODE_OPTIONS/… nor LD_/DYLD_/ANTHROPIC_/
      CLAUDE_/GNOMON_/NPM_CONFIG_/GIT_; a config is part of the mcp widening key);
      validateMcpUserConfig + resolveMcpLaunch admit only what it declares, under
      COMMAND_UNSAFE (first-party servers launch as node <absolute path> from other
      repos; a user-set command is an absolute path, spawn with shell:false). Env
      names: MCP_ENV_DENY + MCP_ENV_DENY_PREFIXES (Aegis M1), and below first-party
      <NAME>_* or *_API_KEY / *_TOKEN only. Each offer contextIn source is its own
      widening key (kind:in:src). Not yet wired into a launch path. RESERVED_MOD_NAMES
      (kernel, gnomon, system, paradigm, claude, …; NAME at every tier), FIRST_PARTY_MOD_NAMES
      and FIRST_PARTY_NAME_PREFIXES (gnomon-, paradigm-, claude-, anthropic-) first-party only. install.ts surfaceOf: one 'offer:' card line per offer and a
      'user may set:' suffix on an mcp line; both absent when undeclared, so pre-812
      card detail hashes are unchanged.
      #manifest-loader + policy (spec §3.6, §4.3, §12.5, §15.4, §15.5, §15.7,
      §16; §19.1 M1.5). src/kernel/manifest/. manifest.ts — manifest:v1
      validation, producer-strict and fail-closed (wire form: the JSON subset
      of the §4.3 YAML in gnomon-manifest.json, zero deps): unknown keys and
      trust:/source:/postinstall refused; kind: mod needs abi mod-abi:v1, an
      unknown abi refused; a name equal to a kernel namespace token refused;
      kind adapter/provider first-party only, a provider ships only
      provides.knowledge; tool effects ⊆ read|write|network|spend; card.* and
      status.mod slots append-only; events under modns.<name>.*; keybindings
      bind declared commands, never the reserved decision keys; capabilities
      are §15.4 forms (approve/band:write never; voice.approve:<class> never
      from a manifest; knowledge:federate/link:send first-party only;
      log.append only on its own modns); provides.knowledge needs
      knowledge:provide and never egress. manifestHash = sha256(canonical
      JSON); detectWidening = additions by field (effects, egress,
      capabilities, events, tools, views, commands, keybindings, knowledge).
      egress.ts — Anthropic hosts (anthropic.com, claude.ai, claude.com and
      subdomains, and any wildcard covering them) refused in manifests and
      grant rows (G-MAN-01). snapshot.ts — the snapshot copier: copies a
      package or linked folder to <home>/mods/<name>/pinned/<hex>/ (owner-only,
      read-only), packageHash over the sorted tree of the COPY (exact bytes,
      .git skipped unread), refusing symlink/junction, hardlink (nlink > 1),
      .node addon, a path escaping the folder, special files, case-colliding
      paths and a swap between walk and read (fstat dev/ino), emitting
      mod.snapshot.refused + manifest.refuse{SNAPSHOT}; lockfile rule
      (node_modules needs an npm lockfile, the card lists every
      package@version; other formats refused, LOCKFILE); lifecycle install
      scripts refused (POSTINSTALL); diffTrees for the re-approve card.
      grants.ts — <home>/grants.jsonl, root-signed rows through the
      RootAuthority seam (production refuses until M1.3), fail-closed reader
      (unsigned, untrusted signer, bad signature, unknown kind, malformed,
      Anthropic egress, egress beside knowledge:provide → rejected[]);
      grant.install carries `name` beside the §15.4 fields; the effective pin
      is the last grant.install row. install.ts — stageInstall (package or
      linked) raises a PENDING card:v1 kind install with its detail (name,
      source, tier, effects, egress, capabilities, events, tools, views, read
      paths, package@version list, widening, file diff, stagedBy) and signs
      nothing (manifest.staged). The kernel-composed line `runs commands: N
      mcp server(s), M hook(s)` follows the packageHash line, before any
      manifest-controlled line (Aegis round 2 C2); mcp.command, mcp.args,
      hooks.command and hooks.event refuse SCHEMA on C0/C1, Zl, Zp and Cf
      (U+2028/U+2029 would otherwise draw a fake card line, C1); a linked
      source whose realpath has such a character is refused SCHEMA at stage
      and INTEGRITY at re-derivation, and the header is one display line
      (version and source middle-truncated for display; contentHash keeps
      the full detail), so the count line is always index 2 (Aegis round 3). The card's lines put every mcp server and
      every hook on its OWN line and are sanitized and WRAPPED by code points
      under the broker's MAX_LINE (continuations `  ↳ `), never cut, so a long
      mcp argument list cannot push a hook out of sight; the sanitizer flags
      travel with the lines (blockFor → rederiveInstallDetail → the broker),
      and contentHash still covers the full detail (T-2026-10-18-015 item 2);
      loadMod loads only from a pinned snapshot
      under a root-signed grant.install, re-hashes it (INTEGRITY), checks
      grants, and for a linked folder re-hashes the source: a change pauses
      the mod (mod.paused) and stages one re-approve card (manifest.widened
      when widened). The card DECISION and the human rollback (re-pin a
      previously approved snapshot, mod.rolled-back) are module-private and
      registered only on the internal seam channel (slot `manifest`), reached
      only through src/kernel/testing.ts (decideInstallCard,
      rollbackModSnapshot) until the M1.6 approval broker owns cards; both
      need a PresenceToken (mod.install / mod.rollback) and a signing root.
      layers.ts — layersHash (managed, user settings*.json + CLAUDE.md +
      skills/plugins/agents/commands listings, project and worktree .claude/**
      + CLAUDE.md + CLAUDE.local.md) and gitHash (.git/config, hooks/**,
      info/** in the common dir, a worktree's .git file followed,
      .gitattributes), CRLF→LF and case-folded NFC path keys on every platform
      (G-WIN-04). policy.ts — the per-session policy DATA for the `session`
      profile (keys/Warpline-token/browser read denies, ~/.gnomon + settings
      layers + linked folders write denies, endpoint-port loopback deny,
      process-info deny-others, launchers deny; realpath'd; policyHash) and the
      matching policy.json permissions block — the platform profile is
      generated by M1.7. generate.ts — plugin.json, .mcp.json, hooks.json and
      .gnomon/.gitignore from a kind: distro manifest into the repo's
      .gnomon/, each with a marker and a ledger; a fixed point; never
      overwrites a file that is not ours; uninstallRepo removes exactly the
      ledger's unedited files and the dirs it created (project.yaml stays);
      uninstallHome removes ~/.gnomon or returns it as the export path.
      ROUND 2 (review + Aegis F1–F11): generate.ts confines every path to the
      fixed generated set under <repo>/.gnomon (lstat each component, refuse
      links/junctions, realpath inside .gnomon, temp file O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW +
      rename) and keys ownership on the manifestHash marker plus the
      kernel-held record <home>/generated/<sha256(realpath repo)>.json (the
      repo holds no ledger); the session policy write-denies the whole
      ~/.claude/** and ~/.claude.json (both also hashed into layersHash — a
      gap in spec §3.3/§15.5 to amend) and .git/hooks|config|info (gitHash
      inputs; §15.5 amendment), and carries the platform-neutral deny classes
      (tmux/screen sockets, helper IPC, sysmond, AppleEvents, service
      manager, mic/camera, launchers) mapped per platform in
      src/platform/session-denies.ts; widening covers mcp and hooks and the
      card lists commands, keybindings, knowledge, mcp, hooks and opaque
      array counts; the lockfile rule is case-folded, applies at any depth
      and cross-checks shipped package.json versions; the tier is derived
      (linked ⇒ local, first-party only from a bundled packageHash allowlist,
      empty for now; a signed claim is refused until trusted keys exist);
      decideInstall validates the cardId, recomputes contentHash, re-derives
      the detail from the pinned snapshot, appends grant.install LAST and is
      idempotent; egress refuses ports, numeric hosts, single labels and
      local/private names; symlinked layers hash the target bytes and
      .claude/worktrees/ is excluded. The case-collision oracle is
      exercised on Linux CI (a case-insensitive FS cannot hold the pair).
      Events: §9.1 kinds only (manifest.*, mod.*); the spec declares no
      signals. Refusals are refusal:v1 with data.manifestCode (not
      data.reason, which would move clientApiId).
    path: src/kernel/manifest/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, manifest, policy, paradigm-8, m1.5]
    tests:
      - test/manifest-offers.test.ts
      - test/manifest-loader.test.ts
      - test/seams-fence.test.ts
    oracles: [G-MAN-01, G-MAN-02, G-MAN-03, G-HON-04, G-LAW-04, G-WIN-04]

  kernel-log:
    description: >-
      TD-2026-10-20-514 (Aegis C1f, G-ID-12): replay's checkRootDecisions
      checks every v2 root decision's rowsDigest against the logged card's
      root-rows detail and, with the pinned root verifier, its rootSig
      (root-rows-missing | root-rows-digest | root-sig-bad).
      #kernel-log (spec §3.2, M1.2; research 29 §4 PR-4) — the one
      hash-chained, append-only, segmented kernel log. node:sqlite; active
      segment <home>/kernel.db, sealed segments <home>/log/segments/NNNNNN.db
      (0600, dirs 0700; read-denied to every session by the session
      profile, T-2026-10-18-010); a session reader (redaction.ts) sees only
      its own hsid rows at every label, and voice.turn.*.audioRef never
      (SESSION_NEVER_FIELDS, limited view); `chain` table per §3.2 plus a write-once `meta`;
      BEFORE UPDATE/DELETE triggers abort (L1). hash = sha256(canonical(envelope
      minus hash, sig)), prev(1) = sha256("gnomon-log-genesis:" + logId), seq
      computed inside the write txn and checked against the in-memory tip.
      Envelope = contracts event:v1, validated strict with default-label check;
      unregistered kinds refused (never guessed), labels tighten-only (L6),
      kernel/userland ownership enforced (L7), daemon.segment reserved to the
      log, session.inject must carry {origin, hash, bytes}; session.compacted
      (T-2026-10-18-016, P8-D93) must pass contracts
      validateSessionCompactedPayload (closed, typed) else bad-payload; replay
      folds its summaryHash as a RECORDED host hash. Signer seam
      (signer.ts): Signer.sign(domain, message) prepends the §3.1 domain
      (gnomon:event-sig:v1 etc.), key classes limited to their domains;
      SoftwareEd25519Signer = the in-memory kernel key (keyId
      gkey:v1:<sha256(spki PEM)>); root/approvals keys are hardware-only and
      plug in after probes P04/P02; KeyRing verifies Ed25519 and P-256.
      Unsigned rows only for daemon/socket/schedule; the §3.2 mandatory list
      refuses unsigned (unsigned-mandatory). Segments rotate at 10⁶ rows with a
      signed daemon.segment closing row and an unbroken chain across files;
      crash mid-rotation is completed on open. Anchors: file <home>/head
      (atomic tmp+rename; fsync only at genesis/rotation/close) and a
      KeystoreAnchorStore SEAM that reports `unavailable` until the helper
      exists (AnchorStore.write may be async; keystore writes are serialised).
      ANCHOR POLICY (anchor-policy.ts; research 33 S33-05..07, P8-D82):
      `append` is async and resolves {seq, hash, anchored, event}. Bulk rows
      coalesce the file anchor (renamed when 63 rows are un-anchored or 50 ms
      after the first, unref'd timer + inline check, and on checkpoint) and
      queue the keystore anchor every 1023 rows / 1 s off the append path.
      CRITICAL_ANCHOR_KINDS (approval.decide/suspect/presence.denied/
      rule.learned/voice.arm/voice.disarm, key.*, grant.*, config.*,
      manifest.*, billing.*, verdict.*, link pair/renew/revoke,
      session.inject{origin:'peer'}, socket.auth.theft) commit with the bulk
      durability (no fullfsync since S33-10 / P8-D83), then rename the file
      anchor, then AWAIT the keystore write (it starts only after COMMIT
      returned), and only then resolve {anchored: true}; no effect may be
      released before that (a failed keystore write rejects
      ENGINE{anchor-failed}). This is an ordering guarantee against a
      same-user process, not a power-loss guarantee. Close writes durable
      anchors (file AND keystore) carrying the clean-close marker
      `closed: true`; open rewrites them without. BOOT SESSION (boot.ts,
      S33-10): every anchor records `boot` = kern.bootsessionuuid (macOS,
      spawned /usr/sbin/sysctl once per process) / boot_id (Linux) /
      `unknown` (Windows until the parity phase); only the keystore copy is
      authoritative. An anchor ahead of the db is INTEGRITY_BROKEN{truncated}
      with data.cause `tamper` (same boot, clean close, or no comparable
      keystore boot — fails closed) or `unclean-reboot` (boot changed, not a
      clean close); a forged file-anchor boot/closed changes nothing.
      The recovery open (an internal seam, see SEAM HARDENING) opens such a
      log refusing every append and writing no anchor; acknowledgeGap
      ({presenceToken}) (a branded PresenceToken, identity/presence.ts;
      unclean-reboot only) appends one kernel-signed daemon.log.gap{lostFrom, lostTo,
      lostHash, bootPrev, bootNow, fileLag} (contracts kind added, reserved to the
      log) and re-anchors durably; the chain verifies across it. ROUND-2
      HARDENING (research 33 S34-xx): every non-recovery open re-stamps the
      keystore anchor with the current boot and the first append waits for it
      (S34-01; cause semantics = "power loss OR tampering"); non-ahead anchors
      get the full check even in recovery, and a file-only lead with a present
      keystore cannot be acknowledged (FORBIDDEN{unauthenticated-lead},
      S34-04); any lag behind a clean-close KEYSTORE anchor is anchor-lag
      (S34-05); acknowledgeGap leaves recovery only after both anchors succeed
      (S34-12) and rotates a daemon.segment tip first (S34-13). ANCHOR FAULTS
      (S34-02): a post-COMMIT failure on a critical row (file anchor, rotation,
      keystore reject or `keystoreTimeoutMs` timeout, default 5 s, S34-11)
      rejects ENGINE{anchor-failed} and faults the log — bulk continues,
      critical appends re-anchor first or refuse ENGINE{anchor-faulted}; on
      recovery one kernel-signed daemon.anchor.failed{seq, kind} per failed
      row is appended (contracts kind added, reserved); projections must treat
      that seq as not having taken effect. `append` resolves anchored false |
      'both' | 'file-only' (stub keystore), also in anchorStatus.anchoring
      (S34-03); live `integrity` getter (ok | truncated | anchor-faulted);
      close() resolves a CloseReport surfacing a failed keystore write. Verify
      (S34-06): daemon.log.gap / daemon.segment / daemon.anchor.lag /
      daemon.anchor.failed / socket.auth.theft are signature-mandatory, and
      kernel-principal rows must carry a kernel key id (signer + kernelKeyIds)
      — failure code `kernel-key`. Boot ids are validated as UUIDs; sysctl is
      spawned with an empty env (S34-09). ROUND 3: the daemon.anchor.failed
      marker is committed IMMEDIATELY in the post-commit failure path (durable
      fault, S34-17); the fault clears only when every failed row has a marker
      AND both anchors cover a head at/after it (S34-19); close() records
      pending markers before its closing anchors and reports `fault`
      (none|recovered|pending) in CloseReport; on open every critical row
      above the keystore anchor without a marker gets one. An interrupted
      acknowledgeGap (tip = kernel-signed daemon.log.gap naming the keystore
      head as lostTo/lostHash) is re-anchored on open and openForRecovery.
      RESERVED_KINDS exported; verify binds reserved-kind rows to the kernel
      principal + key (`reserved-binding`) and VerifyReport.kernelBinding is
      ok|failed|not-checked (standalone verify without kernel identity) —
      S34-18. Keyless logs are non-rotating and diagnostic-only: the boundary
      append and open-time lag/marker rows refuse before commit with
      FORBIDDEN{kernel-key-required} (S34-22). Boot ids are normalised on both
      sides; `unknown` is not cached and emits onDiagnostic `boot.unknown`
      (S34-21). `fileAnchorMaxMs` test seam (S34-23). Keystore timeout runs
      from dequeue; while a timed-out write is unsettled new writes refuse at
      once (order kept, no hang). KNOWN GAPS — DECIDED: (a) P8-D84 —
      accepted dev-only until T-2026-10-17-009: with the stub keystore (until
      M1.3) every truncation classifies `tamper` and cannot be acknowledged —
      a no-recovery window; recovery path = the quarantine command; (b) P8-D85
      — the S33-07 file-anchor lag > 63 bound is not power-loss-safe
      (un-fsynced renames can roll back), so at open, after the truncation
      check, it is relaxed ONLY when the keystore anchor is present, not
      closed, passes the full anchor check, and records a known boot that
      differs from this known boot, and the file anchor's hash still matches
      its row; a relaxed open appends a kernel-signed
      daemon.anchor.lag{rows, which: 'file', bootPrev, bootNow} (keyless ⇒
      FORBIDDEN{kernel-key-required}); every other case (same boot, unknown
      boot, stub/absent/malformed/pre-D83/closed keystore, closed file anchor;
      pre-D83 ⇒ relax boot-unknown) refuses anchor-lag with `data.relax`
      naming why. The file anchor's own boot is never consulted. A relaxed
      lag defers every open-time anchor write (file rewrite,
      marker/keystore-lag re-anchor) until the signed row is committed
      (evidence first); rotation completion runs first but writes no anchor. S34-24: keystore anchor ahead + any file
      lag ⇒ truncated (cause from the keystore boot); the file anchor still
      gets the hash and clean-close checks, only the > 63 bound is skipped,
      and its lag is TruncationReport.fileLag and daemon.log.gap.fileLag
      (additive). S34-30 CHECKS BEFORE WRITES: on open every integrity check
      (file anchor + lag bounds / relax, keystore anchor + clean-close lag,
      keyless refusals for the open-time marker / keystore-lag rows) runs
      before the first anchor write, rotation completion or row append; a
      crashed rotation is completed with NO anchor writes (the file rewrite
      and the closing keystore re-stamp anchor the head), so a refused open
      leaves both anchors byte-for-byte as found and no queued keystore write
      lands after the throw. OPEN-PATH FOLLOW-UPS (T-2026-10-17-012/013/015):
      S34-31 the keystore anchor is read before the create branch — a home
      with no db but a present/malformed keystore anchor refuses missing-db
      with no file written; the durable file-anchor rewrite runs BEFORE a
      crashed rotation is completed (non-relaxed), so a crash after the seal
      rename reopens as a rotation window; S34-25 a relaxed (P8-D85) open
      whose rotation crashed after the seal rename (lagging file anchor, no
      kernel.db) is accepted iff the same relaxation holds against the sealed
      closing row (pure pre-check, keyless refuses kernel-key-required), the
      segment is created and the relaxed row appended there first, the file
      anchor second; acknowledgeGap completes a rotation with no anchor write
      of its own (the closing row never overwrites the ahead keystore
      anchor). ROTATION FAILURE (T-013(a)): any failure after the active db
      is closed (fsync/fsyncDir EIO, seal rename, next kernel.db) halts the
      instance — closed, timers stopped, append / acknowledgeGap refuse
      ENGINE{rotation-failed} (in-process reason; reads refuse `closed`),
      close() reports keystore 'failed' and writes no anchor; reopen
      completes the rotation. Round 2: the halt starts at the closing-row
      commit (durable file anchor, TRUNCATE checkpoint, journal switch), so
      no row ever follows daemon.segment; a critical append rotates only
      after its anchors settled and resolves anchored even if the rotation
      then halts (surfaced on the next call / close). Stated residual: a
      rotation halt inside acknowledgeGap after the seal rename turns the
      acknowledgeable truncation into missing-db (fail-closed; quarantine). S34-29 (documented): a genesis fsyncDir(home)
      failure leaves kernel.db without a head file ⇒ anchor-missing on every
      later open; manual wipe / quarantine, never auto-recreated.
      `forcedAnchorRate` / `anchorStatus` = status.log telemetry (forced
      anchors per row over the last 10⁴ rows; > 1/64 = outside the measured
      envelope). Open refuses INTEGRITY_BROKEN on missing-db (with anchor,
      sealed segment or keys present), truncated (either anchor ahead of the
      db, with the S33-10 cause), anchor-mismatch, anchor-missing, and anchor-lag (file anchor > 63
      rows behind unless relaxed across a boot change per P8-D85, or any lag
      behind a clean-close anchor); a smaller lag is
      accepted only as a chain prefix and repaired; keystore lag > 1023
      appends a kernel-signed daemon.anchor.lag{rows, which}
      (contracts kind added; bootPrev/bootNow additive for which: 'file'). Fsync
      policy: WAL + synchronous=FULL (default 'process-crash'); 'power-loss'
      adds fullfsync per commit, which costs more than the 5 ms falsifier allows
      (the M1.2 figure carries no recorded method or machine, so no number here).
      G-LOG-04 (test:perf, one attempt): bulk p99 < 5 ms / ≤ 0.1 % over 5 ms,
      critical at 1/s under bulk load (60 events in the unit pass) p99 <
      25 ms; the ≥ 600-event 1/s oracle is the manual 10⁶ probe run.
      verify(): full chain (segment links, genesis, anchors) or one segment;
      detects tamper, re-hash forgery, truncation, reorder, stripped or bad
      signatures, unknown keys, relaxed labels. read(): §9.3 projection per
      reader class (human, read-token, community-client, session, mod, peer;
      agent/voice refuse UNSUPPORTED pending M1.7/M4.x). M1.4 SLICE 2 READ
      SIDE: projection uses the EFFECTIVE sensitivity = strictest of the
      registered default, the envelope label and the stored column label (an
      unknown label ranks secret) — labels never widen, and nothing in a
      payload is consulted; readPage({reader, afterSeq, untilSeq?, maxScan,
      kinds?, match?, visit}) re-hashes every envelope (mismatch or
      seq/kind/id column disagreement ⇒ withheld + diagnostic read.withheld),
      returns {lastScanned, scanned, withheld, head, caughtUp}; getById (the
      UNIQUE id index) for ev: refs; onCommit(listener) — tail observers
      called after COMMIT in seq order, exceptions reported as diagnostic
      commit.listener.failed, never reaching the appender; sealed segment
      infos cached (immutable). read() is now built on readPage. ROUND 2:
      releasedHead() = highest seq with no unresolved critical row at or
      below it (a critical row is pending from COMMIT until its anchors
      resolve; failure ⇒ FAILED, withheld forever; failed seqs also loaded
      from daemon.anchor.failed markers); readPage and getById never return
      rows above it; onRelease(listener) wakes tails. readPage projects
      BEFORE hashing for non-human readers, checks prev linkage across
      contiguous rows (prev-mismatch ⇒ withheld) and states the residual (a
      consistent same-user rewrite of a row and every later prev/hash passes
      without signature verification). getById decides visibility before
      the hash (row-integrity only for a row the reader could see).
      Redaction: limited views drop hash/sig/prev, fields → {redacted: true},
      stubs drop the envelope hash; mayBeVisible(e, reader) = metadata-only
      visibility; projectionStats counter. ROUND 3: limited views drop idem
      and refHash too (contracts FIELD_LABELS gain the content-digest audit:
      approval.auto.inputHash, turn.sent.contentHash,
      approval.voice.arm.phrasesHash, approval.voice.intent.handleHash /
      challengeHash, link.msg.out.hash, inject.receipt.shas / source_hash /
      frozenSliceHash); #hashAt uses the sealed-info cache. RESIDUAL until
      M1.3 (header): with the stub keystore, a critical row left unanchored
      by a crash in a previous run is not held out of the released head on
      restart. Not yet: async
      hardware signers, helper anchor, blobs/TTL pruning, projections tables,
      wiring $daemon-lifecycle events into the log.
      Durable-write platform seam (anchor.ts fsyncFile / fsyncDir, the one
      place a path is fsynced; T-2026-10-17-011): on win32 a file is reopened
      'r+' because FlushFileBuffers needs a writable handle (a read-only one is
      EPERM), and the directory fsync is skipped (Windows cannot open a
      directory for flush). STATED RESIDUAL (spec §16, S34-26): on win32 a
      rename is atomic but NOT durable at return — node's renameSync has no
      MOVEFILE_WRITE_THROUGH, so a power cut can undo it; a durable Windows
      rename is parity-phase work (P8-D78). macOS/Linux: the file is reopened
      'r' and fsync(2)ed, the directory fsync(2)ed; an unsupported directory
      fsync (EINVAL/ENOTSUP/EOPNOTSUPP/EISDIR/EBADF, isBenignDirFsyncError) is
      a no-op and any other error (EIO, …) is rethrown (S34-28).
      SEAM HARDENING (T-2026-10-17-009(c), Aegis S34-10, §19.1 release gate):
      `keystoreAnchor` and `bootSession` are no longer KernelLogOptions keys
      (KernelLog.open refuses them at runtime with a TypeError; the private
      constructor demands a module-private construct key held only by open
      and the registered seam closures, so a cast call throws; verifyLog has
      no keystoreAnchor option and always reads the kernel's own store, and
      refuses the key at runtime), and the
      recovery open and acknowledgeGap are no longer public members. The log
      registers them once (class static block) with the internal seam
      channel src/kernel/internal-seams.ts, read only by the internal test
      entry src/kernel/testing.ts (openKernelLog, openKernelLogForRecovery,
      acknowledgeGap, mintTestPresence). test/seams-fence.test.ts proves: no
      src file imports the test entry; only presence.ts, kernel-log.ts,
      manifest/install.ts (M1.5) and testing.ts import the channel; the closure of every tsup entry excludes
      the test entry; no src/cli or $daemon-lifecycle file (nor any other src
      file but the owners) names acknowledgeGap / openForRecovery; dist/
      carries no test-entry marker and its KernelLog / KernelLogOptions types
      expose no seam.
      QUARANTINE (T-2026-10-17-009(a), P8-D89, quarantine.ts + src/cli/log-cli.ts):
      `gnomon log quarantine [--home]` moves the whole home aside intact to the
      sibling <home>.quarantine-<YYYYMMDDTHHMMSSZ>-<hex> (Windows-safe name)
      and installs a new home whose log's genesis (seq 1) is the reserved,
      kernel-signed daemon.log.quarantined{quarantinedLog,
      quarantinedHead{seq, hash, segment}, evidencePath, authorizedBy}
      (KernelLog.createQuarantineSuccessor is the only writer; append refuses
      it; verify flags it anywhere but seq 1 and binds it to the kernel key).
      Identity: EVERY head source (db tip, newest sealed segment, file
      anchor, keystore anchor when one exists) is read — dbs from a private
      scratch COPY (kernel.db + -wal, never -shm), so the evidence is never
      written — and the HIGHEST seq is the quarantined head (a truncated db
      behind its anchor is not hidden); all observed heads go in the journal
      and the CLI readout, with a disagreement WARNING before the prompt.
      Crash-safe: daemon lock (acquireDaemonLock) held across steps 1-4 and
      released through the evidence path; build in <evidence>.new, journal,
      rename home→evidence, rename staging→home (ENOENT there = installed by
      another process). completePendingQuarantine (run by every quarantine,
      by the CLI first and by startDaemon before it creates a home) installs
      an interrupted quarantine ONLY if the unsigned journal checks out:
      staging = evidence + '.new', evidence a quarantine-named sibling, both
      plain dirs owned by this uid (no symlink/junction), the evidence holds
      the journal's quarantinedLog, and the staging log's row 1 is a verified
      daemon.log.quarantined matching newLog / evidencePath / quarantinedLog;
      anything else is abandoned (reported, never deleted). These checks stop
      accidents and planted junk; they are NOT authenticity (the journal is
      unsigned and the staging keys are its own) — that waits for M1.3. The
      staging genesis is checked on rows/chain/signature only, never via
      verifyLog (which reads the per-user keystore anchor; M1.3 tripwire test
      → T-2026-10-18-007), and the keystore anchor is consulted only for the
      default home (spec §22 OPEN 19). Unreadable dbs are reported
      (`unreadable: copy failed: CODE`), ENOSPC refuses no-space; EPERM/EBUSY
      on the install refuses install-busy (retry; the pending install
      finishes). A crash between the step-4 rename and the lock release
      leaves our pidfile in the evidence, reported as lockResidue. An interrupted
      install is reported as outcome completed-pending (CLI: COMPLETED).
      Refuses daemon-running, not-a-home, nothing-to-quarantine,
      quarantine-pending, home-busy (EPERM/EBUSY/EACCES on the move),
      kernel-key-required. The genesis principal comes from kernelPrincipalOf
      (the daemon's root-authority source). CLI output: src/cli/readout.ts
      (#gnomon-kernel; instrument-readout voice — readout rows, `REFUSED  CODE
      reason`, indented `next:`); canConnectSocket shared in
      lifecycle/endpoint.ts. RESIDUALS: authorizedBy 'interactive-confirm' is
      NO PROOF OF PRESENCE — an interactive TTY + typed last 8 chars of the
      log id, passable by any same-user process owning a terminal, until
      presence lands at M1.3; at M1.3 the keystore anchor item must move with
      the evidence; a stale pidfile of a dead daemon is DELETED from the
      evidence by the lock acquisition, its bytes kept in the journal
      (stalePidfile, base64) — the only change to the old home.
      `npm run
      test:perf` = scripts/test-perf.mjs (cmd.exe-safe; --passWithNoTests on
      win32 only, a missing perf file fails loudly elsewhere).
    path: src/kernel/log/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, log, paradigm-8, pr-4, m1.2, p8-d82, p8-d83, p8-d84, p8-d85]
    aspects: ["~ample-internal-logs"]
    tests:
      - test/kernel-log.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-posix.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-gap.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-crossboot.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-open-order.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-rotation-fault.test.ts

      - test/boot-session.test.ts
      - test/durable-fs.test.ts
      - test/seams-fence.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-quarantine.test.ts
      - test/kernel-log-quarantine-names.test.ts
      - test/log-cli.test.ts
      - test/log-cli-quarantine-posix.test.ts
      - test/m112-replay.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LOG-01, G-LOG-02, G-LOG-03, G-LOG-04, G-LOG-06, G-LOG-07, G-LOG-08, G-LOG-09, G-LOG-10, G-LOG-11, G-KRN-01]
    replay: >-
      M1.12 slice 3 (G-KRN-01): replay.ts + cli/replay-cli.ts — `gnomon replay
      [<home>] [--expect <sha256>] [--json]`. The fold runs INSIDE verifyLog's
      single pass (VerifyLogOptions.onRow: the exact bytes verify checked;
      round 2, B1 / Aegis C2), so a row committed after verify's read snapshot
      is never folded, and the projection is discarded unless verify is ok.
      gnomon-replay:v1 = {log, head, kinds, sessions{hsid: inputs
      (session.inject{origin, hash, bytes}, turn.sent{turn, permitId,
      contentHash}), host (recorded *Hash fields of session.spawned /
      session.layers.changed)}, cards (last state)}; projectionHash =
      sha256(canonicalJson(projection)). A turn.sent binds to the session's
      last unconsumed origin:client inject (interleaved kernel/peer/mod injects
      never match) and consumes it; a mismatch or no pending inject is
      input-mismatch; an inject whose bytes is a string is inject-plaintext
      (the append path's checkPayload refuses it; round 2, Aegis C1). Any
      divergence, verify failure or --expect mismatch ⇒ REFUSED
      INTEGRITY_BROKEN (exit 1). Read-only. Trust root: keys from the home's
      keys/kernel-pub.jsonl + kernelPrincipalOf(), so every readout and --json
      print `trust root: home-local` and `keystore: unavailable|anchored` —
      consistency, never provenance (Aegis medium). Rows signed by registry
      keys outside that file fail offline verify (residual).

  gnomon-ts-client:
    journal-cursor: >-
      Offers CLIENT_FEATURES = ['journal.cursor'] by default; subscribe
      takes since as {logId, seq}, cursor() and end.cursor hand the cursor
      back; isCursorEpoch(err) tells the caller to re-read from the start.
      The conformance runner covers the bare-seq and cursor-epoch refusals
      (refusal.bad-request) and resumes subscribe.since by cursor.
    description: >-
      #gnomon-ts-client (spec §4.9, §5, §12.8; §19.1 M1.12 slice 2).
      src/clients/ts/ — the published TypeScript client, built ONLY on
      @a-company/gnomon-contracts + node:* (fenced by test/m112-conformance):
      hello with a fresh 32-byte clientNonce that the result must echo
      (client-nonce-mismatch) and serverSig verified against the PINNED kernel
      key over the SENT nonce before any credential is sent (T-2026-10-18-016); device-key auth (gnomon:auth:v1) or read-token proof
      of possession (gnomon:auth-token:v1); call() typed for the 12 verbs + 2
      relays (ClientMethodParams/Results); refusals reject with RefusedError;
      subscribe() = log.read{follow} with rows in seq order and a visible end;
      logging through an injected ClientLogPort (Paradigm-logger shaped).
      Re-exports the one §4.5 sanitizer for UIs.
    tags: [gnomon, m1.12, client]
    oracles: [G-CLI-02]

  client-conformance:
    description: >-
      #client-conformance (spec §12.8, G-CLI-02; M1.12 slice 2).
      src/clients/conformance/ — driver.ts: the language-neutral JSON-lines
      driver protocol (connect/call/subscribe/events/closed/close/sanitize/
      row/denominator), TsClientDriver (in-process, JSON round-tripped) and
      SubprocessDriver (any language over stdio); runner.ts: runs every
      CONFORMANCE_CHECKS `run` check against a live kernel, `slice-2` checks
      reported todo, report + conformanceReportHash; hello.server-sig-mismatch
      runs an impostor endpoint that REPLAYS a real recorded hello (genuine
      serverSig) verbatim and with clientNonce rewritten to the victim's
      (T-2026-10-18-016): a conformant client refuses both, zero auth frames
      (falsifier: a client that trusts the echoed nonce fails it); the
      impostor listens on a private socket file (POSIX) or a random named
      pipe (Windows) and a listen error fails the check instead of crashing
      the runner. auth.device-refused also requires that the client keeps no
      live connection after the AUTH refusal. subscribe.since checks the
      backlog against a non-follow page (since+1, no gaps); contiguity
      assumes the fixture's full-visibility principal, a restricted principal
      must use the oracle-page comparison only. Falsifiers (T-2026-10-18-015):
      a client that checks the pin's PEM hash and keyId but never serverSig
      fails only hello.server-sig-mismatch, at case (c); a client that keeps a
      connection after the refusal fails auth.device-refused. status.clients[] copies only
      {suite, version, reportHash} (sanitized, capped) and labels a
      declared tuple `declared` and reports clientConformance{n, of} (G-OBS-01).
      M1.12 SLICE 3 honesty CI: test/honesty.ts scanners + test/honesty.test.ts
      (the gate over packages/gnomon*/src, manifests and package docs, plus a
      planted-violation falsifier per rule): G-HON-02 fabrication grep
      (Math.random outside ids, simulated/simulation, sleep()/delay(),
      hardcoded gains, mechanism words the code lacks), G-HON-05 no
      Task-steering descriptions, G-HON-06 unscoped guard names refused,
      G-HON-07 (round 2, B2): every ms/µs number in package docs — prose,
      wrapped paragraphs and Markdown table cells, keyword or not — names a
      concrete machine (chip, versioned OS or arch) and a named method in its
      sentence or table row, unless it is a budget, bound or configured
      interval (the BUDGET EXEMPTION, pending founder ratification); the
      live status of a spawned kernel (device + read-token views) passes
      checkStatusNumbers (#socket) in test/m112-conformance.test.ts.
    tags: [gnomon, m1.12, client, conformance, honesty-ci]
    tests:
      - test/m112-conformance.test.ts
      - test/honesty.test.ts
    oracles: [G-CLI-02, G-OBS-01, G-HON-02, G-HON-03, G-HON-05, G-HON-06, G-HON-07]

  standing-grant:
    description: >-
      TD-2026-10-20-086 F6 (spec §3.4 "Standing grant"). The grant card is
      raised with RaiseInput.standingGrant: its allow is FRESH class (one
      presence, no root signature; Aegis open point 1); other grant cards stay root.
      src/kernel/broker/standing-grant.ts. ONE root-class `grant` card
      (request.grant.standing{tuples, repos} from the human on the GUI only,
      detail.op 'standing') mints grant.standing{grantId (= the cardId),
      cardId, tuples, repos, device (the GUI device's udev: unlock key),
      expiresAt ≤ 12 h, by}; the same touch keeps the device's cached unlock
      so later uses can be signed by the helper. While it stands, a human
      GUI decision on the same device that a tuple covers
      (STANDING_TUPLES: mode.loosen:auto-edits, permission:always,
      voice.widen, voice-arm:allow, daemon-stop:restart with
      detail.binaryUnchanged and no update; NOT reconsider:extend, Aegis M2; repo-scoped
      ones only with a non-null granted repo) takes no prompt: approval.decide
      {presence:'standing:<grantId>'} then the notify row
      grant.standing.used{grantId, cardId, tuple, decision, repo, hsid}.
      Never standing: root class, hand-only, install, gate-class,
      fallback-cloud, declassify, peer-pair, api-token, grant, handoff,
      loop-trust (8.0:loops), peer-flagged or irreversible cards (standingTupleOf
      + broker checks).
      Ends: expiresAt, Revoke (request.grant.standing.revoke{grantId} →
      grant.standing.revoked{grantId, by, reason:'human'}, a tighten, no
      presence), the next screen lock / sleep / user switch or a helper exit
      (broker.helperSessionEnded/helperExited → endAll). StandingGrantBook
      folds kernel-signed rows only (forged rows ignored), like RuleBook /
      RunModeBook; status.standing{built, tuples, active[], pendingCardId}.
      No new signal (events are the §9.1 grant.standing* kinds).
    path: src/kernel/broker/standing-grant.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, broker, approvals, presence, td-2026-10-20-086]
    tests:
      - test/standing-grant.test.ts
  approval-broker:
    description: >-
      8.0:loops (gnomon-loops.md §3.1, §3.3): classes.ts rows loop-trust
      {allow root, deny fresh} (never always, never standing, hand-only) and
      loop-choice {continue fresh hand-only, stop cached}; log-append.ts adds the
      human request.loop.* kinds to the first-party request gate. Test:
      test/loop-classes.test.ts.
      Aegis M1: a root-capable card's detail carries rootDecisions (hashed).
      TD-2026-10-20-514 follow-up: a client decisionSig takes the root-only
      decision.sign form (Aegis F6, 1 prompt); a root-capable card's block
      carries {type:'root-rows', rootDecisions, detail} for the GUI (C1e);
      helperBatch()/refreshHelperBatch() = status.helper.batch (Q2);
      RaiseInput.rootDecisions raises voice.enrol allow to root (Q3).
      TD-2026-10-20-514: every root-class card carries detail.rootRows; #approve
      prepares the rows (RaiseInput.prepare), builds the gnomon-root-decision:v2
      row (rowsDigest) and makes ONE fresh prompt with signDecisionBatch on a
      helper >= 1.8.0, else the legacy one-signature-per-row path over the same
      prepare/commit; the effect gets rowSigs; prepare released in a finally.
      STANDING GRANT (TD-2026-10-20-086 F6): StandingSeam (setStanding) is
      asked before any presence; a match is signed under the device unlock
      (or a verified client decisionSig) with presence 'standing:<id>' and
      #standing-grant writes grant.standing.used after approval.decide.
      RUN MODES: approval.auto modeRef is mode:auto-edits (file edits) or
      mode:auto-edits:read-only / mode:ask:read-only (a Bash command
      #shell-readonly proves read-only; TD-2026-10-20-294 part 1, F1
      TD-2026-10-20-086; path = the anchored root's realpath, inputHash the
      request's) or rule:<cardId>. Not built (would move clientApiId, founder
      gate): approval.auto.miss and an anchoredInputHash field.
      PREFIX SCOPE (TD-2026-10-20-294 part 3): approve{decision:'always',
      scope} — {kind:'exact'} is the v1 exact rule; {kind:'prefix', prefix}
      (1-3 words) is accepted only on a Bash permission card whose SHOWN
      command parses via simpleArgv as one literal simple command whose argv
      starts with exactly those words (else BAD_REQUEST); decisionSig is the v2
      layout whose last line is scopeDigest(scope) (signer decisionMessage
      {scopeDigest}), the presence binding carries scopeDigest, the prompt
      names the prefix, approval.decide records scope and
      approval.rule.learned records {match:'prefix', prefix}. replay.ts
      verifies a scoped always ONLY over the scope layout. A Bash card whose
      command is one literal simple command carries detail.prefixOptions (first
      1-2 argv words) + a {type:'bash-prefix', detail} block node the GUI
      verifies against contentHash.
      #approval-broker (spec §3.4, §5 verb 8, §3.1 approvals key; §19.1 M1.6).
      Voice (TD-2026-10-19-194): whether voice may take a decision is
      gnomon-contracts' VOICE_ALLOWLIST (#contract-voice-allowlist, §14.7), not
      classes.ts; test/voice-golden.test.ts pins abiId and checks the list
      against KIND_DECISIONS.
      src/kernel/broker/. Helper 1.6.0 (V5, TD-2026-10-19-194, §14.7, S29-K5):
      arm-custody.ts VoiceArmCustody keeps voice-arm REFERENCES only (the
      unlock is in the helper per armId) and appends approval.voice.disarm
      {armId, reason, cause?} for every arm on a helper session push (reason
      per cause from contracts VOICE_DISARM_REASONS: screen-lock (also
      screensaver), display-sleep, system-sleep, user-switch), helper exit
      (helper-exit) or a pair connection closing (conn-closed). A push also drops every cached-unlock
      reference (helperSessionEnded); connectionClosed sends conn.close.
      HelperApprovalsKey gains openArm, signUnderArm, closeArm,
      closeAllArms, connectionClosed and onHelperPush; HelperProcess parses
      the v2 push (a malformed one kills the helper); MIN_HELPER_VERSION
      1.6.0, HELPER_PROTOCOL gnomon-helper:v2 (test/voice-arm-custody.test.ts,
      test/identity-helper-pin.test.ts). T-2026-10-18-028: M2 — UnlockStore ends a cached
      unlock at UNLOCK_IDLE_MS idle, UNLOCK_ABSOLUTE_MS (60 min) after it
      opened, or after UNLOCK_MAX_SIGNATURES (64) uses (approval.unlock.
      dropped{reason: idle|expired|exhausted}); M3 — unlock.drop goes to the
      helper on every close and failure path (connectionClosed always, a
      lost keep-sign reply, a close during the keep-sign, a failed cached
      sign, an ended unlock), and HelperApprovalsKey.dropUnlock escalates a
      drop the helper does not answer to unlock.drop-all, then recycles the
      helper process (HelperTransport.running / recycle); helper floor
      MIN_HELPER_VERSION 1.3.0 (test/unlock-bounds.test.ts).
      M4 — the presence prompt (drawn by the OS, not a conformant client)
      gets the card line through promptSafe (every Cf, Cc, Zl, Zp removed)
      and the summary QUOTED with quote/backslash escaped (quoteSummary,
      card.ts); the helper strips the same set from the caller text
      (Protocol.swift promptSafe).
      M5 (#session-runner, platform/darwin/session-sbpl.ts, profile v4): every
      session profile ends with helperDenySbpl() — deny process-exec of
      /GnomonHelper.app(/|$) and /gnomon-helper$, deny file-read* of the
      bundle (no copy out), regexes on resolved paths; backstop: the helper
      refuses serve/once under any sandbox (test/session-helper-deny.test.ts).
      L2 — #freshPrompt: at most ONE outstanding fresh presence prompt per
      connection (FORBIDDEN{presence, why: prompt-outstanding}), and
      PROMPT_COOLDOWN_MS (3 s) after a prompt the human declined
      (why: prompt-cooldown, retryAfterMs); an accepted prompt or an
      unavailable helper starts no cooldown; PromptRateLimited passes the
      presence error mapping unchanged (test/prompt-rate.test.ts). P8-D96: RaiseInput.decisions limits a card's signed
      actions (permission slice cards: allow|deny, no always); approve
      refuses any other decision bad-params; expire(cardId, why) logs
      approval.expire{into:'deny', why}; noteOf; suspect (approval.suspect
      from kernel observation). Aegis A3: approve claims the decision
      synchronously after its last re-check (CardRecord.claim); expire()
      refuses a claimed card and defers while #deciding (applied in
      approve's finally if the decision did not settle). classes.ts (P8-D98: the reserved daemon-stop kind
      is accept|reject, both fresh presence — Touch ID, TD-2026-10-19-528 F3) — valid decisions per card kind and the
      BASE presence class of each (cached: allow/deny on permission, answer,
      approve-plan, reject on plan/reconsider/inbound handoff, deny on
      strictness, incident; fresh: everything else the spec names, incl.
      always; root: install, use-api-token, peer-pair, grant; none: withdraw);
      irreversible and peer flags raise a cached decision to fresh; a decision
      the spec does not class is fresh (fail closed). card.ts — the card is
      composed from the broker's own record: lines sanitized (hidden-chars /
      bidi → visible escapes + card flags, §4.5), contentHash over {kind,
      requestedBy, hsid, detail, flags}, actions[] kernel-signed per valid
      decision (domain gnomon:card-action:v1), and the presence prompt composed
      from the card record (kind, requester, contentHash summary, connId, open
      GUI sessions {n}, the class reason) and signed by the kernel key (domain
      gnomon:presence-prompt:v1); verifyPresencePrompt checks text = the
      record's composition. Line cap MAX_LINE (512 code points, exported for
      #manifest-loader's install-card wrapping); the presence summary takes
      the first line's first 120 code points, never splitting a surrogate
      pair. install-card.ts passes the install card's pre-sanitized lines
      together with their sanitizer flags. unlock.ts — the cached unlock
      (TD-2026-10-20-086 F4/F5, superseding S25-H1 and the M2 60 min / 64):
      created only by a fresh presence on a GUI connection, never for a CLI
      connection; KEYED BY THE GUI DEVICE (broker unlockKeyOf: `udev:` + 40
      hex of sha256 over the launch-token deviceId; a gui context without a
      deviceId keeps its connId) so it survives a reconnect and is shared by
      the device's GUI sessions; ends at screen lock / sleep / user switch
      (helper push), helper exit, 12 h (UNLOCK_ABSOLUTE_MS = UNLOCK_IDLE_MS)
      or 512 signatures, each bound LOWERED to the helper's: helper 1.7.0
      reports `bounds` on a kept sign (helperBoundsOf), 1.6.0 reports none and
      is held to LEGACY_HELPER_BOUNDS (15 min idle, 60 min, 64). Dropped rows
      name the last connection and scope:'device'
      (test/unlock-device.test.ts). broker.ts — ApprovalBroker: raise (approval.request before
      the card exists; permission cards time out into deny, fallback and
      api-token never expire), approve (human only, B4; voice FORBIDDEN gate
      voice `voice-off` for every form, G-ABI-02 voice row; via link refused
      `peer-via`; held ⇒ withdraw only; peer-flagged always refused
      `peer-always`; decisionSig by the approvals key over approveMessage —
      bad ⇒ AUTH bad-signature, no approvals key ⇒ UNSUPPORTED presence
      M1.3; presence per class with a PresenceToken BOUND to {cardId,
      contentHash, decision} on the kernel-trusted clock (T-2026-10-18-003);
      always under a cached unlock ⇒ FORBIDDEN presence when no fresh presence
      comes; root class signs the decision with the root key; approval.decide
      {decisionSig, presence, via, connId, cli provenance} BEFORE the effect;
      always ⇒ approval.rule.learned; approval.suspect{why, provenance} for
      unknown CLI provenance / no TTY / CLI pgid inside a live session /
      unlock beyond class), expireDue, decisionOf (silence and unknown cards
      ⇒ deny, B6), connectionClosed, status. Production presence is
      PRESENCE_UNAVAILABLE, the approvals key NO_APPROVALS_KEY and the root
      unprovisioned until M1.3: every presence-taking decision refuses.
      install-card.ts — the M1.5 install-card decision moved under the broker
      (T-2026-10-18-008): the card is rendered from the detail RE-DERIVED from
      the pinned snapshot (manifest/install.ts rederiveInstallDetail) and kept
      in kernel memory; presence is a mod.install token bound to {cardId,
      contentHash} handed unburned to decideInstall. api-token.ts — the per-use
      billing api-token card (human-only request, root+fresh use-api-token,
      never expires), keystore injection into exactly one child env
      (takeEnvFor, single use; KEYSTORE_UNAVAILABLE until M1.3),
      billing.api.card.requested / active (activation and every turn) /
      ended / refused and the status indicator API CREDITS ACTIVE. port.ts —
      the #socket port: approve, permit.reserve (+ release), connection close,
      status cards / budget / billing, guiSessions[].unlock. The spec declares
      no signals; events are §9.1 approval.* / billing.* kinds. Round 2
      (Aegis C1): GUI vs CLI is decided by the authenticated credential (only
      a launch-token GUI session may hold an unlock), never by the hello
      surface; expiry skips a card being decided and the decision re-checks
      the card and the connection after its last await (one terminal row);
      the sanitizer strips tag characters and escapes C0/C1 controls; the
      install card is rendered by the broker from re-derived lines, readPaths
      and linked source validated, presence bound to the decision; effect
      failures are recorded as exactly one approval.effect.failed{cardId,
      decision, effect: the card kind, reason: a kebab-case code} and no
      approval.suspect row; the card stays pending (approval.decide is a
      record, never authority; P8-D98 slice A2), so replay folds decide ->
      approval.effect.failed -> decide for one card with no divergence (the
      last decide stands). raise refuses a card carrying both a ruleScope and
      an effect BAD_REQUEST (Aegis M3: a learned rule auto-allows without
      running an effect). decisionSig v2 (P8-D98
      slice A2): an `answer` signs the v2 message (signer decisionMessage,
      first line gnomon:decision:v2, last line answerDigest = the noteHash)
      and its presence binding carries answerDigest. With a client-supplied
      decisionSig an answer rewritten on the wire after signing fails AUTH
      bad-signature; on the helper-signs path the kernel computes
      answerDigest from the note it received, so v2 binds the text the
      kernel received, not the text the human saw (Aegis M2); an answer with no note is
      refused BAD_REQUEST; every other decision stays v1. Replay verifies
      both layouts given decisionKeys (replay.ts checkDecisionSigs) with a
      per-log cutover MARKER ROW: the first v2 answer or any 8.0:P8-D98 kind;
      a v1 answer after it is decision-sig-v1-after-cutover. The helper
      prompt line (gnomon-helper Protocol.swift helperLine) parses the v2
      marker so it still shows the decision, card and content hash, plus the
      answer digest. Slice 2: with no
      approvals key (before M1.3) approve answers UNSUPPORTED{presence}
      before the decisionSig shape check, so the GUI (no signing path yet)
      sees the presence refusal (T-2026-10-18-018 item 2); status.cards per
      F4 — the human gets pendingForYou + byKind, every other class counts
      only {n, pendingForYou: 0, held}; theft-card.ts — a logged
      socket.auth.theft (#socket dispatch → port.authTheft) raises ONE
      fresh-presence gate-class card per revoked connId naming it, no
      timeout, no effect (the revocation stands either way). Round 2
      (Aegis C2): the card's promise is stored before the raise awaits
      (concurrent theft rows for one connId raise one card), at most
      MAX_OPEN_THEFT_CARDS (8) theft cards are open — past that a theft
      coalesces into the newest open card — a failed raise is forgotten so
      the next theft row retries, and #socket raises only after the
      socket.auth.theft row was written. The path #web-endpoint (revokes,
      reports theft) → #socket (writes the row) → #approval-broker (raises
      the card) is a one-row relay with no state of its own, documented
      here and in theft-card.ts rather than as a $flow node (a new spec
      node moves Appendix A's count; see lore).
      M1.3 slice 1: with a helper-held approvals key (#identity
      HelperApprovalsKey) an `approve` WITHOUT decisionSig has the helper
      make it: a cached-class decision on a GUI connection with a live
      unlock signs `cached` (no prompt; a helper that lost the unlock ⇒
      approval.unlock.dropped{helper-lost} and a fresh touch); otherwise one
      fresh touch signs the decision and the bound presence challenge,
      keeping the unlock in the helper for cached-class GUI decisions only.
      Unlock drops (idle, closed) propagate to the helper; a helper exit
      drops every unlock (approval.unlock.dropped{helper-exit}). A helper
      that is down or returns a signature that does not verify ⇒
      UNSUPPORTED / FORBIDDEN presence, card stays pending. Root-class
      decisions use the root's signRowAsync. A client-supplied decisionSig
      is still verified as before. No new wire reason or event kind.
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 5 (Orca audit Adopt 5): deny-safe settlement
      pinned by falsifiers, no code change — an undecodable request is
      denied (permission-malformed ⇒ deny line; bad approve params ⇒
      BAD_REQUEST, card stays pending; answerFor: only a decoded allow
      allows); a cancel (control_cancel_request ⇒ expire 'cancelled')
      settles into deny once and answers the session nothing; a late answer
      on a non-pending card is refused before presence with no
      approval.decide row; an unknown card is NOT_FOUND. Tests:
      test/broker-deny-safe.test.ts (every platform).
      F3 (TD-2026-10-20-086): RunModeControl configDefault = the
      root-signed config `runModes.default` (budget/config.ts
      parseRunModesSection; any fallback ⇒ null): a repo with no mode of its
      own takes it (view source 'config') when its folder is known and not
      home / above home / `/` (else ask; a repo key with no live session ⇒
      ask, fail safe); the ceiling still applies; status.runModes
      .configDefault (test/run-mode-default.test.ts).
      RUN MODES (P8-D86/D87, src/kernel/broker/run-mode.ts + supervisor/
      auto-edits.ts): manual | ask (default) | auto-edits, per repo and per
      session, folded from KERNEL-signed approval.mode.set and
      session.open{runMode} rows (RunModeBook, catchUp over modeSourceOf; a
      repo's mode persists across sessions and restarts). A session takes its
      repo's mode unless set for it. Effective = stricter(chosen, ceiling);
      ceiling L0/L1 auto-edits, L2 ask, L3 manual; strictness levels are NOT
      built (no level source ⇒ ceiling auto-edits; status.runModes.strictness
      .built false). RunModeControl.request serves log.append request.mode.set
      from a human on a gui or device credential only (dispatch.ts
      `requests`): BAD_REQUEST on an unknown mode/keys, NOT_FOUND on a dead
      hsid, FORBIDDEN{gate strictness, strictness-mode} over the ceiling;
      writes request.mode.set, then tightening ⇒ approval.mode.set{scope,
      from, to, by, presence:'none'} at once; loosening ⇒ a strictness card
      (allow fresh/hand-only, deny cached; decisions allow|deny so never
      always; 2 min ⇒ deny) whose effect re-checks the ceiling and writes
      approval.mode.set{presence:'fresh'}; one pending loosening card per
      scope. session.open{runMode}: no looser than the repo ⇒ set by the open
      row; looser ⇒ opened at the repo's mode + a loosening card
      (result.runModeCard). Supervisor: manual denies Bash/PowerShell/Edit/
      Write/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit, WebFetch and mcp__* (manualHolds) with no
      card until a plan card in that session is approved (reset whenever
      manual begins; RunModeControl onChange → supervisor.noteModeChange
      records every applied change on each live session at once, Aegis L2);
      auto-edits passes Edit/Write/MultiEdit/NotebookEdit whose path is
      absolute with no `..` (M4), under a root that is not the user's home,
      above it or / (M3, isHomeOrAbove; also refused at admitOpen and
      request.mode.set), with NO symlink component below the root (every
      component lstat'd, the final one too; H1), not gate-class (layers.ts
      gateClassPathRule, incl. editor/toolchain dirs, lint/format configs,
      task/build files, other agents' steering files, non-ASCII and win32
      alias names; H2; TD-2026-10-20-294 re-check C2: .gitconfig,
      .git-credentials, .netrc, curl/pip/conda config, interpreter and
      toolchain pins, build entry points, shell rc files, .env/.env.*) and whose card has no hidden/bidi flags, after
      broker.auto ⇒ approval.auto{hsid, tool, path (real), inputHash,
      modeRef:'mode:auto-edits'} (a failed row falls through to the card);
      everything else cards as in ask. Tightening a REPO pulls every live
      session whose own mode is looser down to it with its own
      approval.mode.set and withdraws a pending loosening card for it (M1).
      permission.ts deniedWrite also tests policy.ts realpathFollow (where
      a write lands, dangling links followed) and denies an unresolvable
      path (H1).
    path: src/kernel/broker/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, broker, approvals, presence, paradigm-8, m1.6]
    tests:
      - test/approval-broker.test.ts
      - test/run-modes.test.ts
      - test/broker-deny-safe.test.ts
      - test/socket-abi.test.ts
      - test/manifest-loader.test.ts
      - test/identity-hardware.test.ts
    oracles: [G-BRK-01, G-BRK-02, G-BRK-03, G-BRK-05, G-ABI-02, G-SUB-02, G-SUB-03]
  gnomon-voice:
    description: >-
      Dictate, the G-VOX-06 exception (founder bug 2026-10-21): under a
      conversation ceiling with send ceiling key, an unverified speaker's
      ambient, non-intercepted turn of the attached human's voice principal is
      not refused voice-speaker at receipt; the other guards run and an
      accepted one is stamped forwardable, draftOnly:true and
      speakerVerified:false (client claims of either are BAD_REQUEST). Aegis
      privacy: its row is HASH-ONLY; its words stay in kernel memory
      (UNVERIFIED_TEXT_TTL_MS, UNVERIFIED_TEXT_CAP) and reach only a human on
      the gui credential, for sessions that human owns now:
      VoiceControl.unverifiedFor(principal), added per viewer by the
      dispatcher as status.voice.unverified (status() never carries them;
      voiceStatusFor strips them for any other class or credential). It fills the draft only: the direct send is refused, and a
      word, piece or send-now claim on that session is refused
      voice-speaker{cause draft-only} while ANY attachment's unverified turn
      there is unsent (a preset switch re-attaches); it is never the send
      word nor an adjacency anchor; only a human session.send (Enter) carries
      it, and originFor stamps that send sentBy 'unverified' (turn.sent
      origin voice-unverified, clamped by ^voice-origin-clamp) when its
      attribution matched one or a source:'voice' send happens while one is
      outstanding (consumed; releaseOrigin hands them back on a failed send). A refused unverified turn under
      VOICE_MIN_VERIFY_SPEECH_MS (contracts) of gated speech carries cause
      too-short.
      Voice drops fix (2026-10-20): busy (voice-playback) ends at the main-chain
      end_turn message_stop (supervisor turnBusy), re-armed by message_start;
      the append result carries refused/refusedCause; capture reports carry
      refusal/cause; voice.turn.user keeps speakerScore (bucket) and
      gatedSpeechMs; the kernel stamps localDrops counts (VoiceReports
      takeLocalDrops) on voice.turn.user and voice.session.end.
      Aegis M2: every enrolments.jsonl write (CLI enrol/forget, card enrol) goes
      through appendSignedEnrolRow (sig verified, seq recomputed under the
      lock, moved seq refused loudly, durable append). A missing `consent` is
      never "unconsented"; the text lives in voice/consents/<hex>.txt.
      TD-2026-10-20-514 (Aegis Q3/F3): the GUI enrol card is ROOT class and
      hand-only; prepareEnrol builds the voiceEnrolRow:v1 row (+cardId,
      consentHash; seq under the enrolments lock), commitEnrol re-checks seq
      and writes the consent text to voice/consents/<hex>.txt; the consent
      never enters the card or the log. ONE presence.
      Dictation (TD-2026-10-20-262): accepted voice.turn.user rows carry a
      kernel-stamped forwardable (a client forwardable is BAD_REQUEST); a human
      session.send{source:'voice'} is hash-matched against the attachment's
      pending turns and tagged turn.sent{source voice|voice-edited, voiceSessionId,
      voiceTurnSeqs, voiceTurnSeq} (attribution never refuses); request.voice.report
      {dictation} is relayed as status.voice.dictation{activity, holdToggles, at}.
      #gnomon-voice kernel side, slices V1 talk-only and V2a ambient (TD-2026-10-19-298:
      the attachment mode is a ceiling, ptt under any, conversation only under
      conversation; widening to conversation takes one fresh-presence strictness
      card per GUI login; ambient turns need an aec claim, an enrolled speaker and
      a not-busy session by the supervisor's own turn state plus the echo tail
      (voice-playback, clock kernel); idle auto-off of a conversation ceiling after
      voice.ambientIdleMs, default ten minutes, written voice.session.end reason idle;
      receipt-refused and unknown-speaker turns are stored as a keyed text hash,
      duration and reason, never text; status.voice attached, hsid, ceiling, since,
      idleAt) (spec §14.1-14.4, §14.7
      Forwarding guards and No level, run modes; §4.10; T-2026-10-19-026,
      TD-2026-10-19-194, TD-2026-10-19-677). src/kernel/voice/voice-control.ts:
      attachment ONLY by a human gesture (log.append voice.session.start|end on a
      first-party GUI/device connection; the kernel mints voiceSessionId and
      writes the row); voice session.send to the attached session only, source
      voice forced, riding the attaching human's turn permit (never its own),
      accepted only for the voice connection's latest voice.turn.user in this
      attachment from an enrolled speaker, not intercepted, push-to-talk, outside
      every voice.turn.agent playback window + echoTailMs (750). Refusals are
      FORBIDDEN gate voice with reserved reasons: voice-target, voice-conn,
      voice-stale-utterance, voice-speaker, voice-grammar, voice-echo, voice-off
      (conversation mode). session.stop interrupt only; voice.* shape-validated;
      the voice reader sees its attached session + voice.*, redacted. approve stays
      voice-off in every form; request.voice.arm stays refused (V7).
      src/kernel/voice/enrolment.ts + src/cli/voice-cli.ts: gnomon voice setup |
      enrol | forget, root-signed rows (enrolments.jsonl, voice device key).
      Aegis V1 conditions: kernel receipt time stamped on voice.turn.user|agent
      (receivedAt); a turn is forwardable for VOICE_TURN_MAX_AGE_MS (30 s) after
      receipt, never before the attachment, echo checked on kernel times too;
      per-turn client mode claim cross-checked against the attachment (ptt is a
      client claim, §A.4); voice follow subscriptions end on every attachment
      change (SubscriptionHub.endReaderClass); turn.sent / session.inject carry
      source voice + voiceSessionId, turnId, voiceTurnSeq (the link); the voice
      reader sees only its attached session's rows; enrolment order is the
      signed seq; windows bounded at VOICE_WINDOW_CAP.
      Slice V2e (spec §14.4.1): src/kernel/voice/voice-child.ts VoiceChildSupervisor
      spawns the voice client over an inherited socketpair (fd 3) the kernel
      authenticates itself as p:<ns>/voice (SocketServerHandle.adopt +
      dispatcher.adoptPreauthenticated, no hello/auth, no key in the child),
      allow-list env, backoff restarts, dies on every daemon exit and drain
      (TD-908). voice-install.ts copies + codesign-verifies GnomonVoiceIO.app
      into <home>/bin/voice and fetches models; T-2026-10-21-028
      (#release-pins): the copied executable is hashed before and after
      codesign (VOICE_IO_REQUIREMENT from gnomon-contracts, the build's own),
      must equal the release's compiled sha256 for this arch in a release
      build (VoiceAppRefused 'checksum', dev flag or not) and answer
      --version ≥ MIN_VOICE_IO_VERSION in every build ('version');
      createVoicePrepare re-verifies it LAST, right before the spawn
      (recheckVoiceApp: path, inode, size, mtime, sha256, the release pin,
      codesign hash-verify-rehash), so the model-fetch window cannot swap it; the client entry resolves
      lazily, darwin only. Aegis M2: at DAEMON START createVoicePrepare checks
      the workspace gnomon-voice dist against dist/voice-client.manifest.json
      and that manifest against the loaded build stamp's voiceClientManifest
      (scripts/build-stamp.mjs; build gnomon-voice first), then copies all of
      dist, voice-models.manifest.json, a module package.json and the optional
      sherpa-onnx-node + platform packages into <home>/bin/voice/client
      (0700/0600), pins a
      sha256 of every file in memory, runs the model fetch and the spawn from
      the COPY, and re-verifies the pin last in every prepare; a changed,
      added or missing file is VoiceClientRefused, phase 'unavailable' with
      the reason, never retried. Every exit (signal, stop, idle, upgrade after
      its drain) goes through shutdown(), which awaits voiceChild.close(). voice-requests.ts: dispatcher-only request.voice.enrol
      (fresh-presence strictness card, detail.op voice.enrol, writes the
      root-signed row), request.voice.forget (no card), request.voice.report
      (client progress + notices relayed in status.voice; 2026-10-20
      prints{stale: names} = speakers whose print predates the client's
      speech gate, held per voice connection with no TTL, cleared when it
      closes, relayed as status.voice.prints; T-2026-10-20-005
      A1 capture{state idle|hearing|transcribing|captured|dropped, segId,
      reason? closed too-short|echo|not-attached|no-words|not-enrolled|
      barge-in|refused on dropped only, level? 0..1 on hearing only} relayed
      as status.voice.capture with VOICE_CAPTURE_TTL_MS 5 s; voiceStatusFor
      projects B1's capture.text{committed, tail} (the live partial words,
      hearing / transcribing only, voice principal only, sanitised, capped
      at CAPTURE_TEXT_MAX 600, in memory with the capture TTL, never a row)
      to a human-class connection on the gui credential only (V2; the shared
      CAPTURE_STATUS_KEYS never include text); voice.capture.level declared in status-numbers.ts. Aegis
      follow-ups: terminal states are marked claim:'client' and cross-checked
      against the kernel's own acceptances on that voice connection
      (VoiceControl onAccepted turn|send, 5 s window): a contradicted drop
      shows captured with kernel:'accepted' and clientReason, a captured with
      no acceptance is kernel:'unconfirmed'; text only for cls human on the
      gui credential; the slot remembers its connId and clears when that
      connection closes); request.voice.client
      start|stop is human only. speakable.ts KernelSpeakable appends
      voice.turn.agent{source kernel-speakable, turnSeq, items} for the attached
      session; the voice reader is narrowed to voice.* rows. speakerEnrolled is
      the kernel's call (readEnrolled). No §9.1 kind added; clientApiId unchanged.
      TABLE TALK (TD-2026-10-20-294, docs/guides/table-talk.md): the reply item
      prefers the first speak fenced block (markdown, URLs and long digit/hex runs
      stripped, 300-char sentence cap, unclosed fence = its first paragraph)
      as from:'block', else the gnome line / first paragraph as from:'heuristic';
      kernel-written ask-waiting and edits items carry from:'kernel'.
      table-talk.ts holds the frozen guidance (at most 160 words, pinned hash);
      VoiceControl.guidanceFor(hsid) returns it only while voice is attached
      to that session, and the #supervisor writes it as a second
      <gnomon-voice> text block on every send (turn.sent guidanceHash;
      contentHash stays the human text, replay unchanged). The spoken scrub
      (scrubSpoken) also covers heuristic and ask items (Aegis L2); gnomon
      trace allows one extra prompt block only when turn.sent names its
      guidanceHash and the hash matches (Aegis M2).
    path: src/kernel/voice/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, voice, paradigm-8, move-4]
    flows: [$voice-turn]
    tests:
      - test/voice-v1.test.ts
      - test/voice-v2e.test.ts
      - test/voice-v2e-daemon.test.ts
      - test/voice-v2e-enrol.test.ts
      - test/voice-v2e-speak.test.ts
      - test/table-talk.test.ts
      - test/table-talk-session.test.ts
      - test/voice-enrolment.test.ts
      - test/voice-golden.test.ts
      - test/socket-abi.test.ts
      - test/socket-log-read.test.ts
    oracles: [G-VOX-04, G-VOX-06]

  desk-guidance:
    description: >-
      #desk-guidance (TD-2026-10-20-812 A1, slice S1; spec §12.9).
      src/kernel/desk/desk-guidance.ts: the frozen DESK_BODY (pinned
      DESK_BODY_HASH) and a kernel-composed roster from manifest fields only
      (mod name, offer kind, phase, title; A7), wrapped in <gnomon-desk>.
      composeGuidance(voice, desk) returns ONE block (voice first, blank line,
      desk) so table talk and the Desk share the single extra text block under
      one turn.sent{guidanceHash} that trace-cli extrasAgree admits (Aegis M2);
      voice alone is exactly TABLE_TALK_GUIDANCE. The supervisor's
      setTurnGuidance takes the composite (setVoiceGuidance is its alias).
    path: src/kernel/desk/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, desk, mod-playbooks, paradigm-8]
    flows: [$offer-handoff]
    tests:
      - test/desk-guidance.test.ts
      - test/table-talk-session.test.ts
    oracles: [G-DSK-01]

  session-names:
    description: >-
      #session-names (8.0:session-rename, spec §3.3 "Session names").
      src/kernel/supervisor/session-names.ts: a fold over the kernel-signed
      session.open{hsid, by} (the owner) and session.renamed{hsid, name}
      rows, seeded from log.read at boot (SESSION_NAME_SEED_KINDS) and fed
      by log.onCommit, so a name survives a restart and a replay with no
      store of its own (bounded, SESSION_NAMES_CAP). rename() serves the
      human's log.append{kind:'request.session.rename', payload:{hsid, name}}
      routed by the dispatcher from a gui or device human connection only
      (FORBIDDEN{matrix} otherwise); closed {hsid, name}; the name goes
      through contracts normalizeSessionName (the ONE section 4.5 sanitizer,
      whitespace folded, trimmed, <= 80 code points; a control or format
      character is BAD_REQUEST, never dropped); '' resets to the derived
      title. The session must be one this human opened (NOT_FOUND
      otherwise, live or ended). Writes request.session.rename then
      session.renamed (checked at append by #kernel-log). status.sessionNames
      lists the asker's names (human class only). A name is display text,
      never a capability: nothing routes, grants or permits by it, and it
      never reaches a path. The raw CLI form is `gnomon session rename`
      (src/cli/session-cli.ts runSessionRename, the gnomon mode device
      connection). Tests: test/session-names.test.ts, socket-abi.test.ts.
  offer-router:
    description: >-
      #offer-router (TD-2026-10-20-812, slice S1; spec §12.9).
      src/kernel/desk/offer-router.ts: a fold over committed rows (daemon
      log.onCommit, like the speakable projector; seed() replays offer.* and
      request.offer.mute at boot). When the main-chain reply ends
      (message_delta or an assistant line with stop_reason end_turn,
      parent_tool_use_id null; T-2026-10-21-006, since Claude Code can emit
      turn.result ~30 s later, after the next turn was sent) it parses the
      text of the turn's FINAL main-chain assistant message with
      offerBlockOf; a tool_use stop never evaluates, a sub-agent message is
      ignored, turn.result evaluates only a turn whose end was not seen, and
      each turn is evaluated once. At most one per reply, none while another
      turn sent before the reply ended is outstanding (a turn sent after it
      does not suppress it), none while the session has a pending card; the mod must be installed, granted, unpaused (DeskMods), not muted
      for the hsid (offer.decided not-now / stop-here) nor its repo
      (request.offer.mute, repo:<16 hex> from the session's repoKey), and the
      kind one of its declared offers; phase, title and contextIn are copied
      from the manifest (Aegis D3). It returns the kernel offer.shown row (the
      daemon appends it). decide(): request.offer.decide from the human on a
      gui/device connection only (Aegis D1), offer live (session running and
      owned by the asker), latest for the hsid, undecided (exactly one
      decision; a concurrent replay is refused before the row commits); writes
      request.offer.decide + offer.decided and on open sends the kernel
      template "Open <title>: use the <handoffTool> tool." as the human
      (session.inject origin kernel). offer.kept once per offer when the
      declared handoffTool on mcp__gnomon-<mod>__ completes without error
      after open. mute(): request.offer.mute row as the kernel. Aegis S1
      fixes: open names the fully qualified tool mcp__<server>__<handoffTool>;
      only kernel-principal rows fold; the next turn.sent retires the shown
      offer; decided once the request row commits (a retry is a replay, no
      second request row), send failures reported (diag send.failed); a mod
      paused or muted after open never counts as kept (diag kept.refused);
      mutes survive session exit until the bounded state is evicted. Every
      evaluation that shows nothing returns a kernel offer.suppressed{hsid,
      turn, why, name?, offerKind?} row for the signed log (founder ruling,
      contracts 8.0:T-2026-10-21-006; closed, checked at append by
      #kernel-log), why in no-block | turn-streaming | pending-card |
      not-offerable | kind-undeclared | malformed; name/offerKind only when
      the block named them; names only, never message text; at most one per
      completed turn, no-block only while the session has an offerable mod.
      Review M2: turn.result{turn} closes every outstanding turn up to the one
      it names (FIFO when absent). Review R3: session.exited, session.open and
      session.drained drop the turn state (outstanding turns, message text,
      evaluation marks; mutes stay, L7), since a resumed session restarts
      its turn ids at /1. Review R4: the turn.result fallback writes no row
      for an errored or interrupted turn (isError, or a subtype other than
      success). Review R1: each outstanding turn is a record evaluated at most
      once; a reply's end is attributed to the oldest outstanding turn not
      yet evaluated (an interleaved stream no longer loses the next turn's
      offer); "others" counts only outstanding turns still streaming (not yet
      evaluated); turn.result keeps the text of a newer turn's message.
      Review R2: a message_delta end_turn is evaluated at its message_stop; a
      new main-chain message in an evaluated turn before its turn.result (a
      Stop-hook continuation) or a blocking Stop hook_response (re-review
      M-4: exact match outcome blocked | decision block | exit_code 2;
      speculative, hook_response needs --include-hook-events) retires the
      shown offer and re-opens the turn; replay lines (isReplay) mark turns
      started, so the stream's turn is exact and a turn folded into a later
      one settles without a row. Re-review L-1: with replay lines an
      unconsumed queued turn is not "streaming"; without them a message after
      every outstanding turn is evaluated continues the previous message's
      turn. Re-review L-2: replay lines before the stream is live (the
      first system.init or turn.sent since a session boundary) are ignored,
      in the router and the supervisor's consumedTurns. Re-review M-2: a retired offer that was
      still undecided is the kernel row offer.retired{offerId, hsid,
      why:'continued'} (OFFER_SEED_KINDS: folded at boot), and a decide on
      it is refused 'retired'. Review C2: gnomon desk stats folds only
      kernel-principal offer.shown / offer.suppressed rows. Review C3
      (verified): desk stats key on offerId (ULID), mod and hsid, the GUI
      slips on offerId anchored by seq, and the router and the GUI drop
      turn state at session.open, so turn ids restarting at /1 after a
      resume collide nowhere. Pause
      and mute stop offers at once; removing the server applies to new
      sessions. CLI
      `gnomon desk stats` (src/cli/desk-cli.ts) folds shown/suppressed (by
      reason)/open/not-now/stop-here/kept per mod from the verified log, and
      the unnamed suppressions (no-block, malformed) per session; re-review
      M-3: a retired, undecided offer leaves shown (counted as retired) and a
      suppression counts once per (hsid, session.open seq, turn).
    path: src/kernel/desk/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, desk, mod-playbooks, paradigm-8]
    flows: [$offer-handoff]
    gates: [^offer-accept]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/offer-router.test.ts
      - test/desk-stats.test.ts
    oracles: [G-DSK-02, G-DSK-03, G-DSK-04]

  mods-admin:
    description: >-
      #mods-admin (the GUI Mods panel's kernel half; founder dogfood
      2026-10-21). src/kernel/modhost/mods-admin.ts: status() merges
      installed / available / catalog into the human class's status.mods
      (pin, tier, source, version, run state, MCP server, offers, Desk counts,
      a shipped first-party tree that is not the pin = update; available =
      packages/gnomon-mods beside the kernel, findFirstPartyRoot, tier by
      hash). request() serves request.mod.install{source:'folder', path}
      (the A8 contracts kind, written as a row) and the dispatcher-only
      request.mod.link|pause|start|uninstall|config, each exactly its
      `gnomon mod` verb (stageInstall, writePause, requestStart,
      requestUninstall, requestMcpConfig), human on a gui/device connection
      only; then StagedCardPickup / McpConfigCards scan at once so the card
      is in the stack before the answer. Signs nothing. A URL source is
      refused UNSUPPORTED (slice S4). 8.0 M4: in a release Gnomon.app the
      catalog is ONLY the app's Contents/Resources/gnomon-mods (#app-bundle;
      never a folder beside the app); elsewhere the nearest gnomon-mods above
      the module (clone, npm package).
    tags: [mods, gui]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/mods-admin.test.ts
      - test/socket-abi.test.ts
      - test/app-bundle.test.ts

  desk-stats:
    description: >-
      #desk-stats. src/kernel/desk/desk-stats.ts: the pure foldDeskStats /
      foldDeskUnnamed `gnomon desk stats` prints (moved from cli/desk-cli.ts,
      which re-exports them) and DeskTally, the kernel's live tally (seeded
      from the log once, fed by onCommit, bounded) behind
      status.mods.installed[].desk.
    tags: [desk]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/mods-admin.test.ts
      - test/desk-stats.test.ts

  mcp-passthrough:
    description: >-
      #mcp-passthrough (TD-2026-10-20-812 A2, slice S1; spec §12.9).
      src/kernel/desk/mcp-passthrough.ts: DeskMods / readDeskMods (installed,
      pinned snapshot verifies, manifest matches its grant, capabilities and
      egress granted, not paused; cached on the grant table) and mergeDeskMcp,
      wired as SupervisorOptions.deskMcp into the session mcp.json: gnomon-<mod>
      (gnomon-<mod>-<entry> for extra entries), never a repo server name nor an
      earlier mod's, repo-muted mods skipped. Per-user launch override (Aegis
      D2): kernel state only (<home>/desk/mcp-overrides.json, 0600), never a
      repo file; `gnomon mod config <mod> --command <abs> --args … --env N=v`
      writes a request under <home>/mods/<mod>/ (session sandboxes cannot write
      the home); McpConfigCards raises a root-class grant card showing
      command, args, env NAMES and the launch hash; only allow writes the
      store. Accepted only for what provides.mcp[].config declares
      (validateMcpUserConfig) and an absolute command; a store entry failing
      the checks falls back to the manifest launch. Fail closed (Aegis S2):
      a server whose RESOLVED command is not absolute (isAbsoluteCommand:
      POSIX /… or a Windows drive path; // and UNC refused) is never added
      (skipped needs-config, daemon diag daemon.desk.mcp.unconfigured with
      the gnomon mod config hint); both shipped first-party defaults fail it
      until configured. S0b regression fix: the session base is the repo's
      VERIFIED GENERATED distro mcp.json (.gnomon/generated/.mcp.json, named
      gm_<pub>_<distro> by renderGenerated) and is passed as opts.distro /
      repoServersOnly(repo, distro): kernel output, so it bypasses the
      reserved-prefix drop that still applies to repo-authored names; a mod
      deriving the same name wins it (PassthroughResult.shadowed, diag
      mcp.distro-shadowed) and a distro server is never in `added` (H1/H1b
      intact). Names come from ONE helper, serverNameOf: an entry
      named <mod> or gnomon-<mod> is gnomon-<mod>, else gnomon-<mod>-<entry>
      (gnomon-neurons-brain); #offer-router's kept check uses the same helper.
      Aegis S1 fixes: repo servers named with a reserved desk prefix
      (RESERVED_DESK_PREFIXES gnomon-, gm_, gx_) are dropped (diag
      mcp.reserved); the daemon records the servers added per hsid and the
      router offers and counts kept only through them; no desk server before
      the router is bound (mcp.unbound); per added server a diag mcp.added
      {server, launchHash, overridden}; launchHashOf is an HMAC under the
      kernel key <home>/desk/launch-hash.key; overrides refuse secret-marked
      env names (manifest provides.mcp[].config.secret), non-.exe commands on
      win32 and args the card would truncate (MCP_CARD_ARGS_MAX); the card
      says env values land in the session's mcp.json and labels the requester
      "a request file under the Gnomon home".
    path: src/kernel/desk/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, desk, mcp, mod-playbooks, paradigm-8]
    tests:
      - test/mcp-passthrough.test.ts
    oracles: [G-DSK-05]

  budget-fuse:
    description: >-
      #budget-fuse as a METER (spec §3.4, §4.8, §10 laws 1/7; §19.1 M1.6;
      founder ruling P8-D92 "track everything, enforce nothing"). Only law 7
      is enforced: permit.reserve of every kind but tool.permission (Aegis L1) on claude-code runs the
      billing scan (env, settings env blocks, apiKeyHelper, mcp.json env
      blocks, any *_BASE_URL incl. loopback; unreadable layers are hits) and
      refuses BILLING_REFUSED, unless the kernel's own spawn of the card's
      hsid holds the per-use api-token (scan still run, only the injected key
      ignored). Every other reserve is granted with a kernel-signed permit:v1
      (attribution, law 1). Removed by P8-D92: day caps, compute and
      concurrency caps, the window threshold and 30 % human reserve, the
      breaker pause, the armada pause and budget card, the resume allowance.
      Tracked per UTC day: tokens + USD per user (P8-D91: an agent's spend
      in its human's bucket; before M1.7 attributed to the root's human
      principal, recorded attributed: root-human on permit.reserve), repo,
      loop, session, model, backend; local-LLM sessions with model and
      projects; subscription-window usage; quota errors (budget.window
      {quotaError}); ladder rungs 50/75/90/100 % as informational
      budget.ladder rows; over-permit usage recorded once (budget.usage
      {overPermit}). Usage only via recordStreamUsage(turn.usage|turn.result);
      malformed ⇒ budget.usage{malformed} and dropped. status.budget exposes
      the spend breakdown (byUser/byRepo/byLoop/bySession/byModel/byBackend,
      local, subscriptionWindow, quotaErrors). config.ts: root-signed
      tracking/display keys only (reference amounts, window shape) with a
      version high-water mark (sidecar ⊔ last signed config.loaded on the
      chain); unsigned/invalid/older/missing-after-signed ⇒ defaults + a
      logged row; project.yaml fuse/budget/strictness/review keys ignored and
      logged. parseReserveParams: no usage params; agents never name
      repo/loop/session/machine (kernel-bound, M1.7). Slice 2: restore(rows)
      at boot rebuilds today's counters, the window, local sessions and the
      announced rungs from budget.usage rows joined to their permit.grant
      (grant-less, malformed, over-permit and non-stream rows count nothing);
      day and window from the chain's last event time (S-L2,
      KernelLog.lastEventAt), never the wall clock. Round 2: restore counts
      only rows whose principal is a kernel principal AND whose keyId is a
      kernel key (KernelLog.isKernelKey; Aegis C1 — these kinds are not
      reserved), and a usage row's user must equal its grant's scope.user;
      a turn counts under its own budget.usage row's `at` (the row is
      written first; runtime and restore agree); chain times beyond wall +
      MAX_CLOCK_SKEW_MS (5 min) are clamped to the wall clock and reported
      once (onClockSkew → daemon event daemon.fuse.clock.skew, no row);
      restore runs before daemon.start, so its "today" is the day of the
      chain's last event. M1.7 note: api-token session turn
      permits are labelled subscription; per-turn billing.api.active needs
      usage against the spawn permit (G-SUB-03).
    path: src/kernel/budget/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, budget, fuse, law-7, paradigm-8, m1.6]
    tests:
      - test/budget-fuse.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LAW-01, G-LAW-01b, G-BUD-01, G-BUD-02, G-BUD-03, G-SUB-04]

  scheduler:
    description: >-
      #scheduler (spec §3.5, §2.4 rule 3, §9.1 schedule.*, §10 law 5; M1.9) in
      src/kernel/scheduler/. Log-derived timers (timer:v1, never stored): the
      last fired due instant of a key is the max dueAt of its
      schedule.automation.due rows, so start() rebuilds every timer from the
      records + the log and no instant fires twice (G-SCH-01). automation:v1
      {id, trigger: {at} | {every, anchor}, model: local|claude|any, attended}
      is a LOCAL shape (not a wire object yet; moving it into
      gnomon-contracts would move clientApiId). Every automation is attended:
      attended:false is REFUSED (schedule.dropped{reason:
      'unattended-unavailable'}) until an #approval-broker decision (M1.6) and
      the run step (M1.7/M1.8) exist (law 5). Instants are strict ISO-8601
      with Z or ±hh:mm; every is 1 min..10 years. Rebuild trusts only
      kernel-principal due rows whose dueAt is not after their own at
      (kernel principals = the unprovisioned p:00000000/kernel and the
      current one, so root provisioning at M1.3 does not refire timers). One daemon ⇒ one timer per key: duplicates
      (N worktrees) are dropped {reason:'duplicate-key'} (G-LAW-05b). An
      instant due while down — or older than one period capped at the
      horizon (a slept host) — fires as an attended event (late:true);
      several missed instants coalesce (schedule.dropped{reason:'coalesced',
      count}). A due row spends nothing; the run step is hard-wired to refuse
      ENGINE{not-implemented} (session.refused) — no spawn, no permit
      (G-LAW-05). Wakes are capped at 60 s; a failed append releases the hold
      and retries in 5 s; stop() is a latch returning the in-flight tick,
      which the daemon awaits before stopping children; the scheduler starts
      before the idle monitor. While the next due instant is inside 10 min
      the scheduler holds idle exit ('timer' holder). Clock and wake are seams
      (now, arm), so the 24 h soak is a unit test. Daemon wiring: DaemonOptions
      .automations (none by default; root-signed config later), handle.scheduler.
      Loops step 6 (gnomon-loops.md §4): triggers add daily {at, tz} and weekly
      {day, at, tz} (zoned.ts: one fire per local date; a time skipped by
      spring-forward fires once at the end of the gap, a repeated one once at
      its first occurrence; zones from the runtime's ICU, an unknown zone
      refuses). parseAutomation's attended:false refusal lifts ONLY through an
      `unattended` predicate (#loop-timers: the loop is trusted at its current
      hash, unpaused, not held by the kill switch). Loop timers register and
      unregister at run time (attachLoops, registerLoop, unregister,
      unregisterAllLoops), keyed loop:<loopKey>#<trigger digest>; a due loop
      timer is re-checked (still registered and trusted), writes its due row
      {source: loop, loopKey, attended:false} and hands off to #loop-runner
      (host.start(loopKey, {type, key, dueAt, late?})) instead of the refusal;
      a late fire is a row for a human unless catchUp: once (runs once); a
      refused hand-off writes schedule.dropped{reason: run-refused, refusal}.
      Non-loop automations keep the hard-wired refusal.
    path: src/kernel/scheduler/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, scheduler, paradigm-8]
    tests:
      - test/scheduler.test.ts
      - test/scheduler-daemon.test.ts
      - test/loop-timers.test.ts
    oracles: [G-SCH-01, G-LAW-05, G-LAW-05b]

  session-runner:
    session-identity-env: >-
      Fix 2026-10-20 (not_subscription_auth after a self-upgrade). runner/env.ts
      resolveSessionIdentity / sessionIdentity (once per process; the
      supervisor resolves it in its constructor and hands it to LocalLane):
      on POSIX buildSessionEnv{identity} drops IDENTITY_KEYS (USER LOGNAME
      SHELL TMPDIR) from the daemon-env copy and sets USER=LOGNAME=
      os.userInfo().username, SHELL=os.userInfo().shell (absolute only),
      TMPDIR (darwin) = /usr/bin/getconf DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR run with an
      empty env, validTempDir: absolute, realpath a dir owned by the uid,
      mode & 022 == 0. Anything the OS cannot give is OMITTED, never taken
      from the env. win32: identity null, allowlist unchanged. The darwin
      profile extras.tmpdir (screen-socket deny) is the session's TMPDIR,
      else DARWIN_SCREEN_TMP_FALLBACK '/private/tmp' (Aegis L1). Identity
      also carries home = os.userInfo().homedir: the user home is never $HOME
      (Aegis L2). missingIdentity ⇒ one session.sandbox{phase:'identity',
      missing} row at daemon start, names only; not_subscription_auth with
      no USER names the cause (Aegis ruling 3). PATH (TD-2026-10-21-077):
      buildSessionEnv{path} REPLACES any daemon PATH with sessionPathFor =
      root-signed config.sessions.path (budget/config.ts parseSessionPath:
      1..32 absolute dirs, <= 1024 chars, no NUL/CR/LF, no ':' POSIX / ';'
      win32; invalid ⇒ whole config falls back, logged, default PATH) else
      defaultSessionPath (darwin: node dir, <os home>/.local/bin,
      /opt/homebrew/bin /usr/local/bin /usr/bin /bin /usr/sbin /sbin; linux
      without /opt/homebrew/bin; win32 null = the allowlisted PATH). Tests:
      test/session-env-identity.test.ts, test/session-supervisor.test.ts
      ("session env identity from the OS"), test/local-lane-spawn.test.ts
      (Aegis L1/L2), test/budget-fuse.test.ts (config.sessions.path).
      TD-2026-10-21-162 (Aegis M): planSessionPath write-denies every
      user-writable PATH dir (the supervisor's #pathDenies, passed to
      LocalLane as the REQUIRED pathDenies with the REQUIRED sessionPath):
      in-home dirs (~/.local/bin, nvm/volta node), a Homebrew prefix whole
      (<prefix>/bin|sbin + <prefix>/Cellar, by realpath, never
      HOMEBREW_PREFIX; /usr/local only bin/sbin/Cellar), any other
      owner/W_OK dir; the home or its ancestors are dropped from PATH.
      PERSISTENCE_PATHS gains .local/bin. sessionNodeDir = dirname of the
      realpath of process.execPath (kept; denied when writable).
      osUserHome() is the one home helper the daemon's law-7 user layer and
      claude source use (never $HOME). Tests: session-env-identity
      (planSessionPath), session-sandbox (EPERM rig: ~/.local/bin and a
      brew-shaped prefix unwritable, read + exec ok), session-supervisor
      (real /opt/homebrew where user-writable).
    model-picker: >-
      Founder ask 2026-10-20 ("is there no way to change the model?").
      session.open{model}: anything other than the routing values
      claude/local/any must pass contracts claudeModelProblem (an alias in
      CLAUDE_MODEL_ALIASES or a strict claude-... id, never a value starting
      with "-"), refused BAD_REQUEST{field:model} BEFORE any row, permit or
      process; `default` opens with no --model. The live session keeps its
      model (Session.model, status.sessions[].model, modelOf(hsid)); the
      session.open row and the spawn permit carry it (the fuse's model
      axis). Tests: test/model-picker.test.ts, test/session-model.test.ts.
    model-switch: >-
      8.0:model-switch (probe p-set-model PASS, claude 2.1.304). The human's
      log.append{kind:'request.session.model', payload:{hsid, model}} from a
      gui/device connection (dispatch.ts branch; HUMAN_REQUEST_KINDS) reaches
      SessionPort.model -> SessionSupervisor.switchModel. Non-human refused
      FORBIDDEN in the supervisor too; refused before any row: local-session,
      turn-running, model-switching, context-too-large (ANY session toward a
      target without [1m] above 160k tokens, the LAST main-chain assistant
      message's usage, subagent messages excluded; a resumed session's
      context is unknown until its first main-chain message and a 200k
      target is refused "send one message first" meanwhile, Aegis M2). Then request row -> law 7 re-run -> a NEW
      subscription session.spawn permit for the new model (assertClaudePermit)
      -> session.inject{channel:model} -> adapter controlSetModelLine ->
      control_response by request id (modelSwitchTimeoutMs, default 15 s).
      switched: the new permit replaces the spawn permit, the old one is
      released; backend-refused: MODEL_UNAVAILABLE{model-switch-failed};
      timeout: ENGINE{model-switch-timeout}, the new permit HELD in
      modelUncertain (Aegis M1), status.sessions[].modelUncertain until the
      next main-chain message.model reconciles it (session.model{reconciled}):
      to the new model the held permit replaces the spawn permit as on
      success and the row cites it; to the old one, or the session exiting,
      or a later switch, releases it. A failed permit release goes to the
      injected Paradigm-logger-shaped SupervisorOptions.logger (Aegis L4).
      The adapter passes claude's control_response error/error_code through
      controlErrorText (contracts sanitizeDisplayText, one line, bounded;
      Aegis L3). session.send refused model-switching while
      in flight. planResume: a switched/reconciled session.model sets the
      resumed model and permit. Tests: test/session-model.test.ts,
      test/resume-plan.test.ts, test/socket-abi.test.ts.
    description: >-
      #session-runner (spec §3.3 Preflight/Environment, §3.4 law 7, §16;
      M1.7 slice 1) in src/kernel/runner/. preflight.ts: the claude binary by
      explicit path (root-signed config) else the platform registry's known
      locations (src/platform/claude-code.ts), NEVER PATH (p01); realpath +
      sha256; claude_binary_changed on a changed sha; version >= 2.1.288;
      `auth status` = claude.ai/firstParty. law7Layers: EVERY layer the child
      reads for THIS spawn's cwd (T-009 / Aegis C5) — managed sources per OS
      (+ managed-settings.d/*.json; unscannable sources such as Windows policy
      keys are hits), user settings*.json, project + worktree
      .claude/settings(.local).json and .mcp.json, ~/.claude.json mcpServers
      AND projects.<cwd>.mcpServers, the session's own policy.json/mcp.json —
      scanned over the CHILD env (scanApiBilling). env.ts: allowlist + scrub +
      GNOMON_HSID/SOCK/SESSION_KEY, envKeys names only (G-RUN-02). The
      darwin `session` profile generator is src/platform/darwin/session-sbpl.ts
      (from the M1.5 SessionPolicy + session-denies registry; T-008 git
      denies: .git pointer file, config.worktree, modules/**/{hooks,config,
      info}; launchers open/osascript/lsregister/launchctl by realpath),
      SLICE 2 (T-2026-10-18-010): the WHOLE Gnomon home is read-denied
      (keys/ incl. the device key, kernel.db*, log/** segments, every other
      session dir, the pin); policy.ts homeReadable carves back only the
      session's own sessions/<short> dir and the exec'd bin/ copy (or the
      runners/<tree> copy), refusing anything outside HOME_READABLE_ROOTS
      by realpath; SBPL order deny home, allow own, metadata on ancestors,
      re-deny keys + kernel log (profile v3; test/session-sandbox EPERM rig).
      registered in src/kernel/sandbox/session.ts (no backend => refused
      sandbox-unavailable; status fidelity per platform, G-WIN-06).
      p10m-claude-compat-generated (probes/) re-ran real Claude Code 2.1.302
      under the GENERATED profile with the adapter argv: PASS, with writes to
      the whole ~/.claude/** and ~/.claude.json denied and NO per-session
      CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR (a custom CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR loses subscription auth).
      ROUND 2 (Aegis H1/M1/M2/L2): binary.ts pins the claude sha256 in
      <home>/claude-pin.json on first use (logged session.sandbox{phase:
      'binary-pin'}); another hash is claude_binary_changed until an explicit
      repinClaudeBinary (CLI/test seam until the gate-class card); the kernel
      executes only a private copy <home>/bin/claude-<sha> written from the
      same verified buffer, and preflight --version / auth status run that
      copy UNDER the session profile. The profile also write-denies the
      binary realpath, its versions dir and every known-location link
      (supervisor binaryDenies) and the user persistence paths
      (policy.ts PERSISTENCE_PATHS: LaunchAgents, shell rc files, git config,
      ~/.ssh); shortcuts/automator join the launcher exec denies; copied
      Apple launchers die by launch constraints (137, spec §A.2(iv)). Law 7
      also flags ~/.claude.json primaryApiKey / customApiKeyResponses.approved
      and forceLoginMethod 'console' (billing.ts). The p10m re-run with the
      private copy and the new denies: PASS.
      ROUND 3: trust on first use — the pin is written only after the
      sandboxed preflight passes (N1); the copy (not the source) is hashed,
      streamed, and not re-hashed when size+mtime match the pin record;
      script installs (#!, the npm cli.js) are refused
      claude_binary_unsupported; `gnomon session repin-claude`
      (src/cli/session-cli.ts: TTY + typed last 8 of the new sha, daemon
      stopped) writes a re-pin the supervisor logs session.sandbox{binary-pin,
      by:'repin', from, to} at the next open; PERSISTENCE_PATHS adds
      .bash_login, .zlogout, ~/.config/fish.
      macOS FLOOR (P8 note, CI run 39100281741): SESSION_SANDBOX_MIN_MACOS = 15
      (platform/darwin/session-sbpl.ts, read via platform/darwin/macos.ts
      sw_vers); below it the darwin session backend refuses
      sandbox-unavailable {floor:macos, have, need} and status shows the
      session sandbox unavailable — on macos-14 a copied osascript reached
      System Events from inside the profile. CI tests macos-15 (enforcement)
      and macos-14 (floor refusal).
      SIP FLOOR (CI run 39100811385, GitHub macos-15 runner, SIP off: a copied
      launchctl not SIGKILLed): darwinSipEnabled (platform/darwin/macos.ts,
      csrutil status, cached; Custom Configuration counts as disabled) — SIP
      not plainly enabled ⇒ sandbox-unavailable {floor:sip, have:disabled|
      unknown}, status unavailable reason sip-disabled; test seam
      SupervisorOptions.sipEnabled / sessionLaunchPlan's 4th arg. Profile v2
      adds (deny mach-lookup (global-name "com.apple.coreservices.appleevents"))
      beside (deny appleevent-send) (APPLEEVENT_MACH_SERVICES); Claude verified
      working (p10m-claude-compat-generated pass). Its coverage of a non-Apple
      AppleEvent sender is UNPROVEN (spec OPEN 5): the existing copied-osascript
      check (`get name` of System Events) is answered without sending an event,
      so its exit is only RECORDED (test annotation), never asserted; the 137
      launch-constraint kill is asserted on SIP-on macOS 15+ only.
      Aegis conditions: darwin automation:apple-events fidelity is 'partial'
      while spec OPEN 5 is open (status must not overclaim); inherited TCC
      grants of the responsible process + the reachable pasteboard are spec
      §A.2(v) / OPEN 22 (recommend launching gnomond by launchd or the signed
      app); the csrutil parser test keeps the exact macOS 15 stdout fixture.
      TRANSCRIPTS (TD-2026-10-19-814 item 1, probe p-resume darwin; profile
      v6): a Claude Code session (supervisor claudeTranscripts: true; local
      sessions never) gets policy.allowWrite = [<userClaudeDir>/projects,
      subpath], rendered (allow file-write* ...) AFTER the ~/.claude write
      deny (SBPL last match wins), then (deny file-write* ...) again on
      policy.denyWriteAlways + the T-008 git regexes; no later rule allows
      writes. denyWriteAlways = the projects dir itself as a LITERAL (Aegis
      M1: no rm/rename/symlink swap of it; children still match the allow;
      the supervisor's ensureTranscriptDir creates it before the spawn, never
      over an existing entry), the denies inside it, and the steering regex
      re-rooted AT projects (Aegis L3; the home-rooted one would re-close
      ~/.claude). Hardlinks (Aegis M2): measured on macOS 15 that link(2)
      into projects from a write-denied source (~/.claude/settings.json,
      ~/.claude.json) is refused by the source's write deny; no file-link
      rule needed, tests assert it. ~/.claude/settings*, plugins,
      CLAUDE.md, the rest of ~/.claude and ~/.claude.json stay denied. The
      allow is derived from the user Claude dir only (a flag, never an input
      path: no manifest can widen it) and WITHHELD fail closed
      (allowWriteRefused, logged session.sandbox{phase:'transcripts-withheld'})
      when projects resolves elsewhere (symlink) or another deny covers it.
      policy.json's Edit deny on ~/.claude/** is unchanged. Residual,
      ACCEPTED by the founder (TD-2026-10-19-814): (a) a session can write
      (and delete) memory and transcripts for ANY cwd's projects/<enc>/, not
      only its own; (b) it can forge transcript history a later --resume
      loads; (c) the readers include the user's own unsandboxed Claude Code;
      (d) a link planted inside projects (e.g. MEMORY.md -> ~/.zshrc) can
      redirect a later write by the host's Claude Code outside projects.
      SESSION-ENV (hooks fix 2026-10-21; Aegis H1/M1 rework, profile v8): a
      second carve-back scoped to THIS session only, ~/.claude/session-env/<uuid>
      (subpath, class session-env), supervisor input claudeSessionEnv: <uuid>
      (strict SESSION_UUID_RE), so Claude Code can mkdir session-env/<id> and
      run command hooks. Never all of session-env: a sibling's CLAUDE_ENV_FILE
      is sourced as shell by that session (host Claude Code included).
      session-env and session-env/<uuid> are literal re-denied; siblings stay
      under the ~/.claude deny. ensureSessionEnvDir pre-creates both at 0700,
      lstat-refusing links, foreign-uid or group/world-writable dirs (L-2). Withheld fail closed (sessionEnvRefused, logged
      session.sandbox{phase:'session-env-withheld'}). A new live id (/clear)
      is logged session.sandbox{phase:'session-env-mismatch'}: hooks fail
      closed again. Probe p-session-env: hook -> CLAUDE_ENV_FILE -> Bash sees
      it; --resume keeps the uuid; B2: a LEFTOVER env file is sourced by a
      later resume even without the carve, so (Aegis M-B) emptySessionEnvDir
      empties session-env/<uuid> at every session end (#onExit: exited,
      drained, killed; daemon orphan sweep via sweepOrphans onOrphaned + the
      session.open uuid), lstat-only, never through a link, logged
      session.sandbox{phase:'session-env-emptied'}. Tests:
      test/session-sandbox.test.ts, test/session-env-cleanup.test.ts.
      Linux/Windows: data only, no backend. repin-card.ts (TD-2026-10-19-063):
      the GUI re-pin of the claude binary — ClaudeRepinCards pre-stages ONE
      #approval-broker gate-class card per new sha (on an open refused
      claude_binary_changed, and on a poll of the resolved source at
      REPIN_POLL_MS cadence that never writes a pin); allow = fresh presence
      (one prompt), card TTL 24 h, a deny suppresses the sha; the publisher
      is checked on the private copy by src/platform/claude-verifier.ts
      (codesign, team Q6L2SF6YDW) and anything else sets claim-mismatch; the
      effect re-hashes the copy and writes the pin with signer + approval
      {cardId, contentHash, decisionSig}; live sessions keep their copy.
      Aegis fixes: the effect refuses unless the pin still equals the card's
      `from` (no rollback) and expires every other pending re-pin card; the
      card is voice-hand-only (broker, §14.7); binaryKind runs on the copy; a
      denied sha's refusal says "re-pin denied; restart Gnomon to re-offer";
      the offline CLI prints the codesign signer (loud on a mismatch).
    path: src/kernel/runner/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, session, law-7, paradigm-8, m1.7]
    tests:
      - test/claude-repin-card.test.ts
      - test/claude-verifier-darwin.test.ts
      - test/session-preflight.test.ts
      - test/session-profile.test.ts
      - test/session-sandbox.test.ts
    oracles: [G-SUB-01, G-RUN-02, G-RUN-06, G-SBX-04, G-SBX-05, G-SBX-06, G-SBX-08, G-WIN-06]

  supervisor:
    session-identity-env: >-
      SupervisorOptions.identity (seam; default runner/env.ts
      sessionIdentity()) is resolved ONCE in the constructor and passed to
      buildSessionEnv for both lanes (LocalLaneOptions.identity), so a
      session's USER/LOGNAME/SHELL/TMPDIR come from the OS whatever env the
      daemon inherited (#session-runner session-identity-env). The darwin
      profile extras.tmpdir is env.TMPDIR ?? os.tmpdir().
    usage-split: >-
      #ledger-mod (K2 feed v2): the Claude turn.result row additionally
      records split {in, out, cacheRead, cacheWrite} (the turn's own totals
      from result.usage) when the stream reported a usage object; additive,
      numbers only, no budget/fuse behaviour change. The #mod-host feed
      projects it as tokensIn/tokensOut/cacheRead/cacheWrite.
    description: >-
      AUTO-EDITS READ-ONLY BASH (TD-2026-10-20-294 part 1):
      auto-edits.ts autoBashReadOnly passes a Bash request with no card,
      after approval.auto{modeRef:'mode:auto-edits:read-only'} (log before
      effect), only when its input keys are command/description/timeout, no
      root is home-or-above, the card is not flagged and #shell-readonly
      proves the command read-only; in auto-edits and (F1, TD-2026-10-20-086)
      ask (modeRef mode:ask:read-only), never in manual. K1 ANCHOR: classified
      with ctx.cwd = realpath(s.cwd); the allow sends updatedInput.command =
      anchorCommand(command, root, shellFlavour(session env SHELL));
      approval.auto{path: root, inputHash: the request's}; the session.inject
      row of the allow carries inputHash and anchoredInputHash. F7: the
      kernel-written policy.json carries env
      CLAUDE_BASH_MAINTAIN_PROJECT_WORKING_DIR=1 (manifest/policy.ts
      CLAUDE_SESSION_ENV). Misses are not logged yet (approval.auto.miss needs
      a clientApiId move).
      session.send accepts source 'voice' from a human only, as a dictation
      attribution hint for #gnomon-voice (TD-2026-10-20-262).
      #supervisor (spec §3.3, §6.1, §5 verbs 2-4; M1.7 slice 1) in
      src/kernel/supervisor/. P8-D96 PERMISSION CHANNEL (probe p01b):
      sessions run --permission-prompts host --permission-prompt-tool stdio;
      each can_use_tool request is answered exactly once (pending map per
      session, cap 64, entry deleted before the answer; card raised inside
      the stdout queue, decision awaited outside it). permission.ts (pure)
      composes the card (Bash full command; Edit path + old/new; Write path,
      bytes, excerpt, inputHash; other tools capped canonical JSON; detail
      {tool, toolUseId, requestId, inputHash, repo, path?}), auto-denies
      writes under denyWrite and malformed AskUserQuestion input, and
      maps the outcome: only allow / approve-plan / answer allow. ASK-USER
      (gui-complete item 1): AskUserQuestion is an `ask-user` card (cached
      class, decision `answer` only, 10 min timeout => deny "no answer");
      the questions are validated (<= 8, <= 16 options, one-line bounded
      text) and shown in a fixed line grammar (question N / option N.M) the
      GUI picker reads; approve{decision:'answer', note} carries the plain
      answer (one question) or JSON {answers:{question: answer}}, checked by
      parseAskAnswer against the KERNEL-held questions before presence
      (broker RaiseInput.checkNote, BAD_REQUEST bad-params); the allow is
      updatedInput = {...kernel-held input, answers}; approval.decide logs
      noteHash/noteBytes only (privateNote). ROUND 2 (Aegis C1/L1/L4): the
      AskUserQuestion schema is closed (an extra key at any level is
      bad-input); the questions are structured card detail (inside
      contentHash) carried in one {type:'ask-user', detail} block node the
      GUI verifies against contentHash; answers are null-prototype objects;
      every multi-line field body is fenced by fieldLines (FIELD_BODY_PREFIX
      + an end marker; model-chosen keys JSON-quoted), the shared helper
      that replaces the hardening lane's T-032(2) copy. Accepted risk (spec
      §3.4, Aegis C3, T-2026-10-19-001): the note is in neither the
      decisionSig nor the presence binding. PLAN TEXT (item 3):
      claude 2.1.302 sends ExitPlanMode with input:{} (p01b planMode, after
      a plan-file Write the session policy denies); the supervisor keeps the
      assistant tool_use ExitPlanMode input.plan (by tool_use id, bounded)
      and the last `.claude/plans/*.md` Write content, and the plan card
      shows it labelled "not in the request" with detail.planHash (so the
      contentHash covers it); a plan in the request wins. updatedInput is the
      kernel-held input; never updatedPermissions. Deny on no broker, raise
      failure, expiry, cancel (control_cancel_request => expire
      'cancelled'), duplicate requestId (both + approval.suspect), exit/stop
      (expire 'session-exited', nothing written). Every answer is logged
      session.inject{origin:'kernel', hash, bytes} first. A permit never
      means allow (P8-D92). ROUND 2 (Aegis): the card block is wrappedBlock
      (never cut); every input field shown (key=value); MultiEdit per edit,
      NotebookEdit source in full, Write the only excerpt; auto-deny
      sandbox-escape / bad-input / too-large; deniedWrite case-folds on
      darwin+win32; policy.json permissions.ask for ALWAYS_ASK_TOOLS;
      control_request turn.event rows hash-only; a malformed body under a
      valid id is denied, a bad id logs approval.suspect
      malformed-permission-request; the ungated-tool-use detector
      (tool_result of a gated tool with no request); pending entries carry
      closedWhy (the expire why); repo-hook cards for PreToolUse /
      PermissionRequest carry TOOL_APPROVAL_WARNING. P8-D94 / G-SUB-07 (M1.7 slice 2, founder
      ruling): repo-hooks.ts extracts the repo's project/local hooks (strict;
      O_NOFOLLOW + fstat; events allow-listed to CLAUDE_HOOK_EVENTS;
      COMMAND_UNSAFE, unknown keys, non-command types refused by id;
      symlinked/oversized/unparseable layer or >64 hooks => none run; roots
      deduped by realpath), raises one gate-class card per undecided hook
      (install-card rendering via wrappedBlock, with the kernel warning line
      REPO_HOOK_WARNING), pins decisions to (repoKey, setHash) — read back
      by repoHookDecisionsFrom from KERNEL-signed gate-class.decided rows
      only (allow|deny; a withdraw records nothing) — and writes only
      approved hooks into the session's policy.json under
      --setting-sources user (hooks mode `audited`, the default; probe
      p10m-repo-surfaces). Pending cards bounded (MAX_PENDING_HOOK_CARDS).
      P8-D97 / G-SUB-08 (founder ruling 2026-10-18; probe p10m-repo-context,
      real claude 2.1.303, pass): repo-context.ts loads the repo's CONTEXT in
      `audited` — memoryDirsOf (cwd up to the git top-level holding
      CLAUDE.md / .claude/CLAUDE.md / .claude/rules, never the user home) ->
      --add-dir + REPO_CONTEXT_ENV (CLAUDE_CODE_ADDITIONAL_DIRECTORIES_CLAUDE_MD=1);
      buildRepoContext copies .claude/{skills,agents,commands} into
      <session dir>/context, a --plugin-dir named `project`, frontmatter
      ALLOW-LISTED (sanitizeFrontmatter: hooks, allowed-tools, permissionMode,
      mcpServers, unknown keys dropped; tabs/quoted/flow/merge/duplicate
      keys or unterminated blocks refuse the file); symlinks refused; caps
      MAX_CONTEXT_FILES / MAX_CONTEXT_BYTES / MAX_CONTEXT_FILE_BYTES.
      Frontmatter hooks are stripped and listed, never approvable. The
      repo's non-hook settings are NOT loaded: repoSettingsInfo /
      repoSettingsLines put counts and key names (never values) on each
      repo-hook card and on gate-class.detected{what:'repo-context'}.
      ROUND 2 (Aegis): auditMemoryDir refuses a memory dir with a symlinked
      memory file or an @ import (importTargets, Claude's grammar, depth 5)
      reaching outside the git top-level (memoryRefused, on cards via
      repoContextLines); hasShellPreprocessing refuses skills/commands with
      !`...` or ```! and policy.json gets REPO_CONTEXT_SETTINGS
      (disableSkillShellExecution); every SKILL.md at any depth sanitized; M3
      continuation/control-character rules; memoryChainOf feeds the policy's
      memoryDirs write-denies (M1); dotfiles skipped, readdir failures refused.
      ROUND 3 (Aegis): memoryFilesUnder audits each memory dir's whole
      SUBTREE (claude.md any case/depth, .claude/rules/**; symlinked
      claude.md/.claude refuse; VCS dir + node_modules not walked; cap
      MAX_SUBTREE_DIRS; outer walk reused by nested dirs); the policy's
      `steering` match (memorySubtree) write-denies CLAUDE*.md and .claude/**
      below the git top-level in any case (SBPL regex + Edit globs); imports
      leaving the repo refuse before lstat, % tokens and entity-encoded @
      refuse, an import still pending at depth 5 refuses; every .md in a skill
      tree (SKILL.md any case) is sanitized and written sanitized.
      FOLLOW-UPS (Aegis F1-F3): symlinked dirs in the subtree are realpath'd
      (outside the git top or into node_modules refuses); `dep-steering`
      read-denies CLAUDE*.md and .claude/** under any node_modules (SBPL
      regex + Read rules); every in-repo import target (existing or not) is a
      write-deny literal (memoryImports, MAX_IMPORT_TARGETS) and the repo
      context is staged before the policy; permission.ts deniedWrite matches
      `steering`, so those writes are auto-denied with no card.
      F3a: an SBPL-inexpressible import target refuses only its dir; F4: the
      subtree audit collects /^claude[^/]*\.md$/i (CLAUDE.local.md too); F1a:
      dangling symlinked dirs resolved lexically (outside/node_modules refuse).
      Residual: carded Bash can restructure ancestor dirs mid-session.
      M1.8 slice 2: port.ts serves session.send{steer}
      (boolean; local sessions only, a Claude session refuses BAD_REQUEST);
      probeLocal() is the daemon-start local probe; the daemon passes the
      root-signed local config. The one place `claude -p` starts. Slice 1:
      ATTENDED HUMAN-started Claude Code sessions only (others FORBIDDEN;
      the scheduler run step still refuses). open: session.open -> preflight
      -> session dir policy.json/mcp.json from generated plugin bytes
      VERIFIED against the kernel-held record (T-008, manifest/generate.ts
      verifiedGenerated) -> per-spawn law-7 scan (hit => billing.api.refused +
      session.refused{api_billing_refused}, BILLING_REFUSED, before any
      permit) -> permit.reserve{session.spawn, claude-code, kernelSpawn} ->
      assertClaudePermit (Aegis: never claude without a live law-7-scanned
      claude-code session.spawn permit for this hsid) -> generated session
      profile -> DaemonChildren.intend (intent row BEFORE fork) -> spawn
      detached -> track (boot + start-time witness) -> session.sandbox{hashes}.
      stdout: every line a turn.event first (S2); usage only from stream
      events against the SPAWN permit (fuse.recordStreamUsage); ask-user /
      plan cards via the broker; init.apiKeySource != none kills the group;
      quota recorded (P8-D92 tracking-only fuse). send: a turn permit (caller's
      validated or kernel-reserved), law 7 re-scanned, session.inject{client}
      -> turn.sent -> stream-json user line. stop: interrupt = control_request
      only; else staged (control message, stdin close, DaemonChildren.stop
      SIGTERM -> SIGKILL on the group). Mid-session watch (G-SUB-05):
      layersHash + gitHash polled every 500 ms; a law-7 hit stops the session
      within 2 s. port.ts: the dispatcher's session port (human class only);
      daemon.ts wires it. ROUND 2: the exit listener is attached synchronously
      at spawn (an early-exiting child is cleaned up: watch cleared, permits
      released, moved to a bounded history); one catch from reserve to track
      releases the permit, kills a stray child, removes the session dir;
      layers hashed before the law-7 scan and re-checked right before spawn;
      child HOME forced to the scanned user home; caller turn permits must be
      live, unexpired, subscription and single-use; turn permits FIFO
      (review M2: turnIds beside them and consumedTurns, the replayed human
      messages; one result closes every consumed turn, a queued message folded
      into the running turn included, releases each permit and writes
      turn.result{turn} = the latest closed; no replay seen = the head only;
      the local lane names the turn it closes too; re-review M-1: probe
      p-replay-order shows the replay is written on consumption, so this
      stands; a result with no outstanding turn writes
      session.anomaly{hsid, why:'result-without-turn'}); the
      watch hashes ~/.claude.json only through its law-7 projection;
      stdout paused above a queue high-water mark and lines over 64 KiB
      logged as {lineHash, bytes}; init without apiKeySource logged absent.
      ROUND 3: 'exit' only resumes paused stdout; the release path runs on
      'close' so a result written just before exit is recorded; #wire runs
      before the post-track log row, and a failure after track stops the
      group (the exit path releases the permits); a stray child before track
      is SIGKILLed as a group; preflight spawns are async; used turn-permit
      ids are pruned on expiry; status shows the claude pin.
    path: src/kernel/supervisor/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, session, paradigm-8, m1.7]
    tests:
      - test/session-supervisor.test.ts
      - test/repo-hooks.test.ts
      - test/permission-session.test.ts
      - test/repo-context.test.ts
    oracles: [G-RUN-03, G-SUB-05, G-SUB-07, G-SUB-08]

  shell-readonly:
    description: >-
      #shell-readonly (spec §3.4 run modes, TD-2026-10-20-294 part 1, Aegis
      re-check C1 C2 H1-H4 M1-M3 + Low closed) in
      src/kernel/supervisor/shell-readonly.ts. Side-effect free, fails closed;
      reads the filesystem (realpath, readdir) only to check symlinks and
      expand globs. parseShell is a real tokenizer/parser for a shell subset
      (quotes, escapes, line continuations, $NAME/${NAME}, $(...), backticks,
      <(...), globs, tilde, comments, ; && || | & newline, subshells, for-in
      loops, redirections with IO numbers, heredocs); it refuses $((...)),
      $'...', $"...", >(...), |&, ;& and ;;&, ;; outside a case, (Aegis C1)
      any line of an UNQUOTED heredoc (<< or <<-) ending in an odd run of
      backslashes (the shell joins it before the delimiter match), braces
      other than the safe brace expansion, ~user, zsh $= $~ $^ forms and every
      other compound. TD-2026-10-20-086 grammar (each fail-closed): brace
      expansion {a,b} (two or more safe literal items, no nesting, no $, = or ~)
      and {N..M} (non-negative integers, at most 200), never right after a
      $NAME; case WORD in pat) list ;; esac (patterns literal + glob chars
      only, bodies walked, the subject is text); a local assignment name=$(...)
      or name=lit with no command word (lowercase, not LOOP_VAR_DENY, not a
      zsh hook array *_functions, not mapfile or a lowercase proxy variable
      (Aegis L2; deniedVarName, also for loop names), not a loop variable) makes name TAINTED text for the later items of its list
      when it ran unconditionally (first or after ; / newline): "$name" only
      where a substitution may be (echo/printf args, <<<), unquoted only as
      the case subject; X=1 cmd and uppercase/special names stay refused. classifyReadOnly proves read-only:
      allowlisted command names with per-command flag rules; C1 a $(...) or
      backtick word is only echo/printf text or a <<< word, never a path, and
      a for loop iterates only literal words and literal globs (expanded now,
      each value one safe token), so every path a reader gets is concrete and
      checked; C2 printf takes no flag (printf -v), echo only -n/-e/-E, test
      only POSIX operators, pwd/which/basename/dirname/tr tight flags; H1 awk
      runs one `[PATTERN] { print ITEMS }` program; H2 globs expanded against
      the filesystem with a superset matcher, every match realpath-checked,
      > 200 matches or a match starting with - refused, no recursive or
      symlink-following reader flags (grep -r/-R/-S/-O/-d/-D, ls -L/-R, du -L,
      stat -L, diff -r, find -L/-follow), rg removed, diff takes no directory
      operand, a leading unquoted = (zsh =cmd) is refused, test -a FILE is
      path-checked; H3 + K1 (TD-2026-10-20-086) ctx.cwd is the anchored
      root's realpath (the caller runs anchorCommand: builtin cd [-q] --
      'root' && builtin unset CDPATH RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH GREP_OPTIONS && {\n<cmd>\n}); a cd is a whole
      top-level item to an absolute path or (with a known cwd) a relative
      existing directory, no .. segment, never -, + or = led, inside a session
      root as given and at realpath; the top-level list tracks the SET of
      possible cwds (only the cd target along an unbroken && run, every cwd
      seen after ; newline or ||; at most 8) and walks each item once per cwd;
      git only while the cwd is a session root; H4 reads only
      inside the session roots and /usr /opt/homebrew /etc /tmp /private/tmp
      /private/etc (as given and at the deepest-existing-ancestor realpath;
      ctx.readRoots may only narrow that system list),
      plus the device/protected-root deny; M1 date display only; M2 the cwd
      is known only along an unbroken && chain from that cd, else relative
      paths, implicit-cwd reads and git raise the card; M3 no git remote -v (RW: anchored only),
      no credential-bearing git config key; Low git branch NAME only after
      the long --list. RC3 git also: show-ref ls-tree rev-list shortlog
      merge-base for-each-ref, worktree list, stash list (flags only), reflog
      show, cat-file -t/-s/-p OBJECT, tag -l/--list (listing flags only); all
      through gitOptBad. Aegis H1 (both modes): git is refused when the repo
      git would use from the cwd (nearest .git dir or gitdir: file, its
      config, config.worktree, the commondir config, every include/includeIf
      path) names an exec-class key (core.fsmonitor unless false, core.pager
      sshCommand hooksPath alternateRefsCommand askPass gitProxy,
      diff.external, diff.*.textconv/command, filter.*, pager.*,
      remote.*.uploadpack/receivepack, gpg[.*].program,
      uploadpack.packObjectsHook, credential.helper; repoGitExecKey); and
      anchorCommand exports command-scope GIT_CONFIG_COUNT/KEY_n/VALUE_n
      (ANCHOR_GIT_CONFIG: core.fsmonitor=false, core.pager=cat,
      core.hooksPath=/dev/null, core.sshCommand=false, log.showSignature=false,
      gpg[.ssh|.x509].program=false, credential.helper=) plus
      GIT_NO_LAZY_FETCH=1 and GIT_PAGER=cat. Prelude hardening (Aegis M2,
      L1): zsh unfunction chpwd cd, chpwd_functions=(), setopt
      no_ignore_braces; bash unset -f cd, set -B; then a runtime guard
      (set -- x{a,b}; test $# -eq 2) so the command runs only with brace
      expansion really on. No background/negation/env assignment, output
      redirection only to /dev/null. simpleArgv returns the argv of a single
      literal simple command (prefix always rules; signature unchanged).
      RW read-only widening (founder decision 2026-10-20: auto-edits never
      prompts more than native Claude Code), ANCHORED posix runs only
      (ctx.cwd set; win32 and unanchored callers keep the rules above):
      grep/egrep/fgrep -r/--recursive (-R -S -O -d -D still refused, the walked
      cwd checked); rg (no -L --follow -z --search-zip --pre --pre-glob
      --hostname-bin; the anchor also unsets RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH); fd (no -x
      --exec -X --exec-batch -l --list-details -L --follow); tree (no -o -R -l
      --fromfile --fromtabfile --output; fd's pattern and tree -P/-I values are patterns); git remote -v / get-url NAME (Aegis M1: a card when any repo/global/system config scope has an insteadOf/pushInsteadOf key or a userinfo URL; gitRemoteUrlRisk); paradigm status|st,
      doctor [--context] [--explain], ripple SYMBOL [PATH], purpose validate
      [PATH], task ls [STATUS], task show REF (no path separator), lore
      list|ls [filters], lore show L-ID (cwd at a session root; they pass on
      their grammar alone: paradigm's declared READ_ONLY_COMMANDS, proven
      byte-identical by its readonly-commands harness from paradigm 7.15.2, and only when paradigmOnPath finds @a-company/paradigm >= 7.15.2 first on the session PATH (realpath + package.json, cached by mtime; otherwise a card);
      never index, task ls --board/--project or a write verb); gnomon desk stats, daemon status, mod list, mod status
      [NAME], rules list, trace status, replay [--json] [--expect H] (default
      home only; never bare trace or a write verb).
    tags: [kernel, security]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/shell-readonly.test.ts
      - test/shell-readonly-fs.test.ts
      - test/shell-readonly-cwd.test.ts
      - test/shell-readonly-widen.test.ts
      - test/auto-edits-anchor.test.ts
      - test/run-modes-session.test.ts
  always-rules:
    description: >-
      #always-rules (spec §3.4 S-L1, S25-C2; gui-complete item 2) in
      src/kernel/supervisor/rules.ts. Permission cards offer allow | deny |
      always; `always` is fresh presence EVERY time (classes.ts, G-BRK-05)
      and needs the card's ruleScope {repoKey} (else bad-params). The broker
      logs approval.rule.learned{cardId, tool, matchExact, presence,
      repoKey}. RuleBook folds KERNEL-signed rows only (kernel principal +
      kernel key): approval.request (permission card, tool, tool_input) +
      approval.decide{always} + approval.rule.learned (presence != cached,
      same tool) => a rule keyed (repoKey, tool, inputHash of the canonical
      tool_input); forged rows of any kind are ignored. The supervisor answers
      an EXACT match allow with no card after broker.auto =>
      approval.auto{hsid, tool, inputHash, modeRef:'rule:<cardId>'}; a
      different input, tool or repo raises the card. updatedInput is the
      kernel-held input; never updatedPermissions. Bounded (MAX_RULES 1024,
      MAX_TRACKED_REQUESTS 4096; dropping fails closed). The daemon folds via catchUp over ruleSourceOf(log) (Aegis C2: lastSeq advances to the captured head, past every scanned row; Aegis H1: that head is the RELEASED head, never the committed tip, so rows hidden behind a critical row pending on its anchors are folded once released, never skipped; repoKey is repo-hooks.ts repoKeyOf, pinned in a test; a failed approval.auto falls through to the card)
      incrementally from the chain (fromSeq). The learned row matches EXACT
      only (TD-2026-10-19-528 F2: matchExact:true or match:'exact'; any other
      match value forms no rule). Revoke (P8-D98, F1): a KERNEL-signed
      approval.rule.revoked{ruleId, repoKey, tool, connId} removes the rule and
      forgets the card's always decision (a replayed learned row never
      resurrects it); a forged revoked row is ignored; a fresh always on a new
      card learns a new rule. get(ruleId) serves #rules-revoke. Not built: the
      strictness L1 suspension of rules (M2.10).
      PREFIX RULES (TD-2026-10-20-294 part 3, F2 amended): a learned row
      {match:'prefix', prefix:[1..3 words]} forms a rule ONLY when the
      kernel-signed request is a Bash card whose command is one literal simple
      command (#shell-readonly simpleArgv) starting with the prefix AND the
      kernel-signed approval.decide{always} carries the same
      scope{kind:'prefix', prefix}; an exact learned row after a scoped decision
      (or vice versa) forms nothing. match(repoKey, tool, hash, input) answers a
      prefix rule only for a Bash input whose command is again one literal
      simple command (no ; && || | & newline, substitution, parameter, glob,
      tilde, redirection or assignment) whose argv starts with the prefix word
      for word, same repoKey and tool, input keys within command/description/
      timeout. A prefix rule widens only within a single literal simple
      command. gnomon rules list shows match + prefix. Aegis H1/M2/M3: the
      fold forms no rule from a prefix #prefix-policy refuses, and
      match(..., ctx{prefixRules, roots}) answers a prefix rule only with a
      context (none => no prefix match), never in manual mode
      (prefixRules:false; exact rules still match) and only when
      #prefix-policy passes the argv against the session roots.
      RC5 (TD-2026-10-20-086): an exact Bash rule also matches by
      bashCommandHash = sha256 over canonical {command} (no description or
      timeout), folded from the kernel-signed request (so older rules match
      too), when both the learned and the later input have only command,
      description, timeout keys; run_in_background or any other key on either
      side leaves only the whole-input hash.
    path: src/kernel/supervisor/rules.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, approvals, paradigm-8, move-1]
    tests:
      - test/always-rules.test.ts
      - test/always-prefix-rules.test.ts
      - test/approval-prefix-scope.test.ts
      - test/rules-bash-hash.test.ts
      - test/permission-session.test.ts
    oracles: [G-BRK-05]

  prefix-policy:
    description: >-
      #prefix-policy (TD-2026-10-20-294, Aegis H1/M1/M3) in
      src/kernel/supervisor/prefix-policy.ts. Which prefixes may be a
      prefix-scoped always rule, enforced at the card offer (permission.ts),
      the broker accept (approve scope, bad-params before presence), the rule
      fold and every match. Default deny: 2-3 printable-ASCII words, never
      one; argv0 no path, never a shell/interpreter/runner; NO package
      manager or build tool is ever a prefix rule (BUILD_TOOL_PREFIX_ALLOW is
      empty after the second Aegis BLOCK: yarnPath, rustc-wrapper, go env -w,
      .pnpmfile and npm view --registry make every subcommand code or
      exfiltration; an entry needs a malicious-config falsifier;
      ALLOW_NPM_TEST_PREFIX default off); git exactly two words with a
      read-ish subcommand. prefixMatchRefusal re-checks at match time:
      gitPrefixOptBad models git's option parsing (a --name that prefixes any
      GIT_REFUSED_LONG option, so abbreviations like --outp/--ext-d/--open;
      any short bundle containing O c C p u; plus shell-readonly gitOptBad),
      non-listing git branch args, -C-style directory/config options, paths
      that leave the session roots (.., ~, \, out-of-root absolute), and
      gate-class paths (layers gateClassPathRule: .git/**, .claude/**,
      package.json ...). shownPrefixWords renders each word quoted with
      non-ASCII escaped \uXXXX for the presence prompt (M1; the GUI mirrors
      it). Residual: Claude's persistent Bash cwd is unknown to the kernel;
      git trusts repo config written before the rule or by a carded command.
    path: src/kernel/supervisor/prefix-policy.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, approvals, security, paradigm-8, move-1]
    tests:
      - test/prefix-policy.test.ts
      - test/approval-prefix-scope.test.ts
      - test/always-prefix-rules.test.ts
      - test/permission-session.test.ts

  loop-schema:
    description: >-
      #loop-schema (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §2, §3.1, §3.4, §3.5; build
      plan LP1) in src/loops/schema.ts, exported as @a-company/gnomon/loops.
      The loop:v2 definition: types, validateLoop (precise {path, code,
      message} issues) and compileLoop to the canonical JSON the kernel reads
      (.paradigm/loops/<id>.loop.json; the kernel never parses YAML, zero
      runtime deps). Rules: closed shape; kebab ids <= 64; every edge target a
      node or terminal (@goal/@stop/@pause); goal names a check node, a run
      goal declares goal.inputs, only check/call nodes reach @goal (LL2);
      argv literal (no {{), argv0 never in the kernel INTERPRETER_DENYLIST
      (imported from #prefix-policy) or python*, never .cmd/.bat; judge and
      summarize nodes have no tools; continuous needs cooldown >= 60 s
      (MIN_AUTOMATION_PERIOD_MS), a goal or run.rounds, and day.runs or
      day.computeSec; call is id@hash8, cycles and depth > 3 refused given
      the known loops. 8.0 subset: model.use claude|any, escalate other than
      never, the claude opt-in block, cron, sentinel.incident.opened, bash
      tools and envelope fields are refused LANDS_IN_8_1 ("lands in 8.1").
      Compile fills every default (absent budget field = global default,
      never unbounded), durations in seconds, edges expanded per verdict,
      flows resolved to path globs from a flow index (else symbolic with a
      FLOWS_UNRESOLVED warning); canonicalLoopJson is the sorted-key hashing
      form, loopFileJson the indented file form. Aegis LP1 review: @goal only
      from goal.node or a call, and any @goal edge needs a goal block; a call
      pin must equal loopContentHash(callee) (sha256 of canonicalJson of the
      parsed definition, never file bytes) and a call reaching @goal needs a
      callee goal; argv0 never a BUILD_TOOLS entry except tsc/vitest/jest
      (LOOP_ARGV0_CHECKER_ALLOW); programName strips trailing dots/spaces,
      .exe and .com; land paths never under node_modules; NaN/Infinity
      refused; compiled `protected` = node_modules + goal.inputs + repo
      argv words across the call chain; `argv0Interpreter` recorded via
      readShebang. The shared INTERPRETER_DENYLIST gained cmd, powershell,
      pwsh, wscript, cscript, mshta, arch, script, stdbuf, setsid, chroot,
      sandbox-exec and busybox; loop-choice joined NEVER_STANDING_KINDS.
      LP2: compiled.sourceHash = loopContentHash(the parsed YAML), the hash a
      caller's pin names; src/loops/compiled.ts validateCompiledLoop is the
      kernel's re-validation of a .loop.json (decompile, validateLoop, compile
      again with the file's own budgets/flowPaths/interpreters, byte compare;
      protected and callsResolved are the kernel's own).
    path: src/loops/schema.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, paradigm-8, security]
    tests:
      - test/loop-schema.test.ts

  loop-registry:
    description: >-
      #loop-registry (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §3.1, §4, §5; build plan LP2)
      in src/kernel/loops/{registry,hash,trust,wire}.ts and src/cli/loop-cli.ts.
      Registry: watches compiled .paradigm/loops/*.loop.json in known repos
      (from kernel-signed session.open{cwd}, live sessions, or a human loop
      request naming the repo) and <home>/loops/*.loop.json (personal; refused
      when a symlink, group/world-writable or another user's); lstat on every
      dir and file, symlinks refused, size-capped; every file re-validated
      (validateCompiledLoop), never trusted. Content hash (hash.ts) = sha256
      of canonicalJson {def: sha256(canonicalJson(parsed file)), argv: per run
      check argv0 as resolveArgv0 finds it (path word vs repo root, bare name
      in <repo>/node_modules/.bin then the session PATH) with realpath+sha256
      and its shebang interpreter (`/usr/bin/env node` = the runner's node,
      process.execPath), every file an argv word names in or out of the repo,
      calls: each pin + the callee's CURRENT hash, closure: digest of the
      gate-class files (manifest/layers.ts gateClassPathRule file/test/code/env
      rules + tsconfig*) in the repo root and on the way to each argv path};
      coreHash omits the closure. The kernel's protected set adds callees' and
      closure paths. State is a fold of kernel-signed loop.trusted|untrusted|
      closure.changed|paused|resumed|deleted rows keyed {loopId, scope,
      repoKey}: untrusted, trusted, waiting-trust, paused, deleted, invalid. A
      trusted loop whose hash moves gets ONE loop.paused{content-changed}; a
      closure-only move asks the origin (gitClosureOrigin: uncommitted or
      Gnomon-Session/Loop-Authored-By = agent, Gnomon-Loop or
      @gnomon.invalid = loop, else human): loop/agent write
      loop.closure.changed{by} + loop.paused{closure-changed}, a human commit
      only loop.closure.changed{by:human} and the trust moves to the new hash.
      resolveCall(): callee in scope, TRUSTED at its current hash, pin =
      its trusted sourceHash. Trust (trust.ts): request.loop.trust{loopId,
      scope, repo|repoKey, contentHash?} raises ONE loop-trust card (root
      class, handOnly, never always/standing) rendering every node, argv0
      realpath+sha and interpreter, argv files, tools, model, triggers,
      budgets, goal/protected inputs, land, call pins, closure, and a line
      diff vs the trusted version's snapshot (<home>/loop-trust/<hex>.json);
      allow re-checks the hash under beginRootDecision and signs ONE
      loop.trusted{loopId, scope, repoKey, contentHash, coreHash,
      closureDigest, sourceHash, cardId, by} row with the root key in the same
      touch (decision-batch.ts and Protocol.swift row table `loop-trust`); the
      effect appends it as a kernel row with rootSig. request.loop.untrust is a
      tightening (no presence, loop.untrusted{by}); request.loop.delete writes
      loop.deleted (a personal file is removed; repo files stay);
      pause/resume/run are #loop-runner's (wire.ts routes them). Wiring: one wireLoops()
      in daemon.ts, one request.loop.* block in dispatch.ts, status.loops for
      the human. CLI: gnomon loop list|show|trust|untrust|trust-status
      (run|pause|resume|runs|logs are #loop-runner's). trust.ts exports
      parseLoopTarget, the one request.loop.* target parser.
    path: src/kernel/loops/registry.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, paradigm-8, security]
    gates: [^loop-trusted]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-registry.test.ts
      - test/loop-compiled.test.ts
      - test/loop-cli.test.ts
      - test/decision-batch.test.ts

  loop-check:
    description: >-
      #loop-check (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §2 check nodes, §4 Sandboxes and
      Evidence, §7; build plan step 4; TD-2026-10-22-129: the loops' own
      read-only profile in place of M2.3's gnomon-oracle, G:1596). Profile:
      src/kernel/sandbox/loop-check.ts (LOOP_CHECK_PROFILE, registry
      LOOP_CHECK_BACKENDS = {darwin}; win32 is absent for W5 and, like linux,
      refuses sandbox-unavailable; darwinFloor macOS 15+ and SIP;
      assertNoProtectedGrant against $HOME, the Gnomon home, keys/ and
      LOOP_CHECK_CREDENTIAL_STORES) and src/platform/darwin/loop-check-sbpl.ts
      (default-deny: exec literals of the kernel node, the hashed argv0 and its
      shebang interpreter only, fork allowed; read the run dir, the linked
      node_modules (write-denied), the Node runtime and the pinned paradigm CLI
      package; write the run dir only; NO network rule; the Gnomon home and
      credential stores read/write-denied last). Executor:
      src/kernel/loops/check-exec.ts runCheck. git ls-files -co
      --exclude-standard (core.fsmonitor off) copied into the KERNEL-OWNED
      run dir <home>/loop-work/check-<runId>/{repo,home,tmp} (0700, under the
      Gnomon home, never inside the repo; the profile denies the home and
      re-allows only the run dir after it). Every parent lstat'd (no links),
      files read through O_NOFOLLOW fds for at most their lstat size, then
      the parent chain (dev:ino), file identity and realpath containment
      re-checked: a swap refuses the whole copy (Aegis step-4 review). Caps
      50 000 files, half a gigabyte, 500 node_modules links (copy-too-large);
      node_modules linked read-only only when its realpath is inside the repo
      (else copy-failed); env from runner/env.ts buildCheckEnv (G-SEC-02);
      argv exec'd directly in its own process group, timeoutSec then SIGTERM,
      SIGKILL after 2 s and a group SIGKILL after every exit, all through
      process-control; the tree is polled (a snapshot every half second) and descendants
      that left the group (setsid) are SIGKILLed by pid + start witness
      (escapedKilled). argv0 (copy-mapped inside the repo) re-hashed against
      its pin; a script argv0 needs a pinned, unchanged interpreter
      (src/kernel/loops/shebang.ts, the one shebang parser shared with the
      registry hash); the paradigm CLI pin is a digest of its whole dist dir
      (pinParadigmCli, dirDigest). Builtins run as node <paradigm cli> loop
      builtin <name> --json and need a JSON verdict for that builtin: exit 2
      or no valid verdict is error builtin-error. A json rule's value is
      bounded (256 chars) and secret-scanned. Residual: node_modules
      dependencies of checks are not pinned (8.1: pin or ship builtins in
      Gnomon). Verdict: check-parse.ts pass rule (exit; tap, junit, tsc
      with failed as the allowed maximum; json JSONPath with == != < <= > >=);
      crash, timeout, spawn failure, sandbox refusal, unparseable report or
      output past 8 MiB is error. Evidence: stdout and stderr tails of 64 KiB
      through secret-scan.ts (AWS keys and secrets, GitHub, GitLab, npm,
      Stripe, Slack, Anthropic, OpenAI and Google tokens, JWTs, URL passwords, PEM private keys incl. cut blocks, bearer and
      generic api_key/secret/token/password assignments), labelled private and
      sensitive, envKeys names only. The run dir is removed after pass/fail,
      kept on error with a retention note; sweepCheckCopies removes kept dirs
      past the window. CheckExecInput.abort (#loop-runner's pause and kill
      switch) SIGKILLs the process group at once (then the setsid sweep): the
      check is error `killed`. Falsifiers: G-SBX-07, G-SBX-04..06 and G-SEC-02 against
      this profile (test/loop-check-sandbox.test.ts, darwin); parsers,
      scanner, env, registry and copy everywhere (test/loop-check.test.ts).
    path: src/kernel/loops/check-exec.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, sandbox, paradigm-8, security]
    tests:
      - test/loop-check.test.ts
      - test/loop-check-sandbox.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/check-exec.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/check-parse.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/secret-scan.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/shebang.ts
      - src/kernel/sandbox/loop-check.ts
      - src/platform/darwin/loop-check-sbpl.ts

  loop-git:
    description: >-
      #loop-git (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §3.2 where loops work, §3.3 kill
      switch, §4 retention; build plan step 10, LP6a; G-GIT-01).
      src/kernel/git/scratch.ts. The scratch worktree for a run with act
      nodes is the HEAD tree, detached, written into the KERNEL-OWNED run dir
      <home>/loop-work/act-<runId>/tree (0700, never inside the repo, no .git)
      from hardened git plumbing only (rev-parse, ls-tree -r -z -l, cat-file
      --batch: raw blobs, no filters, no textconv, no checkout, no hook;
      HARDENED_GIT_FLAGS --no-optional-locks, core.hooksPath=/dev/null,
      core.fsmonitor=false, core.sshCommand=false, protocol.allow=never,
      credential.helper empty; hardenedGitEnv: a clean env with no inherited
      GIT_*, GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null, GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM, GIT_ATTR_NOSYSTEM).
      Not `git worktree add`: that writes the human's .git and runs a checkout
      with the repo's filters and hooks. Files O_CREAT|O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW,
      links last, node_modules linked only when their realpath is inside the
      repo; caps 50 000 files and 500 MiB. liveScratch(runId) is the module
      table LocalLane.openLoop checks (a forged path is never a scratch).
      scratchChanges diffs in-kernel by git blob id against the HEAD
      manifest. parkScratch writes the changed blobs (hash-object -w
      --no-filters), a tree through a private GIT_INDEX_FILE and a commit
      (Gnomon Loop <loop+<id>@gnomon.invalid>, trailers Gnomon-Loop and
      Gnomon-Run after the stripped message) into a kernel-owned bare park
      repo <home>/loop-work/park/<repoHash>.git whose objects borrow the
      human's through alternates, at refs/gnomon/loops/<id>/<runId>; the
      human repo gets no ref and no object until #loop-land moves them in
      under the land lock (src/kernel/git/land.ts). diff-tree -p
      --no-renames --no-ext-diff --no-textconv for the card. removeScratch at
      run end; sweepLoopScratch (boot, LOOP_RETENTION_DAYS) removes stale run
      dirs and parked refs past the window, never a live scratch. Aegis
      review: case- and NFD-colliding trees refused, parents created one
      component at a time and realpath-checked before every write or link;
      reads O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK with fstat (regular, nlink 1, capped), FIFOs
      refused, park re-checks each blob against the scanned oid (PARK_LIMITS);
      git resolved once to an absolute path, at least 2.39.4,
      GIT_ALLOW_PROTOCOL=none, GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES, the uid check in place
      of safe.directory, no gpg signing; names with control, format or bidi
      characters refused. Residual: hash-object through alternates freshens
      the mtime of an object the human store already has.
    path: src/kernel/git/scratch.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, git, paradigm-8, security]
    tests:
      - test/loop-act-e2e.test.ts
      - test/loop-act.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/git/scratch.ts
      - src/kernel/git/index.ts

  loop-land:
    description: >-
      #loop-land (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §3.2 Landing, Conflicts,
      Commits, Revert signals, §3.3 effective autonomy at land, LL4, threat
      37; build plan step 11, LP6b; G-LOOP-07, G-LOOP-08, G-LOOP-09,
      G-LOOP-15, G-LOOP-24, G-LOOP-25). Mechanics in src/kernel/git/land.ts
      (landCommit): the per-repo in-process land lock (withLandLock) plus the
      human's own <gitDir>/index.lock taken O_CREAT|O_EXCL; refusals that land
      nothing: merge/rebase/cherry-pick/revert/bisect in progress, index.lock,
      a human turn in flight (waitable), HEAD detached or on another branch
      (symbolic-ref), the branch moved since the run's base, a rename, a
      gitlink at or above a path, a .git component, unsafe names, case and
      NFD collisions with any index path, unmerged, assume-unchanged,
      skip-worktree or staged entries, an untracked or ignored add, any
      .gitattributes attribute, core.autocrlf/eol/splitIndex/sparseCheckout,
      and a worktree entry whose bytes or exec bit differ from the base blob.
      The land commit is re-made in the park repo with Gnomon Loop
      <loop+<id>@gnomon.invalid> and the trailers On-Behalf-Of, Trusted-By,
      Gnomon-Loop, Gnomon-Run, Loop-Authored-By (and Symbols) last
      (landMessage strips trailer-shaped lines); its objects are re-hashed in
      the kernel and written with hash-object -w, then rev-list --objects
      --missing=error. loop.landed is written (beforeLand) BEFORE any visible
      change. Files: DirGuard (every parent lstat'd a real dir, realpath
      exact, the dir inode held open and re-compared), the original moved
      aside and compared to the base blob again, the new bytes in a temp file
      (O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW, old permission bits kept, exec only per the blob
      mode) linked in with link() (never replaces); any mismatch rolls every
      placed file back. Then update-ref <ref> <new> <base> (CAS), HEAD
      re-checked, and the index copy (update-index --index-info on a private
      GIT_INDEX_FILE) written through the held lock. findRevert finds a later
      commit that restores the land's blobs (content) or names it (message).
      Orchestration in src/kernel/loops/land.ts (LoopLander): auto lands
      re-gated each attempt (trusted at the run's hash, runnable, not held,
      landDecision with the cap re-read, else the runner's propose card);
      approved lands need the trust only; L3/manual wait. Waitable refusals
      write loop.land.waiting once per reason and retry (timer and nudge on
      turn.result/session.exited) until land.maxWaitMin, then loop.parked
      {max-wait} and a toast; structural ones park at once; an undone land
      writes loop.land.waiting{undone, sha}. scanReverts writes ONE
      merge.reverted{landedRef, revertSha, detectedBy} per reverted loop
      commit, from one log --raw read (strictness scoring of it is 8.1, and
      message-only reverts must not drive it). Aegis review fixes: the new
      index is written fully into the held lock and verified (size, read
      back, trailer checksum or skipHash zeros) BEFORE the CAS, so only the
      rename follows it and a failed rename undoes the land (H1); the private
      index copy keeps the original's mtime rounded down (racy git, H2); a
      split index, an unreadable or crlf/attributes-setting global, XDG or
      system config refuse (the read-only queries see the human's HOME, M2);
      the kill switch parks every waiting land (parkAll, M3); a second
      loop.landed{phase:'done'} closes each land and the boot reconcile closes
      interrupted ones (stale index.lock, leftovers to
      <home>/loop-work/recovered/<runId>/, a toast) and parks lands waiting
      at the restart (M4); in-progress re-checked under the lock, the undo CAS
      checked (a conflict leaves the files), changed originals kept in
      recovered/, a branch checked out in another worktree refused, local
      attended sessions counted (L1-L7). 8.1: placement with openat/renameat
      in the helper (M5).
    path: src/kernel/loops/land.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, git, paradigm-8, security]
    tests:
      - test/loop-land.test.ts
      - test/loop-land-git.test.ts
      - test/loop-act-e2e.test.ts
      - test/loop-events.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/land.ts
      - src/kernel/git/land.ts

  loop-act:
    description: >-
      #loop-act (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §2 act role and tools, §3.3
      effective autonomy, §3.4 the clamp and protected goal inputs, §4
      Sandboxes; build plan step 10, LP6a; G-LOOP-22, the card half of
      G-LOOP-23). Act nodes (model role act, tools read and edit; bash is
      refused at validation until 8.1) run through LocalLane.openLoop(ref,
      {role:'act', act}) with the registry run reference (Aegis step 8: abort
      checks, live() re-checks, fenced inputs, closed {verdict, confidence,
      summary}), in the run's live scratch (the cwd and the ONE root allowed
      inside the Gnomon home), under the `loop-act` profile
      (src/kernel/sandbox/loop-act.ts, LOOP_ACT_BACKENDS = {darwin}; win32 and
      linux refuse sandbox-unavailable; src/platform/darwin/loop-act-sbpl.ts:
      the session SBPL plus a tail denying every connect but the loopback
      port of the loops:true endpoint, every exec but the runner node, every
      write but the scratch and its tmp, reads of the human repo but its
      node_modules, the Gnomon home but the run dir, keys and credential
      stores last). Tools come only from the trust card through the loop
      clamp (loop-clamp.ts loopActTools: loop-trust:<cardId>#tool-read|edit
      via loopClampAllows; no card, no tools, FORBIDDEN). The child
      (gnomon-local protect.ts) refuses a write to the protected set (the
      registry's compiled protected list: goal.inputs, the config closure,
      argv-named paths, callees'; plus ACT_ALWAYS_PROTECTED node_modules,
      .paradigm/loops, .gnomon) with a protected-input result and no card;
      the kernel reads it (LoopTurn.protectedHits) and the node fails
      (G-LOOP-22). Checks in an act run copy the scratch (CheckExecInput
      scratch). Propose (src/kernel/loops/propose.ts): after the run the
      scratch diff is parked (loop.parked) and becomes ONE verdict card
      (detail.origin loop, the kernel line "Raised by loop X (claimed by Y |
      from the loop file)", files, goal result, reasons, the diff) and
      loop.proposed; land hazards (a protected path, a tracked deletion, an
      added @ts-nocheck|@ts-ignore|eslint-disable|.skip|.only|xit, an
      unscanned diff, a symlink) always propose; 8.0 is APPROVE-TO-LAND
      (founder decision): autoLandBlockers returns "auto-land lands in 8.1"
      while LOOP_AUTO_LAND is false, so every change raises the card (the
      8.1 auto rules stay behind that seam: goal reached on a check, a land
      block covering every path, no decide before-land, no gate-class path,
      not held); the trust card of an act loop with a land block says
      "Proposes changes to your current branch; nothing lands until you
      accept the card."; L2 caps act to propose; observe edits nothing. The card is hand-only: accept lands it
      through #loop-land, reject drops the ref. A run stopped from outside
      parks and raises no card. Aegis review: the
      tail also denies fork, signals beyond itself, executable mapping of
      the scratch and tmp, deleting or renaming them, and reads of the git common
      dir and sibling worktrees; it re-denies the kernel's loopback ports
      after the endpoint allow (an endpoint on one is refused). The diff is
      parsed by structure; card text is escaped visibly; an
      execution-surface hazard class (hooks, CI, editor tasks, .envrc,
      .gitattributes, .gitmodules, package.json) always proposes. The runner
      awaits model.settled(runId) before parking. Escape suite:
      test/loop-act-sandbox.test.ts (darwin).
    path: src/kernel/loops/propose.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, sandbox, paradigm-8, security]
    tests:
      - test/loop-act.test.ts
      - test/loop-act-e2e.test.ts
      - test/loop-act-sandbox.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/propose.ts
      - src/kernel/sandbox/loop-act.ts
      - src/platform/darwin/loop-act-sbpl.ts
      - src/kernel/supervisor/loop-clamp.ts
      - src/kernel/supervisor/local-session.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/model-node.ts

  loop-runner:
    description: >-
      #loop-runner (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §2 graph semantics, §3.3 kill
      switch and resume classes, §3.5 budgets, §4 rows, restart, concurrency;
      build plan step 5, LP3) in src/kernel/loops/{runner,ledger}.ts, wired
      only through kernel/loops/wire.ts. Walks a TRUSTED loop's compiled
      graph from `start` (registry.verify + canRun before the run and every
      node; a hash, closure, trust or pause change ends the run before its
      next node). check nodes run through check-exec runCheck with argv0 and
      its interpreter pinned from the trusted hash and the timeout cut to the
      run's remaining budget; notify `as: toast` writes loop.notified{as:
      toast, text} (kernel-field template: loop.id, run.id, round,
      failed.node, last.node, last.outcome; unknown fields render ?) and
      hands the toast to the optional GUI port; notify `as: card` raises a
      loop-choice card (continue/stop are the edge keys; no answer by the TTL
      takes `timeout`); call nodes check registry.resolveCall (trusted pin,
      depth ≤ 3) and run the callee as a child run whose meter is chained to
      the caller's (goal → pass, budget → unsure, else fail); model nodes
      (step 8, below); act nodes (model role act) run in a scratch
      worktree and end in a propose card since step 10 (#loop-act, #loop-git;
      a called loop with act nodes is refused); write checks (effect: write)
      are still refused at run start; effective
      autonomy = the loop's until step 9. Terminals @goal/@stop/@pause (a
      missing edge is @stop; @pause writes a kernel loop.paused{reason:
      timeout|unavailable|node-exit}); exit.maxVisits/maxFailStreak end the
      run node-exit. Budgets (LL3) checked before every step: run.rounds (a
      round closes when the walk revisits a node), run.wallMin,
      run.computeSec, day.runs and day.computeSec per loop, global dayRuns
      and dayComputeSec (LOOP_GLOBAL_CAPS); absent fields are the global
      defaults (effectiveBudgets). Rows (kernel principal): loop.run.started
      {runId, loopId, scope, repoKey, contentHash, trigger, budgets,
      parentRunId?}, loop.node{runId, nodeId, type, visit, round, verdict,
      edge, next, durationMs, evidenceRef, evidence (sensitive by the kind's
      label), effectiveAutonomy}, loop.round, loop.notified, loop.run.ended
      {reason, terminal?, rounds, computeMs}. The ledger (ledger.ts) folds
      them back from the log (LL6): open runs, recent runs, per-day counts
      from chain time; replayLoopRuns reproduces every edge and `gnomon
      replay` (log/replay.ts) folds it into projection.loops with a
      loop-edge-mismatch divergence (G-LOOP-17). Restart: recover() at boot
      closes every open run daemon-restart, nothing resumes mid-step
      (G-LOOP-14). Concurrency: one run per loop (a trigger during a run sets
      ONE rerun flag), caps.repo (2) and caps.global per repo and overall,
      more queue FIFO. Requests (human, gui or device): request.loop.run,
      request.loop.pause (a tightening: loop.paused{human}, the run aborted),
      request.loop.pause{all:true} (the kill switch: every run aborted, check
      process groups killed, one loop.paused{kill-switch} per trusted loop,
      no presence), request.loop.resume (after a human pause at once; after a
      kernel pause a hand-only loop-choice card whose continue, with fresh
      presence, writes loop.resumed{cardId}; content or closure changes need
      a new trust; Aegis M3). status.loops adds running, queued, caps and
      recent runs. CLI: gnomon loop run|pause [--all]|resume|runs|logs.
      Aegis step-5 fixes: the ledger's per-day, per-key counters are their
      own fold (record eviction never touches them) and a callee's compute
      is charged to the root caller's key (loop.run.started.rootKey), whose
      day.computeSec gates the chain; card nodes: timeout → @pause and stop
      → a terminal (schema CARD_EDGE and the runner), a card that cannot be
      raised ends the run @stop; the kill switch sets an in-memory flag and
      holds every paused key synchronously (start refuses until resumed); a
      run-budget stop writes loop.paused{budget} and drops the rerun flag;
      untrust and delete stop the run (trust.ts onRevoke); the per-node
      trust gate walks the caller chain; recover() skips live run ids; daemon
      exit aborts runs (no check outlives the kernel). The daemon pins the
      paradigm CLI for builtins once (wire.ts paradigmCliResolver: Node
      resolution of @a-company/paradigm from Gnomon, dist digest); not found,
      builtins fail closed.
      Step 8 (LP5, local model nodes; gnomon-loops.md §2, §3.4-§3.6) in
      src/kernel/loops/model-node.ts: judge and summarize steps open an
      UNATTENDED local session through the `model` port
      (supervisor.openLoop → LocalLane.openLoop), the one unattended open,
      reachable only with a run reference the registry mints for a TRUSTED,
      runnable loop (registry.runRef; module-private WeakMap,
      loopRunRefInfo; forged or released ⇒ FORBIDDEN before any row). The
      session: session.open{attended:false, origin:'loop', loopRun},
      surface 'none' (no tools), one step per turn, no GNOMON_SOCK /
      SESSION_KEY / HSID in its env (LL5: no paradigm_loop write op, no
      loop.request), its model text only hashed in turn.event. Prompt: the
      trusted template (kernel fields only), then each input as a fenced,
      receipted DATA block with a per-prompt nonce (inputs: a node's
      evidence on this run, last/failed evidence, loop/run fields, the
      trigger's kernel-stamped fields TRIGGER_FIELDS); the receipt rides
      session.inject{origin:'kernel', channel:'loop'} and loop.node
      evidence.inputs. Verdict: exactly one JSON object {verdict,
      confidence, summary?} from the closed enum (a judge's classes, first
      class = pass, others fail; summarize pass|fail); otherwise ONE repair
      turn, then `unsure`; confidence < minConfidence ⇒ unsure; nothing is
      searched for in prose (G-LOOP-06). Free text is secret-scanned and
      kept as the node's sensitive evidence (reply, summary). Availability:
      none configured, profile unmatched, endpoint down or incapable, no
      runner, a failed call or a timeout ⇒ LoopModelUnavailable, 3 retries
      with backoff, then the unavailable edge (compiled default @pause,
      loop.paused{unavailable}, a toast); never a fallback card, never
      Claude (G-LCL-02, G-LOOP-12 N/A in 8.0, G-LOOP-13). A model loop with
      no configured local model is refused at start (no-local-model).
      Budgets: each step's spawn permit has scope.loop = the root caller's
      key; the ledger folds permit.grant + budget.usage tokens by permitId
      into its per-day counters (day.tokens, global dayTokens); the run
      meter holds run.tokens; a node's limits.tokens caps the reply and an
      overrun is unsure. Thrash (k=3): in a loop with model or write steps,
      the same check-evidence hash three rounds in a row ends the run
      `thrash` with loop.paused{thrash} and a toast; resuming takes the
      hand-only loop-choice card. ^loop-origin-clamp
      (supervisor/loop-clamp.ts): in loop sessions only the loop's own
      trust rules auto-pass (none in 8.0), cards carry detail.origin 'loop'
      and the line "Raised by loop X (authored by Y)" (authoredBy is `file`
      until step 12's request rows); loop-choice cards carry it too.
      Aegis step-8 review (applied): no public mint (the registry issues
      its run-reference minter ONCE, taken by the runner, which vouches:
      live run, judge/summarize node, the walk's real root); live() also
      fails on the kill-switch hold and a strictness pause; openLoop closes
      the session and throws if the run aborted or stopped being live
      during the spawn, and nothing is asked once the signal is aborted;
      the supervisor permission path routes a loop session's mode,
      read-only and rule/always passes through loopClampAllows (exact
      `loop-trust:<cardId>[#rule]`) and its cards through withLoopOrigin
      (unreachable in 8.0; a source test fails if a pass skips mayAuto);
      prompts are budgeted before building (≤ 8 inputs, END markers and the
      answer line intact, receipts hash what is sent); verdict reasons never
      echo model text; the thrash streak carries across runs
      (loop.round{evidenceHash} folded in the ledger, reset by a thrash
      end); only endpoints marked `loops: true` in the root-signed local
      config serve loops (the runner refuses at start otherwise).
    path: src/kernel/loops/runner.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, paradigm-8, security]
    gates: [^loop-trusted, ^loop-origin-clamp]
    flows: [$loop-run]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-runner.test.ts
      - test/loop-runner-darwin.test.ts
      - test/loop-model.test.ts
      - test/loop-model-e2e.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/runner.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/model-node.ts
      - src/kernel/supervisor/loop-clamp.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/ledger.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/wire.ts
      - src/cli/loop-cli.ts

  loop-timers:
    description: >-
      #loop-timers (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §4 scheduler integration and
      uptime hold, §10 D1; build plan step 6, LP4a) in
      src/kernel/loops/timers.ts, built by wire.ts when the daemon passes its
      scheduler. Reconciles on every registry change (scan, folded trust row)
      and runner event: each schedule trigger of a RUNNABLE loop (trusted at
      its current hash, unpaused, not held by the kill switch) has one
      scheduler timer (loop:<loopKey>#<digest>), every other loop none, so a
      fresh install has zero loop timers (G-LAW-05b). daily/weekly times are
      read in the daemon's zone (the host's IANA zone; the loop:v2 schema
      states no tz yet); every is anchored at the loop's trust or resume
      instant (registry.runnableSince), and fires at or before it are never
      owed. Continuous: the next run is a one-shot timer at end + cooldown
      (≥ 60 s), at boot, trust or resume now + cooldown; a paused loop (a
      budget stop pauses), a killed one or a refused start (no next run that
      local day) gets none. Kill switch: the runner's kill event drops every
      loop timer synchronously before the pause rows (G-LOOP-26). Uptime
      (D1): while any loop timer has a next due instant or a run is in
      flight, the `loop` idle holder keeps the daemon up; status.loops.uptime
      {enabled, running, waiting: {n, of}, keepingAlive, line: "Gnomon is
      staying up for loops", nextDueAt}. The wrapper's window close already
      hides the window and keeps the kernel (gnomon-wrapper main.rs
      CloseRequested); only Cmd-Q stops it.
    path: src/kernel/loops/timers.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, scheduler, paradigm-8]
    gates: [^loop-trusted]
    flows: [$loop-run]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-timers.test.ts
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/timers.ts
      - src/kernel/scheduler/zoned.ts
      - src/kernel/scheduler/scheduler.ts
      - src/kernel/lifecycle/idle.ts

  loop-events:
    description: >-
      #loop-events (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §2 triggers, §4 #loop-events;
      build plan step 7, LP4b) in src/kernel/loops/events.ts, built by
      wire.ts (one marked block). Event triggers for RUNNABLE loops only
      (trusted at the current hash, unpaused, not held by the kill switch),
      reconciled on every registry change and runner event like
      #loop-timers: an untrusted, paused or killed loop has no watch and no
      subscription. file.changed: one recursive fs.watch per repo (realpath
      root), matched against the compiled flow globs (trigger.paths or
      flowPaths); .git, node_modules and the Gnomon home (check copies in
      loop-work) ignored; a path whose realpath leaves the repo is refused
      (loops.event-refused symlink-escape). commit.landed: a watch on the git
      dir (HEAD, refs/heads, packed-refs; worktree gitdir/commondir), parsed
      directly (the kernel runs no git); a new sha on the same branch fires,
      a branch switch is a new baseline. session.ended: kernel
      session.exited rows whose session.open cwd is inside the loop's repo.
      card.decided: kernel approval.decide rows for a card a run of {loop,
      node} raised (loop.notified), same repo. Matching reads kernel-stamped
      fields only. Debounce is trailing per (loop, trigger), at least 250 ms
      for watch events, capped at 3x the debounce. Each fire hands off to
      runner.start FIRST (trust, kill, strictness, day caps) with kernel ids
      only {type:'event', kind, fireId, count, sha|hsid|cardId} (never path or
      ref, which could reach a model prompt); only a run that starts or
      queues writes loop.trigger.fired{loopId, scope, repoKey, trigger{...,
      path|ref}, eventId?}; a coalesced or refused fire is a per-loop counter
      (dropped()), not a row. Replay (G-LOOP-17): ledger.ts replayLoopRuns
      joins each run to its row by fireId over the whole history (`why`); a
      run citing an unknown or foreign fireId diverges. Git dirs are
      validated as git links them (worktree gitdir back-link, commondir);
      only HEAD, packed-refs and refs/heads are watched. Self-trigger guard
      (G-LOOP-15): an event whose causes chain (runId, cardId, loop session
      hsid, causes refs ev:/card:/hsid: transitively, parentRunId callers)
      includes a run of L never triggers L; a commit is a loop's own only
      when a kernel loop.landed{sha} row names it (land writes it before
      moving the ref);
      a file change in a loop's land.paths while it runs, or in a
      loop.landed{paths} row for 60 s, is its own. sentinel.incident.opened
      is refused at validation (8.1).
    path: src/kernel/loops/events.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, paradigm-8]
    gates: [^loop-trusted]
    flows: [$loop-run]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-events.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LOOP-15, G-LOOP-17]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/events.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/ledger.ts
      - src/kernel/log/replay.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/wire.ts

  loop-strictness:
    description: >-
      #loop-strictness (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §3.3; build plan step 9,
      M2.11-lite) in src/kernel/loops/strictness-cap.ts: a loop's EFFECTIVE
      autonomy = min(its declared autonomy, the repo cap {level, effective run
      mode}), re-read at run start, before every node and on every loop.node
      row (Aegis M2), with a capReason for the GUI ("act → propose (L2)",
      status.loops.effective). L3 or manual: no run starts
      (FORBIDDEN{strictness-paused}) and a running loop pauses before its next
      node (loop.paused{reason:'strictness'}, a kernel pause: resuming takes
      fresh presence). L2: act runs as propose and lands nothing alone;
      concurrency 1. L1: concurrency 2 per repo (spec strictness.concurrency.L1); gate-class and kernel-high-risk
      lands need the second reviewer (8.1), so landDecision() makes them propose
      with secondReviewer recorded. L0: as declared. The repo brake
      (wire.ts brake(repoKey)) pauses the repo's running loops at once on a
      level raise or a repo run-mode tightening. A missing or throwing cap
      source is L3 (fail safe). Step 11's land calls landDecision() with the cap
      re-read.
    path: src/kernel/loops/strictness-cap.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, strictness, paradigm-8]
    gates: [^loop-trusted]
    flows: [$loop-run]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-strictness.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LOOP-10]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/strictness-cap.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/runner.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/wire.ts

  loop-agent-ops:
    description: >-
      #loop-agent-ops (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §5 "What an agent may do",
      §3.1 Attribution; build plan step 12, LP7) in
      src/kernel/loops/agent-ops.ts: the kernel pre-gate for an AGENT's
      userland loop.request{op, loopId, …}, routed by one dispatch.ts block
      (wire.ts port.agent). The asker's principal and hsid come from the
      connection, never the payload (Aegis). Targets are repos the registry
      already knows (never addRepo on this path); unknown repo and unknown loop
      are one NOT_FOUND; rows carry the registry's repo root. run: trusted
      loops only. pause: any loop (loop.paused{reason:'agent', by}); a loop it
      did not author toasts the founder. resume: only its own agent pause made
      in the current kill epoch (pauseAll writes one loop.paused{all:true}
      epoch row), then ONE catch-up run if a schedule or continuous trigger
      came due during the pause. delete: in 8.0 always a pause plus a founder
      toast. Reads (runs, logs, trust-status) are scoped to a named repo and
      redacted (verdicts and counts, no evidence or node detail, no row).
      create/update with sourceHash writes a kernel loop.request row (repo is
      a sensitive field) the registry folds into authoredBy{principal, hsid,
      basis:'agent-key-claim'} for that source hash (else `file`), and
      raises the loop's ONE loop-trust card (requested by the agent, decided
      by the human with the root key; no envelope in 8.0). Session-class
      askers and loop sessions (supervisor.isLoopSession, LL5) are refused
      every op; peers, voice, mods, read tokens and humans too (humans use
      request.loop.*). src/agent/index.ts (@a-company/gnomon/agent) is the
      agent principal's client for paradigm-mcp (GNOMON_AGENT_KEY, which must
      resolve inside the Gnomon home, or <home>/agent/device.key, with a
      root-signed agent registry row). Residual: no peer-pid or
      connection-to-session mapping exists, so loop sessions are recognised
      only by a connection-bound hsid (none bound until session-key auth);
      they hold today because loop sessions get no socket or session key.
    path: src/kernel/loops/agent-ops.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, loops, agents, paradigm-8]
    gates: [^loop-trusted]
    flows: [$loop-run]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/loop-agent-ops.test.ts
    oracles: [G-LOOP-03, G-LOOP-05]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/loops/agent-ops.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/registry.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/trust.ts
      - src/kernel/loops/wire.ts
      - src/kernel/socket/dispatch.ts
      - src/agent/index.ts

  strictness-levels:
    description: >-
      #strictness-levels (spec §3.4, D86–D88; M2.11-lite, build plan step 9),
      a sub-component of #approval-broker in src/kernel/broker/strictness.ts.
      Per-repo levels L0–L3 folded from KERNEL-signed
      approval.strictness.raised|lowered rows only (StrictnessBook; an unknown
      level folds as L3; no row is L0, the D87 L1 floor waits on Move 3).
      request.strictness.set{repo, to} (repo: a key or an absolute path) from the human on a gui or
      device connection only (dispatch.ts requests.strictness; every other
      class FORBIDDEN{matrix}, G-STR-01). Tightening writes the request row and
      approval.strictness.raised{presence:'none'} at once, withdraws a pending
      loosening card and runs the repo brake (the loops pause). Loosening is one
      level per step through a strictness card (detail.op level.loosen, allow
      fresh presence, hand-only, no standing tuple; G-STR-04); its effect
      re-checks the level and writes approval.strictness.lowered{presence:
      'fresh'}. The level is run-mode.ts's `level` seam (the L0/L1 auto-edits,
      L2 ask, L3 manual ceiling) and the loops' repo cap; status.strictness.
      Not in the lite slice: signal score, hysteresis, evidence, the L1
      floor, batched L2 landing, the cached-unlock remap.
    path: src/kernel/broker/strictness.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, approvals, strictness, paradigm-8]
    signals: []
    tests:
      - test/strictness-levels.test.ts
    oracles: [G-STR-01, G-STR-04]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/broker/strictness.ts
      - src/kernel/broker/run-mode.ts
      - src/kernel/socket/dispatch.ts
      - src/kernel/lifecycle/daemon.ts

  trace-capture:
    description: >-
      #trace-capture (src/kernel/trace/): a CORE per-project audit mode, on
      every home and build (TD-2026-10-19-057, amends TD-2026-10-19-139). The
      human switches it on per project folder (mode.ts; CLI `gnomon trace
      on|off|status|path`); state lives ONLY in <home>/trace-folders.json
      (0600, keyed by realpath). The supervisor reads the mode at every
      session.open and resume for the session cwd and, when on, tees every
      stdin and stdout line plus kernel ctl notes to
      <home>/trace/<project key>/<hsid with ':' as '-'>.jsonl, one {t, dir,
      line} per LF line. Project key = slug of the folder name + '-' + 12 hex
      of sha256(realpath); project.json in the key dir records the realpath
      (a mismatch refuses trace-unsafe). NOTHING is written into the project
      tree. The session profile's gnomon-home deny covers <home>/trace,
      renames included; no project-tree deny exists for the trace (Aegis L7).
      Dirs 0700, files 0600, lstat + O_NOFOLLOW|O_NONBLOCK (+O_EXCL to create):
      a link, FIFO or device refuses trace-unsafe without hanging (Aegis M3).
      Refusals (TRACE_REFUSALS, published in gnomon-contracts
      D98_CLOSED_VALUES) are reported on session.sandbox{traced:false,
      traceRefused}; traced:true otherwise (both reserved under P8-D98). Never
      blocks a session: bounded queue, out dropped first, in/ctl evict queued
      out and have a reserve, drops counted in ctl notes. Aegis M1: per-record
      bound 64 KiB (truncated copy + hash + bytes); past the 50 MiB file bound
      a capped note, then in/ctl in full and out as skeletons; past 4x out is
      only counted. Aegis M2/L4: span redaction on the decoded value
      ([redacted:<kind>]: PEM, grt:v1:, sk-ant-, ghp_, github_pat_, AKIA,
      xox[bp]-, secret env values). Aegis L3: prior-close-unclean note on a
      resume after an unclean close; a drain flushes with a 2 s deadline.
      Residuals: prompt and tool text in the clear, readable by the user's own
      account outside Gnomon; exact-folder match only (a session in
      /proj/sub is untraced when /proj is traced, follow-up); in/ctl past the
      hard bound are unbounded; win32 has no POSIX modes (sessions refuse
      sandbox-unavailable). Zero deps: no logger; visibility is the kernel
      log row.
    path: src/kernel/trace/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, audit, paradigm-8]
    tests:
      - test/trace-capture.test.ts
      - test/trace-session.test.ts

  rules-cli:
    description: >-
      #rules-cli `gnomon rules list [<home>] [--json]` / `gnomon rules revoke
      <id>` (src/cli/rules-cli.ts). list verifies the home's kernel log
      read-only (verifyLog with the home's kernel pubs, like gnomon replay)
      and folds the verified rows through #always-rules; an unverified log
      is refused INTEGRITY_BROKEN; the kernel principal is namespaced by the
      home's root (homeTrust ns), as the daemon's is. revoke (`gnomon rules
      revoke <id> [--home <abs>] [--json]`) asks the RUNNING daemon: the
      hello's key id is pinned from the home (kernelPinOf; an unpinned server
      gets nothing), it authenticates as the home's human device (device.key +
      its root-signed human registry row) and calls rules.revoke
      (#rules-revoke); kernel refusals (NOT_FOUND, BAD_REQUEST, FORBIDDEN) are
      printed as they come; no daemon => REFUSED ENGINE{not-running}.
    path: src/cli/rules-cli.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, cli, approvals, paradigm-8, move-1]
    tests:
      - test/always-rules.test.ts
    oracles: [G-BRK-05]

  mode-cli:
    description: >-
      #mode-cli `gnomon mode [manual|ask|auto-edits] [--session <hsid>]
      [--repo [<abs>]] [--home <abs>] [--json]` (src/cli/mode-cli.ts; spec
      §3.4 run modes, P8-D86/D87). Connects to the RUNNING daemon exactly as
      `gnomon rules revoke` does (hello key id pinned from the home via
      kernelPinOf, an unpinned server gets nothing; auth as the home's human
      device, humanDeviceOf). No mode: prints status.runModes (default,
      strictness built or not, repos with a mode, every live session's
      chosen/source/ceiling/effective and a pending loosening card). A mode:
      log.append{kind:'request.mode.set', payload:{to, hsid | repo}} (repo =
      --repo <abs>, default the cwd; the kernel turns a path into its repo
      key). Tightening prints the applied change; loosening prints the
      strictness card id to allow in Gnomon with Touch ID and does not wait.
      An unknown mode is refused locally (BAD_REQUEST usage, exit 64);
      kernel refusals print as they come.
    path: src/cli/mode-cli.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, cli, approvals, paradigm-8, move-1]
    tests:
      - test/run-modes.test.ts
    oracles: [G-BRK-05]

  strictness-cli:
    description: >-
      #strictness-cli `gnomon strictness show [<repo>]` and `gnomon strictness
      set <repo> <L0|L1|L2|L3>` [--home <abs>] [--json] (src/cli/
      strictness-cli.ts; spec §3.4, M2.11-lite). Connects to the RUNNING
      daemon as `gnomon mode` does (hello key id pinned from the home, auth as
      the home's human device). show prints status.strictness (one repo when
      named, its default level if none is set). set sends
      log.append{kind:'request.strictness.set', payload:{repo, to}}; a raise applies at once and pauses the repo's running loops, a
      lower level prints the strictness card to allow with Touch ID. <repo> is
      a folder (resolved against the cwd) or a sha256 repo key. A bad verb or
      level is refused locally (exit 64).
    path: src/cli/strictness-cli.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, cli, approvals, strictness, paradigm-8]
    tests:
      - test/strictness-levels.test.ts

  rules-revoke:
    description: >-
      #rules-revoke (P8-D98 human-device verb 14, TD-2026-10-19-528 F1) in
      src/kernel/socket/rules-revoke.ts: the rules.revoke{ruleId} handler the
      daemon wires into DispatcherOptions.humanVerbs['rules.revoke'] (one
      key per verb in daemon.ts `humanVerbs`). Reached only for a verified
      human device connection (dispatch.ts refuses every other class, and a
      connection whose credential is not explicitly 'device', Aegis L3); no
      presence prompt (revoking only narrows). Params exactly {ruleId}
      (RULE_ID_PATTERN) else BAD_REQUEST; the rule must stand in the
      daemon's RuleBook (kernel-signed rows only, shared with the
      supervisor's exact-match check via syncRuleBook) else NOT_FOUND; then
      the kernel appends approval.rule.revoked{ruleId, repoKey, tool, connId}
      (connId stamped from THIS connection; a CRITICAL kind, anchor-policy.ts
      Aegis M1, so the append resolves only once anchored) and re-folds, so the next exact
      match raises the card. Revokes run one at a time (a concurrent double
      revoke writes one row). Result {ruleId, revoked: true}.
    path: src/kernel/socket/rules-revoke.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, socket, approvals, paradigm-8, move-1]
    tests:
      - test/always-rules.test.ts
      - test/human-device-verbs.test.ts
    oracles: [G-BRK-05]

  gnomon-guide:
    description: >-
      #gnomon-guide `gnomon guide [<topic>]` (src/cli/guide-cli.ts,
      src/cli/guide-topics.ts; spec §5 "gnomon guide" paragraph and §9.7,
      TD-2026-10-19-648 item 10, T-2026-10-19-015). A CLI-only, pull-only
      verb: not a socket method, so abiId and clientApiId do not move
      (§3.1 I3). Topics spawn, claims, cards and refusals are COMPILED IN:
      built in guide-topics.ts from literals and from the frozen
      @a-company/gnomon-contracts descriptors (verb refusal lists, card
      kinds, decisions, refusal codes with gate, retriability, exit code and
      default next step), bundled by tsup; no topic file exists on disk, in
      the home or in a repo, and the guide modules read no file, env or cwd
      (threat 11). Every rendered topic carries the abiId and clientApiId
      this binary was built with. No topic lists the topics (exit 0); an
      unknown topic or extra arguments are refused BAD_REQUEST usage,
      exit 64, without echoing the argument. Nothing injects a guide into a
      session, prompt, envelope or card, and a guide grants nothing; status
      stays the man page (F4). Honesty check G-HON-08 (test/honesty.ts
      scanGuides, gated in test/honesty.test.ts): a topic naming a verb,
      param, refusal code or reason, or CLI flag the frozen descriptors lack
      fails the build; durations go through the G-HON-07 docs scan. The id
      G-HON-08 is named in the tests only; the spec assigns it when amended.
    path: src/cli/guide-cli.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, cli, honesty, paradigm-8, move-1]
    tests:
      - test/guide-cli.test.ts
      - test/honesty.test.ts

  gnomon-trace:
    description: >-
      #gnomon-trace `gnomon trace [--home <abs>] [--last | --session <hsid> |
      --since <ISO>] [--json] [--full]` (src/cli/trace-cli.ts) — a CLI-only,
      READ-ONLY audit timeline of a session for dogfood audits by the founder
      and by Claude. Not a socket method: abiId and clientApiId do not move.
      Joins per hsid (a) the kernel-log rows of the session (envelope session
      or payload hsid, approval rows of its cards by cardId, the daemon.start
      before and daemon.exit after), read from the segment files with a
      write-free open (immutable=1 when no -wal exists; readOnly beside a live
      -wal) and a hash-chain check (envelope hash + prev link; signatures are
      left to gnomon replay and the output says so); (b) the Claude Code
      transcript <claude dir>/projects/<realpath cwd, non-alphanumerics to
      '-'>/<uuid>.jsonl (prompts, replies, tool_use, tool_result); (c) the raw
      capture traceDirOf(home, <realpath cwd>)/traceFileName(hsid) — that is
      <home>/trace/<project key>/<hsid with ':' as '-'>.jsonl (TD-2026-10-19-057)
      — when present and a regular file (unparsable lines skipped; span
      redactions read [redacted:<kind>]; a legacy {redacted, hash, bytes}
      record is shown as [redacted], never reconstructed). The header shows whether
      the session was traced, from its session.sandbox row (traced /
      traceRefused). src/kernel/trace/capture.ts mirrors the capture
      builder's traceFileName/traceDirOf. Ordered by time, stable per source. Each turn.sent
      contentHash is checked against the transcript prompt with the
      supervisor's own turnContentHash: "✓ matches log" / "✗ hash mismatch";
      a prompt the log never sent is "not in log". Human output uses the
      readout helpers and sanitizeDisplayText on every shown string,
      truncated unless --full; --json is one
      {session:{hsid,uuid,cwd,principal}, log, transcript, capture, turns,
      usage, events:[{t, source: log|transcript|capture, kind, ...}]} per
      session per line. The output contains prompt text (help says so).
      `gnomon trace path [--home] [--folder]` prints where the folder's
      captures live and writes nothing; `on` says they live in the home and
      hold prompt and tool text in the clear (Aegis L8).
      Subcommands `gnomon trace on | off | status [--home] [--folder]` keep
      per-project trace mode in <home>/trace-folders.json (0600, keyed by
      realpath) via src/kernel/trace/mode.ts (setTraceMode, traceModeOf,
      tracedFolders — a minimal copy reconciled at merge with the capture
      builder's module); they are the only writes, and only to that file.
    path: src/cli/trace-cli.ts
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, cli, audit, read-only, paradigm-8, dogfood]
    tests:
      - test/trace-cli.test.ts

  local-runner:
    description: >-
      #local-runner (spec §13, §3.3, §6.1 step 2; M1.8 slice 1). Loops step 8:
      LocalLane.openLoop(ref, req) (and supervisor.openLoop) is the ONE
      unattended open: a trusted loop run's judge/summarize step, only with a
      registry run reference; origin 'loop', surface none, maxSteps 1, no
      kernel names in the env, permit scope.loop, model text hash-only in
      turn.event, ask() returns the reply; no capable endpoint throws
      LoopModelUnavailable (no card, never Claude); isLoopSession(hsid) for
      step 12's loop.request pre-gate (see #loop-runner). Kernel side:
      local-session.ts maps a SUCCESSFUL compaction_end (summaryHash + both
      token counts, no error) onto the typed session.compacted{hsid, turn,
      reason, summaryHash, tokensBefore, tokensAfter} row (compactedPayloadOf;
      T-2026-10-18-016, P8-D93) after the raw turn.event line, which stays;
      src/kernel/local/endpoint.ts (loopback/LAN rule on the normalised host;
      hostnames, Anthropic hosts, link-local/metadata, public, userinfo,
      forbidden web-endpoint port refused — G-SUB-06 BILLING_REFUSED via
      config.ts), config.ts (compat names = gnomon-local allowlist, no cloud;
      assertNotClaude: law 7 never runs claude under a backend:local permit),
      probe.ts (/slots n_ctx, else /props; tools only on a live tool call),
      routing.ts (local|claude|any; capable = probe ok + tools (or text-tool
      opt-in) + n_ctx >= floor 8192), src/adapters/local/runner.ts (pi rpc
      JSONL driver, tolerant parse, LocalUsageMeter no double count),
      src/kernel/supervisor/local-session.ts (LocalLane: incapable local =>
      fallback card via the broker + model.fallback.requested +
      MODEL_UNAVAILABLE{cardId}, no spawn; fallback-cloud decision opens
      Claude, S7; any => session.routed; capable => local session.spawn
      permit recorded with model + repo (P8-D92 meter, never capped) ->
      session profile -> intend/track kind local-runner; stdout turn.event
      first, model.capabilities/model.repair, usage against the spawn
      permit). Round 2: tools confined to realpath roots (no .git, no symlink final component), probe bodies capped 1 MiB, configured model id preferred, assertNotClaude on realpaths + @anthropic-ai/claude-code, explicit backend pins the route, launchPlan TEST-RIG seam (test/local-lane-spawn.test.ts). Slice 2: config.ts loadLocalConfig reads the ROOT-SIGNED
      <home>/local.signed.json (schema gnomon-root-local:v1; unsigned, tampered,
      unparseable, invalid or older than the sidecar/chain high-water mark =>
      NO local config, logged config.unsigned / config.loaded{file,
      fallback:'none'}; a signed public endpoint is applied and refuses every
      open BILLING_REFUSED), wired by the daemon; round 2 (Aegis H1): the
      runner pin is MANDATORY (node + nodeSha256, package dir + treeSha256,
      entry) — runner-pin.ts copies the tree into <home>/runners/<treeSha256>/,
      hashes the COPY, verifies node and execs <realpath node> <realpath
      copied entry> (PERMIT_REQUIRED runner-pin-mismatch{part}); residual:
      node is not copied (Homebrew node loads ../lib) — round 3: EVERY session profile (Claude and local) denies writes to node, its install prefix and the pinned source dir (policy.ts localRunnerDenies, fed from the loaded local config), and node is re-hashed synchronously right before spawn; a concurrent staging rename (EEXIST/ENOTEMPTY) is success iff the dest verifies; turn.event rows for compaction lines keep summaryHash/summaryBytes even over the line cap; a signed config with maxTokens >= 0.85 x contextTarget is invalid; round 2 (M3): roots resolved ONCE by native realpath (fail closed) and only the resolved paths reach the policy, child config, darwin extras, repo id and spawn cwd; re-checked right before spawn; per-task endpoint choice = first endpoint in
      signed order capable for the floor; probeAll at daemon start records
      model.probe/model.capabilities; roots inside the Gnomon home refused;
      the child gets contextTarget min(~8k, n_ctx); session.send{steer} with a
      turn in flight writes the steer line under that turn's permit
      (session.inject + turn.steer, no new permit), idle it is a plain send; round 2: the steer needs the in-flight turn permit live (else PERMIT_REQUIRED); the child refuses a steer that reaches it idle and the kernel logs turn.steer{phase:refused, by:child};
      Claude sessions refuse steer BAD_REQUEST. Deferred: darwin sandboxed
      e2e rig (G-LCL-01 fake half, G-LCL-06, 6-child G-LAW-01c), gnomon probe
      local CLI verb, the claude estimate on the card, attended/unattended
      local automations (law 5).
    path: src/kernel/local/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, local, paradigm-8, m1.8]
    tests:
      - test/local-runner.test.ts
      - test/local-config.test.ts
      - test/local-lane-spawn.test.ts
    oracles: [G-SUB-06, G-LCL-02, G-LCL-03, G-ADP-02, G-LAW-01c]

  claude-adapter:
    model-guard: >-
      Model picker (Aegis): buildClaudeArgv refuses a model that fails
      contracts claudeModelProblem with a TypeError, because `--model` takes
      the NEXT argv token whatever it is (a value like --verbose or
      --dangerously-skip-permissions would be eaten as the model or reach
      claude's parser). Defence in depth behind the supervisor's own check.
    usage-split: >-
      #ledger-mod (K2 feed v2): parseStreamLine's result event also carries
      split {in, out, cacheRead, cacheWrite} = usageSplit(result.usage)
      (numbers only; Aegis L2: each floored and clamped to a safe integer at
      the source, so the logged row equals the feed projection); the total and the
      UsageMeter charging are unchanged.
    backend-scope: >-
      TD-2026-10-19-252 (spec §1, §2.2, §22 item 26): backends are Claude
      and local models only. Other agents (opencode, grok, ...) get their
      own driver later, each with its own law-7 billing ruling; there is no
      generic ACP passthrough.
    description: >-
      #claude-adapter (spec §2.2, §3.3; M1.7 slice 1) in
      src/adapters/claude-code/adapter.ts. P8-D96: argv --permission-prompts
      host --permission-prompt-tool stdio (assertPermissionChannel throws on
      any other value); events permission-request / control-cancel /
      control-request-unknown; stdin permissionAllowLine /
      permissionDenyLine / controlErrorLine (never updatedPermissions). SLICE 2 (T-2026-10-18-010 L4):
      SESSION_HOOKS_MODES all|user-only|none and settingSourcesFor(mode) ->
      --setting-sources; the mode is the root-signed config.sessions.hooks
      (budget/config.ts parseSessionsSection; default all; rollback/invalid
      after a signed config => none), wired by the daemon into #supervisor
      and logged as session.sandbox.settingSources. P8-D97: pluginDirs
      (--plugin-dir) and addDirs (--add-dir), deduped; repoContextFor(mode)
      is true for audited only. Q6: EXTRA_FLAGS allow-list (--max-turns,
      --no-session-persistence) and ARGV_GRAMMAR/assertArgv (each kernel flag
      once; --agents/--tools never; one variadic --add-dir). The §3.3 argv with FORBIDDEN_FLAGS
      throwing (G-RUN-01, also --flag=value and extras); stream-json parse,
      TOLERANT (unknown fields ignored, unknown types `unknown`, non-objects
      `malformed` — the falsifier); UsageMeter charges each message id once
      and the result only its remainder; 4 MiB line cap; the stdin user and
      control_request interrupt lines. fixtures/2.1.302-turn.jsonl replays
      the shapes p10m-claude-compat-generated observed (G-ADP-01, synthetic;
      recorded nightly replay is slice 2).
    path: src/adapters/claude-code/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, adapter, stream-json, paradigm-8, m1.7]
    tests:
      - test/claude-adapter.test.ts
    oracles: [G-RUN-01, G-ADP-01]

  mod-host:
    description: >-
      TD-2026-10-20-514: the uninstall card shows its grant.uninstall row in
      detail.rootRows, prepares it (pin still effective) and commits it via
      appendSignedGrantRows: ONE presence; a dropped request expires the card.
      S0b (Aegis L1): status.mods.publishers maps each RUNNING mod id to its
      publisher display {host, verified} (publisherDisplayOf(publisher, source)
      from the install row's source), so GUI panel headers and offer chips render
      'name (pub @ host)' or 'name (pub, unverified)'. A status sub-field: not in
      STATUS_RESERVED_FIELDS, so clientApiId does not move.
      #mod-host (spec §3.8, §12.1-§12.3, §15.5 row mod; §19.1 M1.11 slice 1)
      in src/kernel/modhost/. host.ts ModHost - one child per mod, started
      ONLY via #manifest-loader loadMod (root-signed grant.install, snapshot
      integrity, manifestHash, grants; a changed linked folder pauses and
      stages the re-approve card instead of loading, G-MOD-08); effects in
      force = union of tool effects, capped at read while grant.install{dev}
      is live, an expired dev period pauses (mod.dev.expired, fail closed);
      spawns node <index.mjs|index.js> from the pinned snapshot under the
      default-deny mod profile (sandbox/mod.ts registry +
      platform/darwin/mod-sbpl.ts; same floor as sessions: macOS 15+ and SIP;
      other platforms refuse sandbox-unavailable), env GNOMON_MOD_NAME +
      GNOMON_EGRESS_PROXY only; tracked by DaemonChildren kind mod IN MEMORY
      (no mod.spawned/mod.exited kind: adding one moves clientApiId);
      init must echo mod-abi:v1 and MOD_ABI_ID. channel.ts - JSON-RPC 2.0
      NDJSON over stdio (1 MiB frames, stderr tail). Calls serialized per mod
      so mod->host requests are attributed exactly; effects.ts enforces
      state.get=read, state.set=write, kernel.call by verb (approve never,
      log.append only own declared modns, session.send only inside a tool
      call with the caller hsid) => FORBIDDEN + mod.effect.refused. Timeout =>
      cancel{cancelToken}, unhonoured => restart; crash => mod.health{ok:false}
      + restart (cap 3/10 min). block.ts closed kit:v1 gate (256 KiB / 2000
      nodes, sanitizeLine, Button command id only, Image alt + sha256 blob,
      Canvas names a ds; S5: Text may carry a `cap`, the closed contracts
      shape {shape, hue 0..5, filled, state, label <=40} checked by checkCap,
      any other field refused, label sanitized; kitId/modAbiId/clientApiId do
      not move). relayView/relayCommand stamp relayedFor from the
      caller. egress-proxy.ts per-mod loopback CONNECT proxy (443 only;
      granted declared hosts; IP literals, private names, Anthropic hosts,
      private resolutions and calls lacking network refused). state.ts
      per-mod KV <home>/mods/<name>/state.db (sibling of state/, never granted; O_NOFOLLOW reads, random O_EXCL tmp; JSON in slice 1). Round 2 (Aegis): state/ and blobs/ must be real dirs at their expected paths before every profile build; tunnels closed at call end; proxy head deadline, connection cap, idle timeout, resolve-check-connect, wider non-public ranges; explicit verb-effect table; session.send pinned to the caller hsid, permitId stripped; channel caps in-flight mod requests at 16 with backpressure.
      Test seams (unsandboxed launcher, egress dialer) only via
      kernel/testing.ts createModHostForTest. SLICE 2: call-id binding
      (host mints callId on view.render/command.run/tool.call, the SDK echoes
      it on state.get/state.set/kernel.call; a foreign or ended callId is
      FORBIDDEN modCode CALL + mod.effect.refused{why:call-id}; none = idle;
      modAbiId moved 00ae702b -> 7aaf92f4, clientApiId unchanged);
      bridge.ts createModKernelCall (log.append own modns clamped to
      kind/payload<=16 KiB/audience/sensitivity, Aegis C2, appended under the
      mod principal; every other verb via dispatcher.callAs as a
      connection-less mod principal) and createModRelayPort (mod.view /
      mod.command start the mod on first use; relayedFor stamped by the
      dispatcher from the caller connection; GUI mod.command refused until a
      gesture proof, M1.10); startDaemon constructs the host, status.mods +
      status.modAbiId, stopAll before the children sweep at every exit;
      folder watch on a running linked mod (fs.watch recursive, .git
      ignored, 500 ms debounce => stop{changed} + loadMod pause + re-approve
      card). Round 2 (review + Aegis): own-modns appends go as:'userland'
      under the mod principal, kernel-key-signed as an attestation
      (attributed by principal, never keyId; spec §3.2 L7); relay admission
      ModHost.admit (name/id shapes refused BAD_REQUEST in dispatch; an
      uninstalled name NOT_INSTALLED with manifest.refuse folded past 8 per
      10 s; paused answered from memory; relay starts count against
      RESTART_CAP; DOWN until an operator start); per-mod append budget (20
      rows/s, 64 KiB per call) and 8 mod->host requests in flight per mod;
      status.mods full to the human class only, counts otherwise; watcher
      unarmed on exit/timeout/pause/cap and re-armed on a successful start;
      a lost mod.paused / mod.effect.refused row surfaces as
      daemon.mod.row.unrecorded. Round 3 (Aegis R2-1/R2-2): fold.ts RowFold -
      mod.effect.refused (per mod, keyed effect|why|verb, verb clipped) and
      relay-miss manifest.refuse rows: 8 individual per 10 s window, the rest
      one coalesced row on the timer / next window / stop / stopAll, at most
      16 keys + other<Field>s + distinct (exact to 1024, then distinctCapped);
      the bridge drops a call's byte tally on the host's onCallEnd and prunes
      past idle seconds (never a wholesale clear); kernel-side refusals reach
      a mod as code + reason only (generic message, no wrapped data).
      Slice 3: the gnomon mod CLI and its control surface (#mod-cli, below);
      a <home>/mods/<name>/paused marker makes every start return
      paused{why:user}. feed.ts: ModHost.feed (startDaemon queues committed
      rows, bounded 4096, and feeds only rows at or below the log's released
      head; turn.partial coalesced to one edge per hsid; ModChannel.notify
      drops past 256 KiB unread stdin, counted in inspect
      droppedNotifications) forwards a fixed projection of session.open/exited,
      turn.partial/result, approval.request/decide/expire and warpline.knot
      (kernel fields only; project = a hue index from sha256(cwd)) to running
      mods requiring log.read - the #workshop-mod input. Projection v2
      (#ledger-mod input; Aegis M1: ONLY to mods granted the narrower
      log.read:usage capability, named on the install card; log.read alone
      gets v1 and turn.result as {hsid}; feedAccessOf/feedReaches; a
      local-tier manifest may not combine log.read:usage with egress,
      #manifest-loader validateManifest) adds NUMBERS-ONLY rows: turn.usage/turn.result
      {hsid, tokens, tokensIn/tokensOut/cacheRead/cacheWrite from the
      turn.result split the supervisor now records from Claude's
      result.usage, costMicros}, session.compacted {hsid, tokensBefore,
      tokensAfter}, budget.usage {dimension, n, of?, usdMicros?, over?},
      budget.ladder {dimension, scope, rung %}, budget.pause/resume/
      breaker.open/close and permit.deny {dimension}; every number clamped
      (feedCount), strings only ids or enum tokens (test/mod-feed-v2.test.ts
      property test); seq/at validated. status() lists views of running mods
      ({name, viewId, slot, placement, dev}) for the #gui mod panels.
      FIRST_PARTY_PACKAGE_HASHES pins the Workshop, the Ledger and Paradigm Context. Still to come: spend from the caller permit,
      dev-mode cards/re-pin, gnomon-mods MCP bridge, /blob (C4), Tier 0.
      K1 (#paradigm-context-mod): paradigm-slice.ts, the paradigm-slice:v1
      kernel projection of a LIVE session's repo (.paradigm/tasks/entries,
      decisions/TD-*.yaml, lore/entries/**): open/high task counts, top 5
      tasks, last 5 decisions {id,title,date}, last 5 lore {id,title,type},
      symbols touched; zero-dep strict YAML subset (malformed files skipped
      and counted), symlinks and realpath escapes refused, files capped,
      titles §4.5-sanitised, path-redacted, ≤120 chars; sensitivity secret
      dropped; no absolute path in the slice (hue + basename only). Granted
      by paradigm.read:summary (CAPABILITY_FORMS; not an id input; a
      manifest holding it may list no egress, any tier).
      Aegis review (H1/M2/M3/L1/L3): no super-linear regex over file text
      (key lines split by indexOf, trailing comments found by a linear scan,
      scalars and titles capped at SCALAR_MAX 4 KiB before any regex); the
      root walk runs in realpath space (cwd and home realpath'ed, case-folded
      on darwin/win32, never at or above the home); bounded opendir
      iteration (MAX_ENTRIES), MAX_TASK_PARSED 512, SLICE_BYTE_BUDGET 3 MiB;
      the cache is keyed by the .paradigm realpath (hue per cwd, sync read =
      single-flight; a worker is a follow-up); files: dev+ino match, nlink>1
      refused, re-realpath + stat(realpath) vs fstat after open; realpath(cwd)
      and realpath(home) scrubbed.
      K3: a RELAYED render's input is host-composed (host.ts
      composeRelayedInput, HOST_INPUT_KEYS focus/hsid/paradigm): the client
      names only {focus:{hsid}} (or the K0 {hsid}); the host joins that hsid
      SERVER-SIDE to the live session cwd (ModHostOptions.focusCwd; daemon:
      supervisor.scopeOf) and gives a paradigm.read:summary mod exactly
      {hsid?, paradigm: slice | unfocused}; a forged repo/path/cwd/paradigm is
      ignored (test/mod-focus.test.ts). Other mods get their non-host keys +
      {hsid}. Local-lane sessions are not joined yet (unfocused).
      Aegis M1: the join checks the CALLER. The dispatcher stamps
      relayedFor{principal, connId, principalClass}; the host keeps the class
      host-side (the mod's relayedFor stays {principal, connId}). Human: any
      live hsid. Agent/session: only its OWN session (ModHostOptions.
      focusPrincipal; daemon: supervisor.principalOf(hsid) === caller).
      Voice, mod, unknown: unfocused (peer/read-token never reach mod.view:
      RELAY_TIERS false). Falsifier: test/mod-focus.test.ts (agent naming
      another session's hsid).
      HOST-LEVEL FALSIFIERS (test/mod-capability-falsifier.test.ts, real stub
      children; mutation-checked): log.read-only mods get no usage kind and
      turn.result exactly {hsid}; a mod without paradigm.read:summary never
      receives the slice (relayed, forged, or via the feed).
    tags: [kernel, mods, sandbox, M1.11]

  mod-cli:
    description: >-
      #mod-cli (spec §12.4, §12.5, §3.1 I3; §19.1 M1.11 slice 3) -
      src/cli/mod-cli.ts `gnomon mod install|link|list|status|pause|start|
      uninstall` over #manifest-loader, plus the kernel half in
      src/kernel/modhost/control.ts. §5 has no install/pause/start verb and
      adding one moves clientApiId, so the CLI and the kernel meet in the
      home: install/link run stageInstall in the CLI (snapshot + pending card
      record, signs nothing; staging from a session is NOT available yet: the
      darwin session profile denies writes under the home, so it waits for the
      gnomon-mods bridge; a GNOMON_HSID in the env is recorded only as a
      claim, and the CLI's GNOMON_HSID refusals are advisory, the SBPL is the
      boundary) and print the card lines from rederiveInstallDetail
      (the hardened install path: one line per entry, kernel-composed count
      line at index 2, COMMAND_UNSAFE linked-path refusal, linked-source
      re-hash); a url source is refused. The daemon's StagedCardPickup
      (scan every 2 s) raises every pending staged card through
      #approval-broker raiseInstallCard (re-derived from the pinned snapshot;
      install = fresh presence + root key, Touch ID via the M1.3 helper),
      logs manifest.staged{via:cli} for cards the kernel did not stage
      itself, starts the mod on mod.installed; bounded (MAX_PICKUP_CARDS 32
      raised, 256 remembered refusals keyed by file stamp so a tampered
      record is not retried until it changes; Aegis C5: files are listed by
      readdir + lstat only (≤ 1024), raised / settled / refused-unchanged ids
      are skipped before any read, ≤ 64 records READ per scan, 64 KiB per
      record, manifest.refuse carries the ManifestRefusal code and is folded
      8 per 10 s, and the grant table is read once per scan). pause writes the paused marker
      (narrows; allowed from a session), the pickup stops a running paused
      mod (mod.paused{reason:user}); start clears it and writes
      start.request (operator start, lifts DOWN); uninstall writes
      uninstall.request naming the pin, the pickup raises a root-class
      `grant` card (allow|deny) whose allow appends a root-signed
      grant.uninstall row (GRANT_ROW_KINDS, not a clientApiId input;
      pin rows are stamped max(now, newest pin plus one millisecond) and read back - the
      reader rejects a non-newer pin as replay - and the uninstall effect
      reports nothing unless it took effect; junk staged files are
      remembered by stamp; manifest.staged logs by/hsid under claimed),
      stops the mod and logs manifest.uninstall{diff} (+ mod.unlinked);
      effectiveInstall = the last install/uninstall pin row naming the mod.
      start and uninstall are refused from a kernel-spawned shell. list and
      status read the grant table offline (trust from identity.json or a
      DEV home's root).
    tags: [cli, mods, M1.11]
    oracles: [G-MOD-04, G-MOD-08]
flows:
  send-by-word:
    description: >-
      $send-by-word (TD-2026-10-21-514, spec §14.7): the voice client appends
      the spoken send word as its own voice.turn.user (short words by
      adjacency: speakerBasis 'adjacent', accepted only under 1500 ms,
      matching, within 2 s of / pending with the draft speaker) → the kernel
      stamps isWord at receipt (#send-word-matcher, never the text) → the GUI
      (#gui-dictation) sees the word after a draft visible ≥ 1 s, shows the
      1 s grace → request.voice.send.claim{by:'word', turnSeq} (checked,
      consumed atomically, bound to the connection for 5 s) →
      session.send{source:'voice'} gets origin 'voice', sentBy 'word' →
      ^voice-origin-clamp until its turn.result. Backstop: an unclaimed
      pending word turn stamps voice anyway. Aegis fixes: the origin check
      runs on EVERY human send (stale or wrong-session claims stamp voice;
      under a word+ ceiling an ambient-matched send is a piece, so Enter on
      an ambient draft is clamped); a voice turn closes only after its own
      replay and a later result; a short word passes adjacency with or
      without speakerBasis; a word or pieces attach carries liabilityAck
      (sha256 of the ack text), recorded in the kernel-written start row.
      The guided dialog teaches the word as an ATTENDED TRIAL (spec §14.7
      trial semantics; 2 min, send-word-trial.json 0600): every turn of the
      attached human's voice principal is a take with no speaker check and
      no adjacency, stamped trialTake, forwardable:false, isWord false, never
      a dictation turn or adjacency anchor, and never sent (Aegis M-1: the
      voice principal's direct session.send of a take is refused
      voice-grammar, cause trial-take); a take is captured only while the
      trial is listening, and its row is hash-only plus trialVerdict (Aegis
      M-2: the heard text lives only in kernel memory for status);
      send-word-trial.json is written only from VoiceControl onTrialChange
      with the kernel-clock until and only while listening (Aegis L-2); a
      trial binds to the attached session and ends on any detach (human,
      voice principal, idle) or an attach to another hsid (Aegis L-1); the
      kernel is the ONE take counter
      (status.voice.sendWordTrial, human class only: word, state, take,
      takes, maxTakes 4, correct, variants, last, until) and
      request.voice.sendword{op:'save', dropVariants?} commits the word and
      the variants its mishearings collected (each passing the variant guard,
      #send-word-matcher acceptableVariant), minus the ones the human dropped,
      only after a correct take. request.voice.client mic-toggle /
      mic-key / start{capture} drive the mic, the Mic key and the capture
      kind of the kernel-spawned client (--capture ptt|open|toggle).
    steps: ["#gui-dictation", "#gnomon-voice", "#send-word-matcher", "#supervisor"]
    tags: [gnomon, voice]
    oracles: [G-VOX-20, G-VOX-20b]
  send-by-pieces:
    description: >-
      $send-by-pieces (TD-2026-10-21-514, spec §14.7): the attachment's
      pieces send ceiling (voice.session.start{send:'pieces'}, one fresh
      presence per GUI login, refused at strictness L2 or above) → the GUI
      batches finalized segments by gap/cap (1500 / 8000 ms) and holds while
      the session is busy → request.voice.send.claim{by:'piece', turnSeqs} →
      session.send{source:'voice'} with origin 'voice', sentBy 'piece' and the
      piece guidance note on the per-turn guidance block → ^voice-origin-clamp.
    steps: ["#gui-dictation", "#gnomon-voice", "#supervisor"]
    tags: [gnomon, voice]
    oracles: [G-VOX-21]
  offer-handoff:
    description: >-
      $offer-handoff (TD-2026-10-20-812, spec §12.9): a reply ends with one
      ```offer block → #offer-router checks budget, gate and declared kind →
      kernel offer.shown (manifest fields) → the GUI slip → ^offer-accept
      (human request.offer.decide) → offer.decided → on open a kernel-templated
      turn as the human → the mod's handoff tool on its gnomon-<mod> server
      (#mcp-passthrough) → offer.kept.
    steps: ["#desk-guidance", "#offer-router", "#mcp-passthrough"]
    tags: [gnomon, desk, mod-playbooks]
  first-run:
    description: >-
      $first-run (spec §2.4 rule 9): unprovisioned Mac → `gnomon` (or `gnomon
      setup`) → #setup-preflight → leftover purge (presence per key) → name →
      #provisioner init (lock, key.list, key.create ×2, root sign 1 of 2 and
      2 of 2 with shown digests, commit identity.json then registry row) →
      summary → #gnomon-up runUp in the same process (TTY launch URL, folder
      hint). Resume: an identity whose registry row is missing is completed
      as its own root-signed step (I10).
    steps: ["#first-run", "#setup-preflight", "#provisioner", "#gnomon-up"]
    tags: [gnomon, setup]

  daemon-lifecycle:
    daemon-stop: >-
      PARTLY BUILT (T-2026-10-19-014 B0, TD-2026-10-19-528 F3, spec §3.1 I3).
      Kernel side built: DaemonHandle.requestStop(connId, reason='stop') raises
      (or returns the one pending) signed daemon-stop card through
      broker/daemon-stop.ts DaemonStopCards, only for an authenticated human
      principal on a device connection (every other class, a read token and a
      GUI launch-token connection: FORBIDDEN{matrix}, no card). accept is fresh
      presence (Touch ID; a cached unlock never decides it) and calls
      shutdown(reason) on the next event-loop turn; reject, a declined prompt
      or expiry (2 min, into deny) keep the kernel running. DaemonExitReason
      gains 'stop' (daemon.exit{reason:'stop'}; gnomond exits 0, the
      no-restart class). Concurrent requests share the in-flight raise
      (#raising, Aegis L5): two at once get ONE card. The client path
      (TD-2026-10-19-339) is the human-device verb daemon.stop{reason?}
      (socket/daemon-stop-verb.ts createDaemonStopHandler), on the UNIX
      dispatcher's humanVerbs only (the web dispatcher reports it unavailable);
      connId is the dispatcher's own connection context, never a param (Aegis
      L6); reason is checked against DAEMON_STOP_REASONS (unknown ⇒
      BAD_REQUEST bad-params; upgrade raises the same card with its own
      UPGRADE lines since B1); the
      result is {cardId, state}. `gnomon daemon stop` pins the kernel key
      (kernelPinOf), authenticates as the home's human device (rules-cli
      humanDeviceOf), calls daemon.stop, prints CARD RAISED with "approve it in
      Gnomon with Touch ID" and exits 0 without waiting; no kernel ⇒ REFUSED
      ENGINE not-running; no human device ⇒ REFUSED FORBIDDEN no-human-device.
      B1: DaemonStopReason is the wire's DAEMON_STOP_REASONS (stop, upgrade);
      an accepted upgrade card runs supervisor.drain() and then
      shutdown('upgrade'). Falsifiers in test/daemon-stop.test.ts, test/daemon-stop-verb.test.ts and test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts.
    upgrade-drain: >-
      OLD-KERNEL HALF BUILT (T-2026-10-19-014 B1; TD-2026-10-19-908,
      TD-2026-10-19-528 F3/F4, TD-2026-10-19-814 item 2). daemon.stop{reason:
      'upgrade'} raises the signed daemon-stop card (UPGRADE lines, reason in
      contentHash); a Touch ID accept runs SessionSupervisor.drain({deadlineMs})
      then shutdown('upgrade') (DaemonExitReason 'upgrade', EXIT_REASON_CLASS
      ok: exit 0, no restart). drain(): idempotent; from its first call
      session.open and session.send (Claude and gnomon-local) refuse
      ENGINE{draining}; it waits for opens already past that check, then for
      every Claude session's in-flight turns (turnPermits) to reach a result
      line, up to the deadline (DRAIN_DEADLINE_MS default; no root-signed
      config names one); a session still mid-turn gets the interrupt control
      message, logged session.inject{channel:'drain', deadlineMs,
      deadlineSource}. For EVERY live Claude session: pending cards expire
      why session-drained (F4), session.drained{uuid, reason:'upgrade'} is
      written (closed payload, checked in kernel-log checkPayload), then the
      child stops and session.exited{reason} is written. THE B2 RECORD:
      session.exited.reason is 'drain-clean' (at a boundary: resume normally)
      or 'drain-interrupted' (mid-turn at the deadline: resume IDLE with a
      "turn interrupted by update" note, never continue); DRAIN_EXIT_REASONS
      in supervisor.ts; in-process also DrainReport and status().drained.
      Then ApprovalBroker.expireSessionBound('session-drained') expires every
      other pending card with an hsid; cards bound to no session (install,
      api-token, fallback) stay pending for the new kernel to raise again.
      gnomon-local sessions get no session.drained (they cannot resume until
      B3) and are stopped by shutdown (session.exited{reason:'upgrade'}).
      Falsifiers: test/session-drain.test.ts (darwin), the B1 case of
      test/daemon-stop-verb.test.ts. NEW-KERNEL HALF BUILT (T-2026-10-19-014
      B2): supervisor/resume.ts planResume() reads the kernel-signed rows
      BEFORE the rule-4 sweep: session.drained followed by session.exited
      {drain-clean|drain-interrupted}, with no later session.open or
      permit.reattributed, is resumed; drained with no exit is left to the
      sweep and never resumed. sweepOrphans{resuming} never sweeps a spawn of
      this boot for a resumed hsid. SessionSupervisor.resume() runs the
      ordinary spawn path with --resume <uuid> (same hsid, same uuid): sandboxed
      preflight, law 7, binary pin, layersHash and gitHash must equal the
      drained session's (else session.refused{resume:'layers-changed'|
      'git-changed'}), and BudgetFuse.reattribute() re-attributes the old
      permit.grant's permit (permit.reattributed{permitId, hsid,
      drainedEventId}; no permit.reserve/grant; expired/other-log/not-this-
      session refuse PERMIT_REQUIRED). Nothing is sent to the child; a
      drain-interrupted session also gets session.sandbox{phase:'resume-note',
      note:'turn interrupted by update'}. The new broker holds no unlocks.
      resumeAfterUpgrade() retires every card an earlier kernel left pending
      (ApprovalBroker.retireForeign: approval.expire{why:'cancelled'}); no kind
      is rebuilt from the log (the row lacks the detail its effect needs);
      install cards come back via #mod-host pickup from the staged record.
      A decision against an old card id is NOT_FOUND. The hello feature
      session.resume is advertised (SERVER_FEATURES, already reserved: no
      contracts move); session.send/stop on a resumed session without it are
      refused FORBIDDEN{matrix, needs:'session.resume'}. The daemon exposes
      the run as handle.resume. Falsifiers: test/session-resume.test.ts
      (darwin), test/resume-plan.test.ts (every platform).
      Aegis fixes (T-2026-10-19-014 B1+B2 review): M2 founder carry-over
      ruling — BudgetFuse.reattribute() takes drainedAt + principal; a permit
      live at the drain (drainedAt <= expiresAt) is re-signed under the same
      permitId with a fresh window and permit.reattributed{..., renewedByDrain:
      true, expiresAt}; expired-before-drain refuses; nothing minted
      (test/budget-fuse.test.ts). L1 a kernel-signed session.refused{resume}
      (RESUME_REFUSALS) is terminal in planResume. L2 DaemonStopCards
      refuses a request whose reason differs from the pending card's
      BAD_REQUEST{bad-params, pendingReason, cardId}. L3 principal checked in
      reattribute (assertClaudePermit does not). L4 principalClass carried
      from session.open (resume refuses non-human 'not-human'). L5 a failed
      drain interrupt write logs session.inject{channel:'drain', failed:true}.
      L6 resumeAfterUpgrade retires old cards BEFORE resuming.
      Original plan: A kernel update drains and resumes:
      the old kernel rides `gnomon daemon stop` with reason upgrade (no new
      verb), starts no new turn, drains each session to a turn boundary
      (interrupt after a deadline), writes session.drained{uuid,
      reason:'upgrade'} then session.exited, and exits. The new kernel
      respawns each drained session with --resume <uuid> through the
      respawn path (law-7 preflight, layersHash and permits re-run).
      Cached unlocks and voice arms never carry over; permits are
      re-attributed, never minted; pending cards are raised again. Rule 4
      amended: a drained-and-exited session is not an orphan (G-DMN-04
      covers both cases). Claude sessions first, gnomon-local second; live
      handover is an unprobed idea after Move 2 (spec §22 item 25).
    description: >-
      $daemon-lifecycle (spec §2.4; PR-5 core of M1.9). (1) client activation —
      `gnomon daemon --detach` re-execs gnomond with the clean env and home in
      argv, then waits for its ready line (item 3 below); (2) one per user — home 0700 read back,
      endpoint path chosen (never /tmp), O_EXCL daemonPid:v1 pidfile acquired
      (live holder refuses with daemon.lock.refused, dead holder reclaimed;
      Aegis L2: an EPERM holder — another user's pid — is residue and
      reclaimed, and a same-user live holder is `already-running` only if a
      kernel answers hello on OUR socket within 2 s, else `holder-silent`,
      transient, pidfile left alone — a silent holder may be a racer —
      UNLESS its `ps -o lstart=` start witness (TZ=UTC) lies more than a
      2 s tolerance (HOLDER_START_TOLERANCE_MS, a configured bound) after
      the pidfile's startedAt: a recycled pid after a crash or reboot, so
      the pidfile is reclaimed (pid+startedAt+inode re-checked, at most once,
      daemon.lock.reclaimed emitted; T-2026-10-19-017); no witness or an
      unparseable startedAt stays holder-silent);
      (3) #kernel-log opened with the kernel key (M1.4 seam: a supplied or
      ephemeral in-memory software Ed25519 key, custody partial; keystore
      item is M1.3) and the root authority (default unprovisioned), #socket
      listens at 0600 with the hello/auth dispatcher, daemon.start is
      emitted AND logged (daemon.exit logged at shutdown, then the log is
      closed before the lock is released); (4) idle exit —
      no open connections for idleMs (default 10 min) ⇒ daemon.exit{reason:
      'idle'}, socket closed, pidfile and socket released only if still ours;
      SIGTERM/SIGINT take the same path with reason 'signal'. Since M1.4
      slice 2 every log.read{follow} subscription is an idle holder
      ('subscriber') and the hub is closed first at shutdown. Not yet built:
      sessions/timers as holders, mod stop, orphan sweep, the Windows pipe.
      M1.4: the #web-endpoint is started by requestWebLaunch, its WebSocket
      connections are idle holders, and it is stopped (child ended) before
      the socket closes at shutdown. M1.9: (5) home — the daemon takes its
      home only from --home (daemonHomeOf; GNOMON_HOME is a client-side test
      seam it never reads — both gnomond paths, foreground and detaching;
      only `gnomon` client commands honour the seam); (6) orphan sweep at
      start, before the socket listens — every KERNEL-written session.spawned
      with no session.exited/orphaned is recorded session.orphaned{probe,
      action} BEFORE any signal, and its group is signalled only when the
      row's boot session (log/boot.ts) equals this boot AND the live
      leader's start-time witness (process-control.ts: macOS ps lstart,
      Linux /proc stat field 22, read with TZ=UTC before any await at track;
      ps forks — never on a hot path; win32 = parity phase, never signalled)
      equals the recorded one — else probe stale-boot | unattributable,
      action none; orphans stopped in parallel, counted only when gone
      (G-DMN-04); (7) supervised children (DaemonChildren: session,
      local-runner, mod) — intend() writes session.spawned{phase:'intent'}
      before the fork, track() writes {phase:'running', pid, pgid, boot,
      startTime} and refuses a session child that does not lead its own
      group; sessions write session.exited and hold idle ('session'); mods
      never hold, and have no spawn/exit kinds in §9.1, so a crash-orphaned
      mod is not swept (residual); stop = SIGTERM → grace → SIGKILL, then
      group members outliving the leader are SIGKILLed and counted;
      every shutdown (idle included) stops the scheduler, then stops and
      AWAITS every child (mod.stopped{reason}) and the web transport child
      (socket.web.stop{reason:'idle'|'daemon-exit'}) BEFORE daemon.exit
      {reason, liveChildren} is written — the falsifier "daemon exits with a
      live child"; (8) idle holders are connections, subscribers (log.read
      follow AND open GUI WebSockets), sessions and #scheduler timers due
      inside 10 min. Not yet built: the session runner and mod host that
      call children.track (M1.7/M1.8/M1.11), the Windows pipe, job object
      and owner-only DACL read-back (G-WIN-02, parity phase P8-D78).
      T-2026-10-18-030 (1): the clean re-exec passes exactly one node flag,
      --disable-sigusr1 (env.ts DISABLE_SIGUSR1 / reexecArgs), and
      runForeground refuses to start without it in execArgv
      ([sigusr1-enabled]) — a same-user kill -USR1 would otherwise open the
      V8 inspector in the key-holding process; a JS SIGUSR1 listener never
      counts. Both bins use `#!/usr/bin/env -S node --disable-sigusr1`.
      Falsifier: a running gnomond receiving SIGUSR1 opens no inspector and
      keeps running (test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts, control arm proves the
      detector). A SEA must bake the flag into sea-config execArgv.
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 1 (descendant sweep): before the first signal a
      session / local-runner stop takes an ASYNC process-table snapshot from
      the kernel (process-control snapshot(): `ps -A -o
      pid=,ppid=,pgid=,lstart=`, TZ=UTC, execFile — never spawnSync on the
      event loop), collects the leader's descendants (ppid walk) and group
      members with their lstart witnesses, SIGTERMs out-of-group ones with
      the group, and after the grace SIGKILLs only pids whose witness still
      matches (a recycled pid is never signalled). session.exited{tree} =
      exited | alive | unverifiable (no snapshot / failed re-check / win32
      ⇒ unverifiable, never folded). Residual: a descendant reparented to
      init before the stop began is unreachable; lstart is 1 s resolution.
      A leader already gone at snapshot time with no group member left ⇒
      unverifiable + treeDetail 'leader-gone-before-stop', never exited.
      stopAll takes ONE snapshot for all its children (T-2026-10-19-011),
      not one `ps` per child; a single stop takes its own.
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 3 (readiness handshake, Orca Adopt 3): the
      activator (lifecycle/ready.ts launchDaemon, used by `gnomon daemon
      --detach` and plain gnomond) spawns the kernel with an inherited ready
      pipe (`--ready-fd 3`) and a bounded stderr tail (8 KiB; after an exit
      the tail is judged once stderr ends, waiting at most 250 ms); the kernel
      writes one gnomonReady:v1 {pid, startedAt, socketPath,
      kernelPrincipal} line once it holds the lock and serves, then stops
      writing events to the activator's stderr (EPIPE swallowed). No socket
      polling. A child that exits superseded (lost lock race) or transient
      before ready makes the activator wait for the winner's socket and
      report `attached` (exit 0, no error); no ready line on a live child
      (Windows fallback) = socket probing until the GLOBAL deadline. Probes
      are a 1 s `hello` RPC (not a bare connect) and every probe loop stops
      once the launch settles. Our own live child owning the socket (pidfile
      names it) at the deadline = timeout, never attached. The ready line is
      capped at 4 KiB and must name the child pid AND the expected
      socketPath, else it is rejected (noted in the tail). kernelPrincipal
      from it is an unauthenticated hint, never pinned (only M1.4 mutual auth
      authenticates). gnomond seals the inherited ready fd first thing
      (sealReadyFd: re-open via /proc/self/fd or /dev/fd with O_CLOEXEC,
      close the inheritable one; unsealable => closed) so the identity helper
      and web child cannot forge it. Linux (libuv's stdio pipe is a
      socketpair /proc/self/fd cannot re-open) uses the 'fifo' channel
      instead (T-2026-10-19-011): a private 0600 FIFO in a fresh 0700 mkdtemp
      dir, read end opened by the activator before the spawn, only its path
      passed (`--ready-fifo`), the kernel opens the write end O_CLOEXEC
      (FIFO/owner/mode/inode checked, its parent dir ours and 0700 (Aegis L2), O_NOFOLLOW) and unlinks the name first
      thing, the activator removes the dir at settle; mkfifo runs by
      absolute path (/usr/bin, then /bin, never PATH); a FIFO failure passes
      NO ready channel (never the unsealable fd, Aegis L1): the tail and the
      attached outcome's readyDowngrade record `ready fifo unavailable:
      <code>; probing the socket` and the socket is probed until the global
      deadline. macOS keeps the sealed fd (kqueue never reports the FIFO
      writer's close, losing the no-ready-line fallback). Residual: win32
      handle inheritance; pid + socketPath pin applies on every channel.
      `gnomon daemon status` PINS (T-2026-10-19-016(a)): an unpinned hello
      learns only the CLAIMED serverKeyId; the pin is read from the home
      (root-signed registry kernel row, else keys/kernel-pub.jsonl =
      pinSource home-kernel-pub, same-user custody partial) and a fresh
      pinned hello must verify, then `running:true, serverVerified:true,
      pinSource`. A key the home does not know prints `running:null,
      answering:true, serverVerified:false` with clientApi/serverKeyId only
      under `unverified` (exit 0); a known key whose serverSig fails is
      refused (exit 1). Never running:true on an unverified hello.
      Aegis L4: KernelLog.open runs after the pidfile lock and BEFORE the
      socket is published (the sweep, dispatcher and pre-auth rows need the
      log first; a refused open must not leave a served endpoint). Its
      exclusivity rests on the pidfile lock, which assumes same uid and same
      pid namespace for every contender; a home shared across pid namespaces
      is unsupported (a holder there can look dead and be reclaimed).
      Exit codes are defined ONLY in
      lifecycle/exit-codes.ts: 0 ok, 4 superseded, 64 usage, 70 internal,
      75 transient, 78 config; config/superseded never restart, transient
      and internal restart with backoff. Every LifecycleRefusalReason is
      classified by a Record (a new reason is a compile error until it is).
      Tests: test/daemon-ready.test.ts (POSIX, registry row),
      test/daemon-exit-codes.test.ts (every platform).
    steps:
      - "#gnomon-kernel"
      - "#kernel-log"
      - "#identity"
      - "#socket"
      - "#scheduler"
      - "#web-endpoint"
    tests:
      - test/daemon-lifecycle.test.ts
      - test/idle-exit.test.ts
      - test/home-resolution.test.ts
      - test/reexec-env.test.ts
      - test/sigusr1-guard.test.ts
      - test/daemon-children.test.ts
      - test/daemon-children-tree.test.ts
      - test/daemon-ready.test.ts
      - test/daemon-exit-codes.test.ts
      - test/socket-ownership.test.ts
      - test/scheduler-daemon.test.ts
    oracles: [G-DMN-01, G-DMN-02, G-DMN-03, G-DMN-04, G-DMN-05]

gates:
  voice-origin-clamp:
    description: >-
      ^voice-origin-clamp (TD-2026-10-21-514, spec §14.7 "Voice submodes";
      src/kernel/supervisor/voice-clamp.ts): every turn.sent carries a
      kernel-stamped origin (hand | voice | voice-unverified, sentBy word |
      piece | hotkey | unverified; voice-unverified is a Dictate Enter send
      holding an unverified voice's words, clamped the same way). While a
      voice-origin turn is outstanding (turn.sent to the turn.result that
      closes it; never closed ⇒ until the session ends) #supervisor skips the
      auto-edits pass and every always rule (exact and prefix); only the
      *:read-only modeRefs (#shell-readonly) still pass (clampAllows, default
      deny for any new modeRef). Every other tool call raises a card with
      detail.origin 'voice', detail.sentBy and the line "Asked during a
      voice-sent turn (…). Check this was you."; #approval-broker refuses a
      by-voice approve of such a card without readback + confirm
      (voice-readback-missing, cause voice-origin-confirm); deny unchanged.
    prizes: []
    tags: [gnomon, voice, security]
    oracles: [G-VOX-17, G-VOX-18, G-VOX-19]
  loop-origin-clamp:
    description: >-
      ^loop-origin-clamp (docs/specs/gnomon-loops.md §3.4, LL5; build plan
      step 8; src/kernel/supervisor/loop-clamp.ts, after
      ^voice-origin-clamp): every loop session opens with origin 'loop'
      (session.open{origin, loopRun}) and only through LocalLane.openLoop
      with a registry run reference. In those sessions standing grants, the
      human's always rules, auto-edits and the read-only passes do not apply
      (loopClampAllows admits only the loop's own trust-card modeRefs, none
      in 8.0; default deny); cards carry detail.origin 'loop', loopId,
      authoredBy and the kernel line "Raised by loop X (authored by Y)"
      (withLoopOrigin, loopClampLine); the session env has no GNOMON_SOCK,
      GNOMON_SESSION_KEY or GNOMON_HSID and judge/summarize have no tools, so
      a loop session has no paradigm_loop write op and no loop.request.
    prizes: []
    tags: [gnomon, loops, security]
    oracles: [G-LOOP-05, G-LOOP-06]
  offer-accept:
    description: >-
      ^offer-accept (TD-2026-10-20-812, spec §12.9; Aegis D1): request.offer.decide
      and request.offer.mute are served only for the human on a gui or device
      connection, stamped by connId at dispatch (never an agent, MCP, mod or
      model connection, nor the offer path itself). decide: the offer is live
      (its session running and owned by the asker), the latest for its hsid and
      undecided; exactly one decision per offer, replays refused.
    prizes: []
  setup-token:
    description: >-
      ^setup-token (spec §11.2 rule 11) — the one-shot setup token of the
      #setup-endpoint: 32 bytes, 60 s TTL, single use, carried only in the URL
      fragment #setup=<tok>&k=<spki>[&cwd=…] on the TTY line or the wrapper's
      channel pipe; never argv, env, files or logs. A reuse inside the TTL
      aborts provisioning and journals setup.token-reuse (no token in it); a
      second setup connection is refused.
    prizes: []
  unprovisioned-only:
    description: >-
      ^unprovisioned-only (spec §2.4 rule 9): #first-run provisions only a
      home with NO keys/identity.json. An existing identity is shown
      (createdAt, root keyId) and never adopted or replaced; a malformed one
      is shown, never touched; a dev or symlinked home is refused. The one
      write allowed on a provisioned home is the I10 completion of a missing
      registry row (the saved, already root-signed row; no new signature).
      Declared in the repo-root portal.yaml (gates: unprovisioned-only).
    tags: [gnomon, setup]

  subscription-only:
    description: >-
      ^subscription-only (spec §10 law 7, D35): no kernel-spawned Claude
      process bills API credits — #budget-fuse refuses permit.reserve
      {session.spawn|turn, claude-code} BILLING_REFUSED on any API-billing
      path the law-7 scan finds; the only API path is the per-use root-signed
      api-token card of #approval-broker, injected into exactly one child,
      with the API CREDITS ACTIVE indicator and billing.api.active rows.
      M1.7 slice 1: #session-runner scans every layer the child reads per
      spawn cwd before any permit, #supervisor refuses claude without a
      law-7-scanned claude-code spawn permit and watches the layers
      mid-session (G-SUB-01, G-SUB-05).
    tags: [gnomon, law-7, billing, paradigm-8, m1.6]
    oracles: [G-SUB-02, G-SUB-03, G-SUB-04]

signals:
  voice-clamp-applied:
    description: >-
      !voice-clamp-applied (TD-2026-10-21-514): a permission request was
      raised under ^voice-origin-clamp. Carried as clamp:'voice' on the
      answer's session.inject{channel:'permission'} row (an opaque payload
      field, no new log kind, so clientApiId does not move) and as
      detail.origin 'voice' on the card; the supervisor logs it on the
      #supervisor component.
    emitters: ["#supervisor"]
    tags: [gnomon, voice, security]
    oracles: [G-VOX-17]
aspects:
  ~ample-internal-logs:
    description: >-
      ~ample-internal-logs (spec §9) — everything the kernel does is an event:v1
      row in #kernel-log with the §9.1 default labels set by the appender
      (audience private; turn.event bodies, session.inject bytes,
      approval.request.tool_input, oracle evidence and voice text sensitive;
      a kind without a default is refused, G-LOG-09), and every read goes
      through the §9.3 redaction matrix (stubs {kind, hsid, at, hash} for
      redacting readers, secret never to a read token, nothing to a peer).
      PR-4 covers the label and read-projection halves; M1.12 slice 3 adds the
      honesty CI (§9.7: G-HON-02, 03, 05, 06, 07; test/honesty.test.ts) and
      the status-number declarations (src/kernel/socket/status-numbers.ts);
      retention/TTL pruning (§9.4) and the remaining §9.6 metrics follow.
    category: observability
    severity: high
    tags: [gnomon, logging, redaction, paradigm-8]
    anchors:
      - src/kernel/log/chain.ts
      - src/kernel/log/redaction.ts
    applies-to:
      - "#kernel-log"
